      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>AVOID</head>
        <p>AVOID</p>
        <p>I 7 "</p>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. b 'r' • - We do not think we can point to a St. Patrick. Day ! 'Which was observed with such enthusiastic celebra- * . tins. as that which has just passed. The circum- '' • ,Mances were peculiar. In Mr. Redmond's fine phrase ' Irishmen, all the world over, met with the very light -cif victory shining on their cause. And whether it be -" scrmAthe water in Ireland, or there in Great Britain, 'L • - whether' in the Western world of Canada and America tg down in the underworld of Australia, everywhere -. • we find the same exultant note of a people who believe dry are nearing the end of a long vigil. The speeches r •of Mears. Redmond, Dillon, Devlin and O'Connor pat. sustain that note, and what is perhapemore significant, • ithe'Unionist critiog in their despondent utterances also - . •t" • jektify the hope of the Irish. These mass meetings in • - Manchester, Glasgow and Liverpool where speeches were delivered by the men most responsible for the • ' Haim Rule campaign were exceedingly well timed, • , and formed excellent occasions for the much . • ' • needed propaganda on the Irish question. As • ' -e we have repeatedly pointed out in this column during tbe past few months, the opponents of Ireland's demand for autonomy, destitute of anything resew- bling fact and logic, are steadily attempting all over Great Britain to raise a storm of religions prejudice and bitterness against • non-sectarian movement Some of the old cries are being revived and a me of the later ones which we have shown to be discredited are being continued. For example, Mr Balfour, with characteristic andacity, has recently repeated the suggestion that if Home Hale were not at stake, the veto of the House of Lords would be a matter of indifference to the Irish parry, and that, on this account, the attitude of the Irish party should not be treated as a serious factor in the movement for a constitutional change. On more than one platform this week-end that impudent assumption has heen riddled with hard fact. Admittedly the Home Rate demand is the first point with all Natiosaliet members bat if it were out f consideration altogether, does any man, knowing the treatment which Ireland has received from the Home of Lords in the past, imagine for a moment, that a single Nationalist representative would not be quick to straighten that obsolete Chamber's account? Shoulder to shoulder with the representatives of Britain's democracy he would, apart entirely from Home Rule, strive with all his power to curb the power which had worked disaster in both countries. : Mr. Balfour and some of the leader writers of the • Unionist press are deceiving themselves--the worst and most hopeless form of deceit—when th.y imagine that any Nationalist member could watch the Veto Bill Debate and not call to his mind the tab of wrongs that comprii.e the relations of Ireland and the House of Lords. From early in the last c.ntuy down to Mr. Birrell'a recent Bill dealing with the Land aims- tion every measure of reform calculated to bring light and hope for Ireland has been maimed, ,delayed or rejected in the House of Lords, and even supposing for one wild moment, the Irish party forgot so salient a feature of the matter, the Irish people who back them eta consistently would not be wanting in an immediate and an effective reminder. We spoke earlier on of the absence of fact,logic and argument from the Opposition. If anyone be sceptical on that point let him consider the cry that Irish disloyalty would flourish under a Home Bute Government. Would</p>
        <p>any self esteeming politician give out such a weak atribboleth to a people, such as the British of this generation who have seen a rare that gave them hard and long battle in the field a few years ago, today endowed with self-government—an endowment . . . .</p>
        <p>admitted alike by Unionist Radical and non-party man to be for the greater security of Empire? To such attack., puerile and senseless though they are seen to be, we must be ready with a clear and solid front of rational hypothesis and indisputable fact, and these recent gatherings are replete with both. Before we leave this point of last Sunday's meetings we would like to call attention to one gratifying feature and that is the large number of Englishmen who sought and gained admisaion to them. This lea heartening sign and augers well for the autumn campaign which Mr. Redmond informs us is in coarse of preparation. The Unionist laudation of Sir Edward Grey's arbitration speech will be of small value we imagine if they persist in opposing the one condition which will make that much to he desired proposal possible. The whole world knows that the only reason why the American Senate rejected the Arbitration Treaty of 1858 was that it was proposed by a Government notoriously hostile to Home Rule. In the State. the Irish exiles and their descendants, number to-day 16,000,000 person. Every one of that vast number is an ardent Home Ruler. Many Of the richest and most influential men in America ate of that Irish blood and Irish National conviction and if the men in the House of Commons who speak no glibly about peace with America would exercise a little reflection on the plain fact we have just given it would he clear to them that peace with the Irish means peace with the Americans.</p>
        <p>Prom y</p>
        <p>pdd</p>
        <p>ant in spirit than if they were just living an some sere affecting to do oepending upon State aid. Ile did not regard State Insurance as altogether an unmixed binn- ing inasmuch as it had n tendency to take away the spirit of incependence which had been the characteris- tic feature of the British race in the past. There was • danger of State Insurance undermining the spirit of</p>
        <p>thrift and a society like that,managed byauch elcellent and efficient officers, deaerved hearty eupport. After the toast had been heartily drunk, 11r0 Thorn'ley responded end remarked that they bad recently started a tent for females which promised to hen greet success. (Applause). Friendly nocietlee bed done well in the past end he believed they would do even better in the future.</p>
        <p>One of the most important items of the evening was the presentation of an illuminated addrese and a purse of gold to P.D.S., P.D.C.R. Bro. Llewellyn Davie., who had discharged the onerous duties of district aecretary for 22 years, daring which time the order bad greatly increased in numerical number and in iolinenee, D.C.R. Bro. George klaybury made the presentation and testified to Mr. Davies salvable services, P.D.C.H. Bro. P. Peacock supportiog. Bro. DAVIES in replying thanked the members for their appreciation of his services and gave some very interesting figures showing the progress of the Order during the period he had held the office as District Secretary The illuminated address, which was very much admired was designed and written by Mr. Tom Sergeant of 19, Alesandra-street, Warrington. tiro. Colonel BALL WALKER, M.P., then made a presentation to P.D.C.R.Bro.James Grayson of Widnes, oils P.D.C.R.'e emblem as a recognition of his services no chairman of the district and in doing no spoke in : earn) appreciation of the splendid wey in which Bro. Grayson had discharged the duties of his office. Bro. I Grayson suitably replied and thanked Bro. Colonel Hall Walker for the honour he had conferred on him by making the presentation. A splendid musical programme was submitted, the artistea including the following :—Hadvn Glee Party. Memos T Roberts. Gray, E A Sanders, W Critchley, W Sutton, W Warburton, and J Stockton. Mr W Pemberton waa an efficient mmorepardat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MR. HALDANR AND THR LORDS.</head>
        <p>MR. HALDANR AND THR LORDS.</p>
        <p>The times" Parliamentary correspondent says:— It is understood that the Government decided on Wed- nesday that, owing to the present situation of their front bench in the House of Lords, it should be rein- forced by Mr. Haldane. The necessary steps will be taken almost Immediately, and it is expected that Mr Haldane will go to the Cpper Hoore within the next week or ten days. It is expected that Mr. Haldane will retain his office of Secretary of State for War, though this is not yet dennitcly known. In this event, the debate on Lord Roberts' motion on April 3rd in the Home of Lords aill prove to be of greater interest than could have been expected, ns the Secretary for ' War will be able to answer Lord Roberts across the floor of the House of Lords. Some further change in the Mlniatry will be necesoitated by the departure of Mr. Haldane from chore he bee made so great a reputa- tion, and where he has won much esteem.</p>
        <p>Dos'r LOOK OLD—keep the ravage. of Time et Be by Derkening the Grey Streak. with LOCKYER'S BLILPIIUR HAIR RESTOREH,deepens to natural colour in few day. Maintains youth appearance. Large botta .yarywhere,Poat,l,lld. Bedford Laboratory LondowS.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>KNUTSFORD LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>KNUTSFORD LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>Annual Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>The anneal Council meeting of the Kanter ird Liberal Association was held on Saturday last at the Manchester Reform Club. There wan a liege and representative gathering from all quarters of the division.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Tomlinson moved the adoption of the annual report, in which it was stated that the committee did not come before the Council in any spirit of depressitn as regarded the constituency, in spite of the last two e'ections in twelve months, which went against Liberaliem. Without desiring to minimise their defeat they pointed out that the division had never returned a Liberal except in 1906. They were convinced the division offered a fair chance of conversion to Liberal- ism, provided sufficient work were engaged in, and a candidate obtained who could give sufficient time to the division and Income acquainted with the electors. Gratitude was expreseed to Mr. J. 11. Whitworth for coining forward at the last contest within seventeen days of the election and making a most strenuous fight, He aroused remarkable enthusiasm in the division. With the abolution of plural voting, the Spread of education and the loosening of semi-feudal and territorial shackles, the committee felt that the division formed a hopeful field for pi ogress. (Applause). Captain H. Gordon Roberts having seconded the resolution, it was unanimously adopted. I The Chairman proposed the re-election of the flight I Hon. Lord Sheffield as president of the Association, and congratulated the members on having so demo- cratic a member of the Homo, of [ordain iheir midst. If there were more peers like lam there would he no trouble about Veto Bill.. (Applamw. : Mr. F. A. Haworth (vice-chairman) seconded the resolution, and Lord Sheffield was unanimously !re-elected.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. L. Goodson, having been unanimouely re-elected chairman of the Executive, eeid he was firmly of opinion that the Knuteford Division could be won for Liberalism, and nothing should be left undone to win it once more. Mr. Frank A. Haworth was elected vice-chairman, Mr. H. I'. Greg, J.P. hon. tressurer and hlessrs. F. I. Hoy and J. H. Tomlinson hon. secretaries.</p>
        <p>A most interesting and practicaldiscu— anion then took place and representatives from all the polling districts spoke on various aspects of the work and suggestions for the future. A resolution expressing thankfulness for the recent great speech of Sir Edward Grey inviting an alisoluic peace treaty between this country and lie United Staten, proposed by Mr. J. B. Shewell, eeconded by Mr. W. Crothers and supported by Mr. C. J. Holds- worth, J.P. was carried unanimously. Amongst those present from the Warrington side of the division were, Captain H. Gordon Roberta (Appleton), Mr Josiah Longlund (Lower Walton), Mr. and Mt-s. J. (I. Pidduck, Messrs. A. Albinson, T.Millar, J. W. Brayshaw, W. W. Watkins, W. H. Grounds (Stockton Heath), Mr. and Mrs. A. Radtirth (Geappenhall). Mr. and Mrs. J. Hose (Stratton), Mr. Intl Min. John Jackson (Moore), Mr. H.Kay (Whiney), gind Mr. A. Potts (Preston Brook).</p>
        <p>CANADIAN EMBARGO ON ENGLISH CATTLE In consequence of the outbreak of foot and mouth disease in England an embargo has been placed by the Canadian Department of Agriculture on the importa- tion into Canada of all English livestock except horses It is feared that this embargo will seriously interfere with the importation into Canada of thoroughbred stock from Great Britain thin year, unless the disease is stamped out before the month of May next, when the season for importing cattle for breeding purposes gener- ally begins. There is no foot and mouth disease in Canada, and the Canadian Government is prepared to go to any lengths in order to ensure the con t inued immunit y of Canadian herds.</p>
      </div>
