        <p>small-holder ; this is by purchasing late hatched fowls from neighbouring farmers and fattening theta off fr market. Often enough you are able to buy a sufficient quantity to keep sou employed until early spting hatching begins. 'The quantity reared will be your ' best guide as to what class id Market to seek. If you intend opening out on a large scale then you cannot hope to dispose of them all privately; but in any case I should strongly advise you tr obtain good private customers as already pointed col yno could always I send the surplus to a wholesale market.</p>
        <p>I'EEDINf, FM: TAIN.E. The feeding of fowls for table is very different front feeding fowls for egg production. Their die t should consist of Sussex ground oats, middlings, and barley meal mixed with skimmed milk; if the milk is at t procurable then fat in some form should be added when the food is melded. To be sure that the fat is of Rood quality and pure, it would pay you to purchase direct front the butcher and render it down yourself A very good and easy way of doing this, is by placing a piece of meat-fat in the water and when welt-boiled add it to the meals. The mixture should not be given too sloppy but sufficiently moist to pass into the system quickly. Feed them as often as they will eat ; but see that they clear up one meal before being fed again. As noon as you are anti-lied that they have reached good condition they should be killed off. It is a mistake to keep them on beyond this •s sometimes they are apt to ao back in condition. The period for fattening a raw young cockerel in about four or five weeks. 0; course when the cramming system is adopted the procese is much quicker ' in ' fact, the Stases fatteners can turn them out in good condition in about three weeks. In conclusion, thin article specially written for small-holders is in no wise conclusive, and any question asked by my readers I shall be most happy to answer and give you all the assistance I can. THAT COMPETITION!</p>
