      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>(By Redcap.)</p>
        <p>SMALL HOLDINGS. (Continued). Last welt when dealing with this subject I recom- mended specialising with one breed or variety. I did not wish to infer that you should specialise with a pure breed only. As a matter of fact I would far rather recommend a awful first cross in preference to pure bred birde. The ails of the small-holder should 'be • useful general purpose fowl. And while we have many pore breeds almost fulfilling these requirements, I should always advise a first cross. Inasmuch as the farmer finds it necessary to crossbreed his stock, so it is with poultry. Judicious cross-breeding means vigour and fertility. There in just as much scope for specialising with crossbreds as with pure breeds. And if I select a few of the beat it may help the small- }midst in deciding what lie ehould really go in for.</p>
        <p>The two salient utility points are eggs aod table birds. It may be however, that Horne would find a profitable market for table birds alone. But it is often and nearly always advisable to cater for both. So for the present I shall devote myself to writing on the best all-round first-crosses. After some little experience I shall place Houdao—White Orpington first on the list. In these twu breeds we have many useful utility points. The progeny of such a combination make an almost perfect general-purpose fowl. As egg producers they excel many other breeds; as table birds they are most suitable, the white flesh and shanks being a most important point for culinary purposes. The white Orpington is a wonderfully good winter egg- producer, and it is bard to heat the Hoodoo as a summer layer of large white-shelled eggs. The Orpington is a sitter, the lioudan a non-sitter; so you see that the aitting propensities of the Orpington would be somewhat reduced as a result of cross- breeding. For the small-holder I knots of no better first cross. Cockerels from such a mating make fine table fowls, while the pullets make most prolific egg- producers both winter and summer. Similar remarks apply to the Hoodan—Buff Orpington cross,and while I prefer the white Orpington 1 hhould most certainly recommend the buff if I had any difficulty in obtain- ing Whites of a good strain.</p>
        <p>114,U0AN-FAVEROLLE.</p>
        <p>... Next to the Houdan —Orpington I recommend the above cross. When off a good strain the Faverolle to a good winter layer. I am afraid that it is eome- what difficult to get a good strein now-a-days. There has been far too much in-breeding of late years and the Faverolle has suffered as a consequence. However, if you are able to obtain a good strain they are a very ' good breed to cross with the Hondas. Once started ' they are soon improved in the procens of cross- breeding. Another useful first cross is the Houdan Dorking. They are not no extensively bred ; perhaps it is on account of their not being so good layers as the Orpington and Faverolle.</p>
        <p>_ _ TABLE MIMS. To the small-holder who intends specialising with table fowls the two following first crosses are !unbeatable. Indian--Game— Doi king and Indian , Game—Orpington. There are other useful first crosses, but I need not spare the time in deliberations when there are none better than those already named. With the introduction of the Indian Game blood you have always the yellow leg to contend with, and while this is no real detriment some fastidious people object. Other fine qualities far out-weigh this detail and is very often the means of removing predudice.</p>
        <p>sYSTEMATISIN" OPERATION.</p>
        <p>To make the poultry branch of • smell-holding profit- able it needs systematising. Hapayard calculations and trusting to luck " sort-of-business " never pays First you must make up your mind what you intend going in for. Theo with all the energy and ability you posses, throw yourself into the business. Do not be disappointed if the fret season doesn't pan. Remember that yen are building up a linsinees and " Rome was not built in a by !" Neither is a utility poultry business. Breed good stuff and obtain fair prices for your products, and in time you will be succe.sful.</p>
        <p>1f4.“ AND _TABLE TO% S.</p>
        <p>.„... ...... Let us suppose that you intend catering for the above. You must start hatching in February and con- tinue till the end of April. During these three months hatch out every egg possible. At the end of this period devote as much time and energy to rearing the stock as you possibly eta ; and when the youngsters are Liirly well matured, venerate the cockerels from the pullets. Straightway you should commence feeding the cockerels for market ; Either in a fattening pen or by the cramming system. There would be far Item labour involved by adopting the fattening shed than by cramming, and I would advise you to adopt such a system. The pullete should be fed sparingly ; that is, of course, if they are allowed a free range. In the meantime r.ou should be OD the lookout for a market for the table birds. The most profitable is certainly a private one. If you ,cult make a running contract with a good hotel or restaurant it would Rave much labour in soliciting family orders ; not only so but in so doing you often enough get an order for a reinter supply of egg.. After having cleared your stock of table fowls special attention should be paid to the pullets. These should be separated in lots of not more than twenty and brought on by good feeding to laying point. Well housed and properly fed you should ',rye no difficulty in keeping up a good winter egg supply. j Continue operations with a •iew to eggs until hatch- ' iog begins in the following season ; and as you require the room sell off the laying stock—for which you should be able to obtain good prices.</p>
        <p>,pTIIE/1</p>
        <p>We have dealt with one branch. now about the other; that of table fowl production, pare and simple. This branch knows no end. You commence hatching operations and continue all the year round; that is of course, if you intend to make a thorough 'mimes of it. Tne main difficulty is rearing the chickens during the winter months. To cope with this difficulty you would be compelled to erect a brooder-house. This is a construction artificially heated but with access to the open air; iu a word it is a foster-mother on a large scale. There is an alternative to winler rearing and one which may be very profitably adopted by the</p>
        <p>,Old tlabar CHICALA A DRY FEED</p>
