      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ORDER OF FORESTERS.</head>
        <p>ORDER OF FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO BRO LLEWELLYN DAVIES.</p>
        <p>Col. Hall Walker, MP. and Investments.</p>
        <p>The Ancient Order of Foreeters of the Warrington district held • highly auccemful smoking concert on Saturday evening at the Lion Hotel. The large room was well filled, about 300 members being present. Before the concert began, Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., for Widnes and Mr. J. Fulton-Smith were initiated honorary members of the Order. Bro. Ononut MAYBURY, D.C.R., presided, sod among those present were Bros. John Cbrimes, P.D.C.R., Henry Maybary, P.D.C.R., Llewellyn Davies, P.D.C.R., Into as.. Alderman Tinnion, P. Peacock, P.D.C.R., Owen, P.D.C.R.,W.Hipkiss, P.D.C.R., Robert Thornley, D.S., P.D.C.R., and Bancroft, P.D.C.R., C.D.T. ' "</p>
        <p>Communications of apology were received from Mr. Arthur Crosfleld, Major F. hl. Appleton, Dr. Martin, and Dr. Smith. The CHAIRMAN remarked that it was 77 year. since the Ancient Order of Foresters was estaulished and during that time they must have had a great deal of info- once for the welfare of the community., Applausaghey had 2733 l branches registered in the United Kingdom. ' ' '</p>
        <p>The CnsinmAsi proposed the toast of the Kil; and aid they were looking forward to the Coronation when they Intended to niake a big display in the town. They would .bow by their presence and numbers on that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>'W EL</head>
        <p>'W EL</p>
        <p>BRO. LL</p>
        <p>AVIES.</p>
        <p>occasion that they were an organization which was worth taking online of. (Applause.) Ile mentioned that the late Queen Victoria was a patrnnese of the Order and said that the present Queen before her marriage was a financial member of the London district.</p>
        <p>The toast wan drunk, with musical honours. The toast of the four newly initiated honorary mem- bers was entrusted to Alderm. Tinnion, who at the outset of his speech referred to the presence of so many veterans whom they were all pleased to nee. He men- tioned especially Bro. Tilling, to whom theyeatended hearty sympathy owing to the lose of a promising sod dear son (Captain Tilling). They were all delighted, ho continued, to receive the handsome stag'. head from Sir Peter Welker some little time ago and although he wan not a member they hoped to welcome him amongst them lan abort tittle. (Applause). Alluding to the new boo, member. the Alderman mid that he mold certainly say that they had not been attracted by the funeral benetits—(laughter)--or any other financial considers. Moon. Rather had they been attracted to the society on account of the valuable work which they recognized Forestry was doing. (Applause). When the new mom. bars fully grasped sod became acquainted with the great desires and aims of the Order they would never regret the steps they had token that night. (Applause). The objects of the Order were to inculcate thrift, self- help and brotherhood nmong the members. The new 'members would be benefited by joining, sod the Order would also be benefited. (Applause). The toast was cordially received. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>1 i.;"-r:F:lyin-i;-ii;;;.ii;;;i76;i7 w,...ER .ia that friendly societiee net before the people the noble senti- mentof thrift, and if they were all thrifty there would be no necessity for old-age pensions. If they were not euocessful it might not be their own fault. They could not all prosper and command the eeme capabilities. It can Fate ; it was ordained, they might say, by the Almighty that they could not all succeed, and much organisations in that came along with A helping band. (Applause.) Continuing, the Colonel maid that all the friendly societies' Investments were more or law in connection with lend, mortgages, and cottage property, How past legislation might affect their investments time alone would prove. Whether it injured them or did not, at all event. the working classes) through the friendly societies were interested in land to an enormous extent, and, therefore, whe they heard of certain classes only being associate with that kind of investment, they knew perfectly wet that they were connected with it just am much es are. body else. Now they had such eecurities in danger, 1 end if they had Nislation for one side or the other 1 which was not regular, and which interfered ith 1 people putting money into land no an investment, here on no manner or means of Belling their levee ems, and in the inveetments might fall from 119.000, the capital of the Order of Foresters) to £6ollo.oby one etroke of the pen. The speaker also contend that by State insurance the sentiment of thrift whic friendly societies deeired to net up would be nil a nag the people, because they would realise that when t ey were in need the State would relieve them. Mr. Fet,voN-Sannt proposed the toast o "The Ancient Order of Nei-este!..." He thanked the istrict for initiating him as a member of that int °rtent organization and observed that it was bight satis- factory to know that the financial position was very strong end that it had attained above whet was own as the "solvent point." the minimum of whic was fixed at lie. 011. per £l. They had, he was gla to note, considerably exceeded that point. (Apple, se). He congret elated them that they were able to boil op Diode for future contingencies, and that there All a very substantial amount of capital invested in h ghly satisfactory ...ride.. By being membern of a fri ndly society et that character it made them more lode ad- _</p>
