      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>COULD HE WORK 1</head>
        <p>COULD HE WORK 1</p>
        <p>Interesting Case at the County Court.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his HODOOr Judge Hoary Stanger, K.C. Mr. E.A.Connor (solicitor), applied on behalf of John Oulton, labourer, Green-lane. IA inwich for continuation of computation pry from Memo. Henry Omen and Sons, contractors. Cheatham, Manchuter, for whom Mr. Lee, barrister, "r 17.1. 41. A. Connor raid that Oulton was s bricklayer's labourer and was employed by Messrs. Green and Sons, at the old taper mills at Newtoule-Willows, starting In Anemic 1910. On August 21st he met with an sodden when be was following hie usual employrout by brick falling from. scaffold shoot 17 feet high. Thin struck him on the shoulder knocking him scainst a standard. However.be did not leave his work until Aug 24thothen he found he was unable to continue his work . be sensed shout nt 4d per week and respondents paid half earnlnges to him for seven wesksmhen they stoppe payment. Oulton returned to his work on December 19th, but after three days he left ale employment, after baring informed his foreman that he could not continue. Since then and up to now the respondent bad not paid any compensation. The applicant was suffering from neurasthenia and was incapable of doing any work. In reply to Mr. Lee, applicant said he could not,d o !aoy work still. He was willing to try sad don 11th. ' work each day. After medical evidence for both sides had been glee as regarded Oulton's condition Mr. Henry Green, contractor, Cheethom Hill, Mon chester, applicants employer. stated tint •fter the accident be measured the scaffold from which the brick fell, and found it to he twelve feet. He was prepare to offer Oulton light employment for two months t see whether be could do any work.—Applicant solicitor accepted this proposal, and the cue w adjourned until June 12th.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. H. Field Lectures on America. Under the auspices of the Literary and Philvsophicali Society of the Rwanda Recreation Clnb, a lecture on America was delivered by Mr. H. H. Field (general manager of Messrs. Ryland. Bros. Ltd.) on Wednesday evening. Mr. Leslie presided over a large number of workmen and friends. who assembled in the concert room of the club. The Lecturer, with the aid of lantern slides made. his subject very intereatingand enlivening his story with humorous sallies he described the journey through Canada, down the centre of the States, through Mexico and the Southern provinces. The pictures showed the various cities of the constrict mentioned and also the chief industr..es, with especial regard to those where wire was extensively used. Mr. Field said that some portions of Canada especially in the Far Went were being cultivated by Yankees and although they might be the best ~f Yankees would rather that they were c-ilonised lip Britisher.. He considered that ranada gave a fine recipe fur young men who were ii ,t afraid of work and he advi;ed all those who were not settled here and silo had no prospects before them to go oat three. The lecturer was liPar ti ly thanked on the prop-sitica of Mr. Massey seconded by Mr. Knott.</p>
