        <p>Irrelevant Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>In the [louse of Commons, on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Sykes (I".,KnutsfordMaked some questions c,incerning ! the Air Battalion. Ile mentioned that he was present at a meeting where Mr. Grahame-White, rtn an expert, was demonstrating what he could .lo in the way of carrying lespatelics over the country. The Speak, : These reminiscences would be more interesting on another vote. (Laughter). Mr. Sykes asked a question an to the weight of the men who were to form part of the Air Battalion. The Speaker : he number of the men appears in the Vote, but nothing about their weight. (laughter.) Mr. Sykes asked whether in fixing the number of men he could afford to take on in the Air Battalion Mr. Haldane took int, consideration the fact that in order to teach theta it was necessary to lake down the telephone wires stretching over Sainsbury Plain ? (Laughter). Was it not the fact that these telephone wires— The Speaker : That does not arise on the number of men voted to the army. Mr. Sykea regretted that he should have offended against the rules, and proceeded to put his question in another may. The Speaker The hon. memlocr would be getting behind my ruling in that way. Mr. Sykes : I apologise, anti will act proceed further (resuming his eat).</p>
