      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BORDER CITY.</head>
        <p>THE BORDER CITY.</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY THE DEPUTY TOWN</p>
        <p>CLERK.</p>
        <p>An interiefing lecture on " Carlisle the Border City " was delivered be Mr. A. T. Ilaßalmy, the Ihputy Town Clerk, before a goal atandence of members of the Warrington Society at the Old Academy on Monday evening. The chair wan occupied by Alderman 'Fluid.. Mr. Ilallaway traced the city'e history hack t, the days of Augustus, Whell it was one of a border hoe ol powerful military olefin.. It wan flaring the reign of Henry Plantnamiet that the city became a munici- pality of the English realm, the first rreord of a Mayor tieing in lito. The right of local self-government was rota' ned until the advent of the Local Governmeot B.,ard Mr. Ilallaway dealt in a particularly interesting tininner with the OA? ly days of municipal Government in Carliele, aml his contratne with present-day civio management were both apt and pleasing. We quote the following in rxien.to. • -</p>
        <p>-- - The custom of carrying a sword and evict, in front of Mayors is quite universal brit there am few towns which do no as a matter of right. Prior to the year eacept from a primal of the charter. an I the scanty records of the Merchant Guild we can only conjecture as to the municipal life of :our city. But in that year the keeping of a record of the doings of the Town Council was commenced. The Book which in called the Dormont Book is Mill in existence. It trot contains a code of byelairs for the government of the city, which are most quaint and interesting and ran be compared honorably with the queritionable results of centralised wisdom as represented by tiro products of the Local Government Board,and in emery towns I am not sure that the business would not be better c mducted if the old Carlisle Byelaws with regard to the conduct of the business by the Town Council were in force.</p>
        <p>The proceedings were evidently held in 4,aware no we find that the door was to be locked :Lod the key laid beside the Mayor. Each c aincillor had lo give Iris opinion in order sad anyone who internlpted was pit out. They had n moet effectual way of settling the minority:— Item. "If any counselor bops present he obstinate or led by affection and stand in ple against alt his fellows beyng counselors an.l not reconailed that then the nisyr either to millilitre Lyra extremely or else to esclude from that company for that cause or matter only and the determination of the residue of the Counsele to he good and effectual." Might this not be recolunended to the local city Fathers</p>
        <p>• A Carlisle Town Councillor in those days had not all his own way. If he stayed away to avoid an awkward debate he was fined fis. SI If he disagreed with the majority the Mayor as we have heard punished him " extremely" or put him out, and thin was a k the last of his troubles lie after his harried exit, assisted we may be permitted to imagine by the Mayoral boot, he was precluded from reshing to his friends and pouring his troubles into their ears, for by another byelaw a Councillor who dieclose Council businesa .</p>
        <p>" Was not hereafter to he taken as one of the Counsole, but ele,rly objected from the ume as a man not worth., vocation."</p>
        <p>Furthermore he wan not allowed even to bear a grudge againat a fellow Councillor, for if he diil, the Mayor awl four other Councillors tinder the oth Byelaw would proceed to "appease " it for hint and it he would not be appeaeed he was immediately expelled. and he was similarly treated if he railed against his fellows. No one had to sue the Mayor and bailiffs without first applying to the Council—the 1 penalty for this was imprisonment. Thus the Mayor of Carlisle, if he hal the Majority of the Town Council behind him, could exclude the minority from the Council ; and put them in prison if they tried to find a remedy at Common Law. The majority had also a further lower. The :Mail bye- law prohibits any inhabitant from speaking or repeat- ing any unlionest, undecent or slanderous words or unreverently using them against the Mayor or Giuncil, First offence YOrs. if a Councillor, and eximlsion from the Council for the do,l. If a Commoner (i.e. not n Councillor) he lose hie freedorne, and much other punishment as the Mayor and six other touncillors might direct. For a toil. offence he was excluded front the City. . .</p>
        <p>'lke 12th' and 13th Byelaws remind us of the I troubled times in which the Citizens were ' living ; how every man had to attend and areitit the Mayor in case of stray Cr "soden fyer" and the minute instruction, as to setting the City Watch and the locking of the lintesi. No uncharted Scot was to dwell within the City or us liberties upon payo fan toe bylaws put it) of forfeiture of his gods and punishment of his body. I No ticutchman or woman could walk in the city after the Warch Bell was rung. The Town Council also carried their researches into domestic life. They did not believe in walking the streets after dark; and the youths who make a promenade of Sankey-street and Bridge-strect would have had a very bad time, for byelaw WI nays : lint that nos mans sones servants nor appren- tices shall walk or goe sbrod in the street within tlie eitie aftr tene of the club in the nyght except it be upon tirmt fathers or meters busier,e, upon pays of puoyshment two hours in the stak the noxt day after and further to be orderyt by the mayra' discretion.</p>
        <p>e strange part about this, byelaw is that the pro- Itihitimi is ouly extended to a tuan's sons. Tho idea of daughters going out after dark was evidently not even thought of ! " . . .</p>
        <p>Provision was also made for the sanitation of the City, and the cleanliness of the et rests. Carts wore tl be left in ilie streets for more than three nights. Middens not to be felt at the iv mt doors form .re than eight dnys. Every inhabitant bad to clean the pavemeut in front of his house weekly or at leant once a month ; probably every one took advantage of the elasticity of the byelaw and made it monthly.</p>
        <p>The regulation of the markets is the subject of many rule.. Free Trade ditl not enter into the calcu- lation of the Council. They looked with the greatest horror upon a middlentio and did their best to bar him out. The great object wan to get plenty of good goods at a reasonable price. The tenants of the Corporation fisheries were bound fit bring to the city half the fish they caught. Fore- stallern i,•. people wino bought •ieteals he. on the way to market or before the Market Bell had rung,intend- Mg to resell at a profit were neverelydealt with, being expelled from the city. There was no po‘sibility of putting up the prices of neceenaries, as the prices of bread, beer, and wine were settled by the Council. All skins heti to be broughrto market with the flesh ; and no butcher or glover was allowed to tan the sheep skins, the reason for this being to prevent them bold- ing the skin. back until the prices were high. Probably the tanner, were in ascendancy rn the Town Council at the time. 'There are various byelawa UT the reputation of the city courts. Both foreigners and outman could sue and be sued. When a foreigner sued a foreigner, the court impounded as many of their goods as it could,so as to have a hold upon the litigant.. An interesting point to note in the city court. was that a married woman might sue and be stied ad a femme sole. woman's rights being recognised to that exteut. Theo, Byelaws showed a strong detertnimition OD behalf of the citizens to govern themselves properly ; and I am not at all sure that the Mayors of the present tlay would not welcome the wide powers of the old Mayors of Carlini° in dealing with the stalku, troubles that occasionally seine. A vote of thanks was unanimously accorded the lecturer, on the prop,ition of the Omits.. (who in himself a ('umbrian) seconded by Mr. F. H. 11.81111,11T5, told supported by Mrs. Rotteur Gaitttcrr (another Cumbrian).</p>
      </div>
