      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ALTRINCHAM LIBERALS. Sir William Crossley and the Next Election.</head>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM LIBERALS. Sir William Crossley and the Next Election.</p>
        <p>At the annnal meeting of tho Altrinchain Nviaion Liberal Asanciation, held on Saturday afternoon, Sir Willi un Crossley. who was defeated at the last election, made an important declaiation as t, his future intentions. The alerting was presided over toy Mr. W. E. Thompson, chairium of the Executive, soil there was a large attendance of rep einutativen from , all path; of the division. ! The Chairman, in proposing the adoption of the report, said it should not ho the policy of the meeting to blame one another for their nine, but it would be their duty to improve their organisation with an eye .to the flame. If they were tius to their principles !and energetic in the application of them, he thought they might look forward with confidence to the time ‘vhen they had another chyme in the division. Thin led them to think of who would lead theism when the next hiilmt came. They had only one man who had been the firat Liberal member fir Altrincham and theme was ~sly man whom they 17 add look to as the enilimlamo. (Applause.) As the one who was, perhaps, more ti blame than anyone for the catastrophe, lie would like to ask Sir William Crosaley to at all events give him a chance, and when the time mine he had no doubt of the success of their cause. (Applause).</p>
        <p>A resoloth,n was moved by Mr. Reid, wended by kfr../. 11. Greenhow, expressing profound dissatisfaction with the geowing expenditure on arma- ments. Mr. Owen opposed the tesoltition on the ground that its effect would be to einbarrase the Government. Sir William Crossley and a few other, voted against in, but the Chairman announced that it was carried by a large majority. On the motion of Mr. William Gibbon, seconded by Mr. George Rhodes, K.O , a resolution seas quoted approving of tt treaty with the United States for he settlement of all ildernational disputes by arbitration. Sir William Crossley was unanimously eh vied to emceed Sir William Bailey as president of the A 340- c iation for the ensuing year. Speaking of the last election, Sir William said be had failed, but, he added, "I ma not dead yet." (('heers). " I say I urn not dead yet, but I arn getting very oh]. My hair is , getting very white, and it will Ito four veera before another election takes place, unless something happens that we don't expect. . . . If an elect, .n M to come this year, I would fight with al the power I have got in me-- (cheers) but what is the use of my looking forward to for years? Where shall I be in four years P" " Member for Altrincharn P") Ile was hardly strong enough to look forward for four years, as lie felt the strain of each Parliamentary acesion, lon he would . rink it again if the election came soon. (Cheers.) He could not, however, think that lie would be able to do it in three or four years, and Lis advice was to find out a young man. Ile would not leave them in the lurch for a year or no, but his opinion watt that they might to be getting another man who could begin to make himself acquainted with the constituency. His advice to them we not to have a large employer. o,augliter.) Ile exp.:wed too much front ti the enemy. , The Association re-elected Mr. W. E. Thompson, chairman of the Executive, and re-appointed Mr. J. W. Robson legal agent.</p>
      </div>
