        <p>A Socialistic Age."</p>
        <p>The leirtri Union boa compicuouly failed in voneing horning iudignation against the Liberal Government'n land taxation 111 Werrington—in fact very little Interest appears to he taken in the doings of the local breech of Captain Priapism'. organisation. At a public meet- ing held by the branch at the Lion hotel on Monday evening the intereeting fact was disclosed that shoot 2-00 circularn of invitation boil been vent out —mostly to email owners—nnd the meeting had been well • advertised. When the proceedings began, the attend- anon numbered 11l end during the evening it was swollen to the iniposing dinienaions of 21. The chairman (Mr. W. Farms.), however, found it consoling to reflect that although the audience was email it wee very select. The Breeches, of course, took the form of the usual touching meals to the otnali property owner and the thrifty woreingroemlittle being said about the woes of the rich lendowner. The prin- cipal speaker was 1t... Rev. J. Jeffery Vince whose subject ens" The work and polio, of the land Union." There were also on the platform Sir Gilbert (lreenall, Bart., the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. C. D. Parkinson, and Mr. .1. E. Seeker (non. secretary). The Citeinsts'r raid the time had come at hen they should It, something to organize theinselvea to protect the land interest, both in a smell and in a greet way. Thin was a Socialistic age—an age of 14ier Hardie:l— aud it therefore behoved them to combine to protect their interests. The Government'. land Act was an Act of epoliation and if landowners did not take action they would be very much in the position of the sparrow arid the hawk. Ile could not see why a Liberal Government should have seen lit to pus an Act which would bear so heavily not only upon great landlords but upon small owner. of land and property throughout the country. It would greatly injure the development of land and the free sale of property. In taxing land the Government hod acted inconniatently. They pro. fus