        <p>of a Socialistic nature. I WM. told by • represeste- tire of the Oovernment--• man holding • very high position—that it was quite clear to him that if the Government did not receive fair results from the increment miu, linty, they would move the undeveloped land tan unto land generally and irc aaaaa it from • halfpenny to • penny. Therefore It I. absolutely necessary that we should band °tweet.. together to protect our interests." (Applause The New N, cAnt,terici mid the moat foolish things about the Art were the •aluation classes. lie made • challenge that anyone who approved of Mc. Lloyd Geirge's land taxes thould itelect any house or Muir, he liked within say a quarter or half•mr lo radruuf Market Cate, and he (Or. Carleton) ventured to say he could show that the sits value of the property as arrived at by the ntethod that Mr. Lloyd-George suggested would not Ire the true site val.—that wet, it would not be the selling value. lie never kn. before that any commodity or property had any other value than the selling vol.. Mr. Lloyd-George had Invented a value of his own when he said that in order to got at the site value of • boo., the first thing they had to do was to valon the whole of the property. That might be fairly essy, but neat they had to •alue the hones apart from the site and deduct that value from the valoe of the whole of the property in order to ascertain the site ' value. The diMmilty nitS that they could not val. • house •p•rt from the site 0p.,11 a hick it mood. A hot., was not • bowel without a site, any more than • oolTee kettle would still be a kettle if the bottom were knocked out—it would eimply be • cylinder. Numerous questions were then asked and Mr. Vince anaivered them. . .</p>
