      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>............---,--,,,,, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductfil by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>............---,--,,,,, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductfil by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>THE EVENING STAR..</p>
        <p>There is a good opportunity just now to observe what is commonly called the even- ing star for it is shining brightly each evening in the western sky. But I shall have to begin by telling my readers that the "Evening Star" is not a star at all ; it is the planet Venus. A planet, of course, is very different from a star. What we describe as stars are great luminous bodies like our sun and they are immense distances away from us sur- rounded perhaps by a lot of little worlds like ours which are too small for us to see. The planets are different. They are bodies like our world and they go round the sun. They are not bright in themselves but when the sun shines on them they glisten just as the moon does.</p>
        <p>Just now we can see the side of Venus which is facing the sun and it looks very beautiful indeed. If you will look towards the west after the sun has set you will see Venus shining out in the sky. We shall be able to see Venus for about six months. At present it dips out of sight about nine o'clock but it will get higher in the sky as the summer approaches. Whilst looking at Venus it will be interesting to see if you can find Saturn, which is also to be seen in the west. The two planets will get quite close to each other about the middle of next week, or, as the astronomers say, will be in conjunction, and very pretty they will look if the skies are clear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>THIS WEEK'S STORY</head>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY</p>
        <p>DIFFICULT QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>"How many seed compartments are there in an apple?" he queried. No one knew. "And yet," said the school inspector, "all of you eat many apples in the course of a year. and see the fruit every day probably. You must learn to notice the little things in nature."</p>
        <p>The talk of the inspector impressed the children. Tbey earnestly discussed the matter at play time, and the teacher over- heard this conversation in the play-yard: A little girl, getting some of her companions around her, gravely said : "Now, children, just s'pose that I'm Mr. Inspector. You've got to know more about common things. Now tell me how many feathers has a hen." MASTER JOHN lIORNER UP TO DATE. One of my readers sends me the following very clever version of the story of little Jack Horner. It is a satire upon the people who use big words thinking they are better than 'simple ones.</p>
        <p>"YE OLDE 2,.URSERIE LEGENDE IMPROVED."</p>
        <p>Maater John, the youthful and diminutive scion of the diatinguisherl House of Horner, had one day deposited himself in the angle formed by two of tie vertical planes which constituted the boundary of his apartmcnt. He was temporarily cccupied in trans- ferring to his internal regions a palatable comestible, concocted with especial reference to the annual mid- winter festival. Dexterously he introduced into its interior recesses the two-jointed digit which forms no valuable an auxiliary to the other phalanges by which the upper limbs art terminated. With considerable triumph and supreme sat sfaction, the youthful explorer extracted therefrom a fruit, which is usually deemed indiapentable ti culinary concoctions popular at the festive mason to -which reference has been made.</p>
        <p>Master John, Monsieur Jean, Mynherr Johannes or Signorino Giovanni (a hichever form the reader may prefer), then took advantage of th-• occanon to male n remark, which combined the simplicity and ingenuous• nese of youth with the intr, spection and perspicacity usually attributable to riper years. It wan to the following effect: "To what a remarkable extent i- my individuality characterised by a degree of virtue, to which I take this appropriate opportunity of (Ling definite and adequate justice!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>-THEIGOIN'-TO DO.</head>
        <p>-THEIGOIN'-TO DO.</p>
        <p>There's a peer liitle thing that lives among boys, That hides in their homes, their books and their toys; No one has described it, though some say it's bhae ; It has such a strange name, it's called Gain-to-do.</p>
        <p>I think to a fairy ; not content is this elf To be around with a boy—it gate into his self. He forgets his own name (George, Willie or Who n Bays he's somebody else. "I'm a G in'-to do." His father snys, "Willie, I want you to go On an errand," or "There's weeds in the garden to</p>
        <p>hoe," But while father is waiting and work in the view Willie's watching the pranks of this queer Goin'to-do his mother says, "Willis, ben good hide man ! I know you can help me if you say that you can." " Yes, mother," says he. Just then, strange, but it's</p>
        <p>_ true, In dies like a bird that queer gaolif-to-do. Ills teacher says, " Willie, give atlention to me.' And Willie says, " Yes, a greet scholar I'll be." But the studies are bard and tbe questions not few And he can't, see his books for a big (loin-to-do.</p>
        <p>So every day when this elfin he meets, He loves to play with him though he knows that he cheats; And night aft r night he finds something that he ' cheats : Because he's been fooled by this queer</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>As a singer of popular songs Miss Florrie Gallimore who is appearing at the Palace this week, stands pre-eminent. Her name is usually coupled with that well-known song, " You want to go to Tokio," I,r it was she who first made the air so popular with the isiblic generally. Miss Gallimore, though at time. perhaps a little rough-anthready in her style, is never- the-less bright and breezy enough to suit the majority, and this week it is simply by her verve and " go " that her turn is such a successful one. Warrington audiences, at any rate, have shown their appreciation of her efforts. The remainder of the programme is entirely satisfactory. The sketch " Our Mary Jane," presented by Miss Florence Smithent, is a side- splitting absurdity with an abundant vein of humour. There is, of course, very little plot, but Miss P. Smithers as the unquenchable "maid of all work " has all the humorous attributes of that most necessary ad pinch of modern domestic life. The other members of the cast are Mr. Brooks (the uncle), Mr Owen Dacray ; Jack Harrison (his nephew), Mr. H. Reynolds-Owen ; Mrs. Ilarrison, Miss Lillie Arnold. The Merrion.s (Will, Harry and Ted) as comedy vocalists, instrumentalists, and sketch artistes are novel and amusing. Clever dancing is the chief characteristic of the Three Duggans, whe accomplish some remarkable feats on roller and ice skates, and some wooden-shoe and sand dancing. They put the final touch on a charming display by a little very acceptable singing The other artifacts are The Three Krakes, in an amusing comedylact entitled "The Electric Tram Conductor " ;Wat Tyler, a character vocalist, in a vocal scene, I The Desecrated Home" ; and Tod Lowen, a versatile comedian. " Perfection " pictures are shown. and Mr. E. Widdop conducts some excellent music' in Ms usual able style.</p>
        <p>HOME RULE FOR SCOTLAND. The Prime Minister received in his privet:. TOOM at ' the House of Common. on Wednesday evenihg a small deputnion of Scottish Liberal members, mho pr.- meted the resolution recently passed by the National Committee, urging that • measure of Home Role for Scotland should be passed duripg the present Parlia- ment. The Prime hliniater who was accompanied by Mr. J. W. Oulland, M.P., replied at some length.lppeek. jag en a Scottish member of twenty-five peers siding hemid he was fully conscious of the growing di nlty of Scottish affairs receiving proper considerati n at Westminster. lie had always pressented the ca./ for Irish Home Hole as part of a fuller scheme. He was fully alive, he added, to the force of the arguments which bed been addre,fred to him. He underttiols to convey the views of the deputation to his colleigoes, who, be doubted not, would receive them sympathetically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HAREM SKIRT IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HAREM SKIRT IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Photox Tonge.</p>
        <p>Ell COS:</p>
        <p>accustomed to severely conventional fashions. The troueer-skirt has, however, received the approval and admiration of a famous English artist and a well- known London maffstrate. Both Mr. Harry Fuming and Mr. Cluer commend the new form of feminine attire as a healthier, cleaner and more artistic thing than the ordinary skirt. The above illustrations show two members of Messrs. Button's staff costumed in the new germents. The ' one on the left is an example of the indoor harem gown for reception, bridge parties or home wear. The trousers, which are of oriental printed foulard, are gathered in to the ankle with bow of rot* coloured ribbon. The tunic, back and front, in of rose coloured satin, and bodice and sleeves are trimmed with name. The photograph on the right shown a tailor-made harem costume in navy serge. This is a smart specimen of the style brought out specially for out-door wear. In repose the dresa has the appear- ance of an ordinary skirt. The trousers only show slightly, but give perfect freedom in walking and consequently are much more convenient than any variety of the narrow skirt now no much worn.</p>
