      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HAREM COSTUME.</head>
        <p>HAREM COSTUME.</p>
        <p>Warrington has not had its harem skirt street sensation. It has had something better. Thanks to Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-street, the ladies of the town have had the opportunity of inspecting the new garment iatelligently and at leisure. On Tuesday and the two following days this enterprising firm held, as is usual at this time of the year, a parade of the latest fashions for ladies, including the much-talked-of harem costume. Although the intimation of this unique exhibition simply took the forte of Messrs. Dultmi's customary spring announcement, the mere mention of the harem skirt being on view had the effect of thronging the showrooms to an extraordinary extent, it being estimated that some 3,000 ladies passed through to see the modelli between Tuesday and Thursday. As the name suggests, the harem skirt is an adaptation of the garment worn by Turkish women and its admirers claim that it is "sensible, hygienic, graceful and unrivalled for comfort and elegance." As to whether such praise is justified there is roost for two opinions. and ladies who have bad the courage to appear in the coeturrie in the atreets clone large town,, have aroused much cariosity and excitement among a populace</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ORDER OF DRUIDS.</head>
        <p>ORDER OF DRUIDS.</p>
        <p>King William Lodge Hot-Pot. Quite an enjoyable time was spent in the large room of the Royal Oak Hotel, Bridge-street, on 'faraday ,rvening when a hot-pot supper and concert were redulged in by members and friends of the Ring William IV. lodge of the Warrington Er' mistimed District of Druids, Bro. G.M. J. T. Rushton preeided over the gathering. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and Alderman Smethurst sent lettere of apology for non.attendance. After the loyal toasts had been honoured the Cttoi R. NI AN made. abort speech in which he empba sired the importance of AOCRII IotercOURNIA Such gatherings misted to promote the feelings of brotherlinese and also advanced the welfare of the Order. They stood nun. Order second to none in the town. Their benefits were never withheld or curtailed. Referring to the proposed scheme of State Insurance be said that they would have nothing to fear from legislation of such a character. Rather would they ban need to bail it with delight. Those who might, unfortunately, be atricken with permanent illness, would be protected to the extent of ss, per week. Friendly societies were to be recognized in the scheme and that won. great thing. If they had not here able, by voluntary effort to draw all workmen into friendly societies for the protection of themeelves, they were now to have legislation, which would compel them to band together it they ' were alive to their own interests. It ought to be remembered that there were some whom they could not, according to their rules, accepts. membersauch as the blind end 0080 who had any disewe. They were now going to be provided for and that was a step in the right direction. (Applause.) Those great draw- backs—tontine clubs—whirl friendly societies had hod to contend with, were not going to be recognized. (Applause). Bro. A. T. Cr.ov proposed the toast of " The Order of Druide." He said there must have been great endeeroure on the part of the officials end members to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>16, 18 &amp; 20, Sankey St,</p>
        <p>Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BERLIOZ'S " FAUST."</head>
        <p>BERLIOZ'S " FAUST."</p>
        <p>Some Notes on the Composer and</p>
        <p>his Work. 13Y HENRY BOND.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday next Berlioz'a " Faust " is to be performed in Warrington for the first time—an event not only in itself noteivoriby in the annals of the Warrington Musical S ciety, but one par tidal got a unique character from the fact that the society's members 11 ill, for the purposes of this particular work, be conjoined with those of the Warrington Male Voice Choir. A body of choral voices nearly 300 strong will in this way be under the control of Mr. Crossley's baton - a truly magnificent force, though one that is urgently demanded by the exigencies of the ccmoosern colmaal score, particularly fr the due performance of the important choruses it c n- tains for male voices only. To a large proportion of our townsmen Berlicz's "Faust" will accordingly be a new Pork, the only itrm in it likely to strike a note of familiarity being the Hungarian March—an excerpt which naturally lends itself to occasional performance in the concert room. With a view theiefme of preparing readers to some extent for listening to no important a work, the follow- ing notee have been compiled. Being for the moat part confined to a particular subject,it should he premised that they fail to take cognisance of the magnitude and varied character of Berlioz's work as a whole, icon k which, it should be remembered, extends beyond the dcmain of music into the allied field of literature. Hector Berlioz was born at La Cote St. Andre, near Grenoble, in 1803, and died at Paris in 1889. A man largely out of sympathy with his day and generation, it is riot surprising that he was ala curiously lacking in balance of temperament and character—a weakness which is often a peculiarity of genius. Of an ardent and mercurial disposition, a certain intractability of thought and • disdain of accepted ideas characterises much of his music ---peculiarities which show them- selves at times in quite a contradictmy way. The unequaloese of many of Berlioz's compositions is indeed "thus explicable only "—as the writer of a</p>
        <p>luminous article In Grove's 'Dictionary-1 uts it-, a; the work of a vivid irnaginath n striving to explain itself in a tongue which he never rerfectly under- d," for Berlioz was undoubtedly et his weakest when working according to the strict methods and when dealing with the 1.1(.0111 s of expression which are a feature of the work of the master minds of his Limo. Deficiency of early training and a certain impatience of limiting and environing conditions arc held to account for acme curious perversities evidenced in Berlioz's mimic, for while he possmsed undeniably the faculty of beautiful and unaltoyed melody, this was It times sacrificed to the (-malty a emphasis in such • way as to sungest that nt critical moments he ia, in the language of the writer already alluded to, "Po auxicus to impress that he forgets how to charm." The Evening Song in " Famd." is in thin connection cited as an instance in which atriving for effect in certain cadences over-reaches itself to the clear detriment of a realisation of Berlioz's obvious</p>
        <p>purpose. And, is showing those- ''' ''''' impulses by which Berbo wus 11011.thrlefl moor ri</p>
        <p>another ',umber in " Faust" my be here referred to. 'di/ the' Amen Chortle" on the LIIPIVIO of Blander's . .</p>
        <p>Iso—og. Betlioz never concealed a dislike for, if not a positive contempt of,counterpoint as exemplified in the works of Cherubini, Handel and, there, a circumstance which, when the fact is horns in wind that he himself had little skill in it, might have seggested on au ittule less aggressively bitter towards those comt tome Yet, each was the twist in his ratere that he wan, in this "Amen" fugue, moved to a demons:ration in a der i-i ve crooner of his ability an a contrapuntist which hen in it .omething of the patine of a parade, far though the technicalities of contrapuntal art are here quite rigonusly observe d, it haa been pointed out that the difficulties of this particular fugue are of the rn at elementmy bind. Yrt, despite these anomalies and personal idionvecracies there can be no doubt that Berlioz pweemed geed. of • high rder. Few comp sere are more distinctly original and lower attll lane evolved ideas so forceful in their epordancity and so vigorous in the manner of their sxpreesion. "The Damnation of Faust," first produced in 181 ti, is admitted vault hands to be Berlioz's finere work in the cantata form. Quotog again from Grove we are told that "it is indeed folio in quintessence. All his metal are here, all his defeets,—a fabric now clumsily moven, now I f the closest texture; cruel modulat'orrs and phrast a of ImuntOg sweetness, the moat exquilite tenderness alternating with the wildest violence, all clothed with his vivid colour and with his consummate mastery of orchestral re.ource." Goethe's poems ste known to have early attracted the a ttenti..n of Berlioz they did that of early musical genius, s iuf,oe and after him. With " Fount" be was deeply impressed, and, while quite a young man, he net sonic pot lions of the legend to music which was publisl oil under the title of "Eight Seems from Faust," touch of which material he afterwards remodelled and incorporated with the later and matured cantata. Himself the author to • large extent of his own "book," Berli-w was temp'ed to alter the locale of art of the action and incident if Goethe's "Faust' to Hungary, time making it p, wade to irclude in the score a March he had already composed ashen on a visit to Per,th— the well-known "Rakoczy " founded upon • national air of that country. This " War-song of the Hungarians " as it is sometimes called—though it partakon more of the political than the martial—hrouraht Berlioz into a position of great prominence and ravour, and it is not surprising therefor, that he shorld desire to give a lasting foundation to mu,ic no markedly esteemed. Hence the change of venue, k licence which finds justification in the fact that there are many versions of the Ferret Lagend from which a composer is obviously at liberty to pick and choose, and which,if thought fit, he could vary to suit his own purposes. Among other alterations one involving a change in Goethe. 'denouement calls for particular mentinn. Whereas in Goethe's poem Faust is saved, Berlioz has chosen to five to his work the grim pictureequent as of a climax involving a" Ride to the Abysm" and a scene cast amongst the lord, souls of Pandemonium—the Epilogue on Earth telling how in the sequel 'The wail of souls in torment no more to earth siecended—deep in the vast abyss, to the utter- most doom, went a soul"! One need- hardly remark that from smelt a thought the subpplent " A pother.. of Margherita "is which we are translated to a Boone in Heaven, s th a chomm of celestial spirits, comes in the way of a happy relief. The following lucid summary of the poetic ',object - matter of Berlioz's "Faust" is taken from Mr. Barry's preface to the vocal score published by Mews. Breitkopf And If.irtel. Part I. The opening Scene is laid on a plain in Hungary. Faust, alone and weary of life in cities, apostrophises the tieing sun and the happiness of life in the enuntry. Introduced by an Orchestral Interlude the sound of peasants' merry-making falls morn his can and be envies their innocent pleasure. Anon, on the approach of a band of Hungarian troops, martial stains are heard, and seem to find a warm response in every breast but his own, which remains mute and cold.</p>
        <p>, Fart IT. The Scene changes to Northern Germany. Faust, alone in its study, and in the lowest depths of despair, is on the point of ending his w.-es by poison, but is deterred therefrom on bearing the Easter Hymn sung by a hand of pilgrims in the street below. As their pious strains die away, Mephistopheles suddenly presents himself, and after promising Faust all that Isis soul can desire, the two start off together in Finest of the joys of life and pleasure. They are nest seen in Auerbach's Cellar in Leipzig. Among the salient points of this Scene may be enumerated (I) A Drinking Chorus; (2) Brander'. Song " The History of a Hat," complimented by a Ilegurisrat and a choral Fugue (- Amen ") on the theme of the said song ais a parody of the style of the old masters ; and (3) Mephistophleles Song, " The Iliatory of a Flea," which is no less grotesque. Disgusted at this levity on the part of the topers, and , longing for pleasure of a more ruined character, Faust urges Mephistopheles to depart, and they are transported to the Banks of the Elbe. Here, during a chorue of Sylphs and Gnomes, Faust falls asleep and in his dreams beholds Margaret for the first time The spells, which Mephistlpbelee hart woven around him, have done their work ; and on awaking Faust is inflamed with an inaatiahle desire to meet the Margaret of his dreams in the flesh. Joining a band of soldiers and students, who are marching towards the town, the two wend their way to Margaret's dwelling</p>
        <p>Part 111. Faust having found his way to Margaret's chamber, in a onting and loving mow], examines its contents and apostrophisea its owner. At the eclairs of Mephist Ipheles, who suddenly breahu in upon Lim, he Loire himself at Margaret's approach. As Faust has dreamed of his future love, so Margaret has dreamed of her future lover ; and the pair are there- fore ready to fall in love with each other at first sight. aM rgaret distutbed by her dream and wondering if it '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>forbodes aught, esters, with a lamp in ber hand, and thinking herself alone, while braiding her hair preparatory to retiring to rem, sings the ballad of " The King of Rule, which she had learnt in her childhood. In the street without,: Mephistopheles invokes the spirits of inconstancy and caprico to enchant and compel the maiden to yield to Faust's love. After a dance rf round Margaret's house (Orchestral), and a mocking Serenade by Mephistopheles (with Chorus of Spirits) the Scene changes to the interior of Margaret's dwell- ing. Margaret and Faust are alone, and give voice to their feelings in a passianate love-doer. Mephistopheles, suddenly entering, warns them that it is time for them to appeals; for, formed by his song, the neighbours are throngieg to the host's', end with them Margaret's mother. Margaret points out to &amp;wit a way of escape through the garden, Lint before taking Lis leave the pair renew their declaration of love, while Mephistopheles looks on triumphantly and rejoicing at the ides ihat he hoe enslaved Faust for ever. A Chores of neighlwmrs, eailng noun Margaret's mother to look _after_ her daughter's safety, close, the Scene.</p>
        <p>Part IV, IlargareCalone in he;i.hamber, bemosns her desertion by Faust Without, fragments of the Soldiers Chorus and Students' Song are heard in the distance.</p>
        <p>From Margaret's Chamber we are transported in the Lnagination to a forest glade, where Fau4, grateful for the peace which Margaret's love Las brought him, addresses himself to the Spirit of Nature. lie is interrupted by Mephstophelers, who tells him that Margaret has been cast into prison on a charge of having poisoned her mother, but promises to release her On Foust signing a parchment which he presents to him. To thin he assents, and the pair start off on a couple of black steeds, as Faust thinks, h r Margaret's prison; but Mephistopheles has got the better of him, and in a Devil's Ride, which has much in common with that of Burger's " Lenore," and other similar legends derived from a common source, gallops him off to the infernal regions. On their arrival in Pandemonium, they are greeted by a Chorus of Lost Souls, and Demons, and in a language which (as we learn from a note appended to the hill score) Sweden- borg detsignated as "I. humus in/creole, et pill ero.Tist en usage parmi lea rb,rnona et lea damnes."</p>
        <p>Epilogoo.Res:. is brought to earth of the awful Iced that has bren wrought below. Above. Seraphs kneel before the Mont High, and plead Margaret's forgiveneas Purged by contrition and redeemed by faith, hope, and love, she is received into heaven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MR. J R. CLYNES, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. J R. CLYNES, M.P.</p>
        <p>A Plea for Trade Unionism.</p>
        <p>" THE HOPE OF THE WORLD."</p>
        <p>Under the auspicen of the Warrington brand, of the One Workers end Demirel Labour... Society, • meeting was hell in the Co-operative Hall, Ceiro.etreet, on Nlonday evening. Mr. Dukee presided over a fair attendance find enoporting him on the platform were Messrs J It Clynes, T Itoh y, K Plinstoo, S llorrocks,C Parker, and J Yates. The CHAIRMAN, in hi. 11,110 n op ech, mentir Ond that , the Union hod about 350 branch. throughout the orrobtry including 52 in hams/dare. The objects of the Union were Ilinli In to all trade unions, let,, encore for the labourer better conditions of work, shorter hours seri better ply. (Applsuso). The c ,nditione of labour for the workingmen of the present day were by no roams rosy. The unity of labour ears the hope of the world, VD it Ilependtd at only the betterment of the individual but the peeoe and prosperity or.ftstO,na. (Airport.).</p>
        <p>M. st.sv (Chairmen of the Warrington Trades ant/ Ltbour Stounzil) said that the Union catered for • clan itf workmen so .1 ide in its scope and operations that there 050 00 semen ashy thoze who were not provided with tt particular anion for a particular trade should not join. The general Inbcurer was one of the wont paid osen who not into • wean' which was because they were sot organised ea they should be and became they stood practically sa an undefended noit, the prey of time who</p>
        <p>pioneliTlD DV TH6III. MINYOUTUNIt. It seas very unfortunate that the general body of labourers were not °remised, for be was convinced ' that such an oriperientinn en that one ',mid nerve ' their interest.. and would very materially improve their 1 tots. short time. (Applasse). Mr. J. It. CLVxm, M.P., n ado • very line mead], Inn lucid explannlino of trade 11301011 principle. being entirely convincing. Tho Oust Gm of defer ore in the workers' ntiuggle for "intentn wan provided,he said,by the trade unions and iris, things we mooed i t ions would be immeasurably worse if it vv, re not for the restrain- iug hand of trade union., who were n great power to Mete things better whenever a difficulty fermented itself. Tiede unions, he said, stood for what the mem- bers wanted—better condition!, better wages, better treatment, a sense of on-corny. They wanted to be sopi 'intended like Mtn, and to te able to feel that in the performance of their daily task, they were not only being tolerated tut respected, to be able to feel that they were beiog treated like human beings, and not so much like machinery for the purpose of cresting wealth (Applause). Tbe Gan Worker, and General Labourers' Union had been in exists°. for 21 yearo, sod hod become firmly establinhed es one of the largest not most active trade miona is the country. Grey asked th• men not only to take an interest in the imntediate at.tivitiee of lite union but to tale an interest In housing questions, became then they would really he taking an lowen in their own homes ; they salted them to take an Interest In the franchise "ten ion, to study social and industrial stehjects so that they would be wise and educate d enough rightly to decide for themselves when they were called upon to cart their voter. (Applause.) In that way trade unions bad become eduoatora They all lime there wm •</p>
        <p>nANGILR IN .10 IGNORANT ;pour, for ignorant people were not mil, a lore to the nation huts distinct lose to theniselrea Trade unions ware interested in raising the standard of understanding so a. to enable their members to perceive where their 01n interests lay. They realised that if any one class were to be saved, redeemed or improved that could not be done by mere outside shin the initiative must also come from the inside. Patronage, sympathy and other side might be good and well so far no they went but the real lasting, permanent, benefit could only result from the determination to put right in the place of wrong. (Appian...) Unlike other union, their union had devised • plan by which men who were out of work and could not pay their contributions could keep in the union without any payment for • reasonable time. (Applause.) Also if • man were working and though he were earning very low weirs the contribution was err low that he avoid afford to pay. They wanted the men who (mold Testify to join the union, because atreegth lay in unitedaction. Probably there were some there who said that their wage. were et, low even when they were working that they could not afford any nub- scriptiun. Why, hew., jllBt the man who was the</p>
        <p>-LOWPAT PAID WHO WARDED TO HP OHO AN,PD. If they looked at the theated trades of this country they fonnd the remarkable fact that they were trades which had never been assisted by trade unioni.m ; they were trades which had gone bad for the capitalists and for the workingmen because there .01 no trade union to waist them. On the other hand it was lathe trades where they had most trade unionism that the greatest prosperity had prevailed. That vow a complete anther to the delusion which they sometime, beard exprothed that trade unionism drove Indostries out of the country and that they did berm to the com- mercial and industrial enterpriser of the country. They toned that where the trades were highly orgthised those businesses were thotheeful, no in the cases of cotton, coal, eogineering and building. Continuing, Mr. Clyne. said that speaking for himself he thought that there were too many trade unions. They could do with fewer unions, and that HOUid increase their greater unity. Meer, board It WWI in the LON% PR PAH, HRANCHAS OP H4DPSTrt where trade unionism am weakest. What he wanted to tell workingmth was that they were too satiated to take toe lathing. of thing., to feel that they were pieced in the schema of Nature just to oriel in order that they might keep alive to creme wealth for other.. There had been too much submissiveness and excess of contentment</p>
        <p>on tir• part of the workingmen of thin country. The class which gave the greatest service to the couetry was the lowest paid ; they were for more entitled to the good thing, of life than the pri•ileged idlers vrho had been able to arrears and obtain a hold of the inatru- meats of producing wealth. Mr. Clyne@ conteeded that those who were not members of the union and received benefits through the efforts of the union should join theatrical. (Applarrae.) in conclusion the speaker said that the increasing arrogance of °vitalism could only be met by combined labour. (Loud applause). • THE CRIMINAL NEGLECT</p>
