      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>Photo 1</p>
        <p>THE RUINS OF THE PATTERN SHOP AND BOILER-SHED. A tire, which assumed alarming dimen- sions, broke out in the pattern shop of Messrs. Vv'hyman's foundry, Dallam-lane, on Saturday afternoon. On discovery of the outbreak, a message was immediately sent to the Fire Station, but unfortunately most of the Brigade were out at practice, only six of the men being on the spot. This number, however, went to the foundry with the utmost promptitude and by their efforts the flames were kept under control until the arrival of the remainder of the Brigade some time later. The flames bad obtained a good hold in the pattern shop, and it was thought likely at one period that they</p>
        <p>would spread to the other parts of the works. A good supply of water, however, was at hand, and the Brigade (who were under the command of Superintendent Turner and the deputy-superintendent extinguished the flames before they spread. The roof over the pattern shop was burnt through, and the Brigade experienced great danger from falling debris and slates. There was no ole about the works at the time of the outbreak, and the origin of the fire is unknown. The damage, which is excessive, and is estimated at over £1,500, is fortunately covered by insurance.</p>
