      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>A SPLENDID YEARS WORK. SUBSCRIPTION LIST RISING</p>
        <p>A most encouraging year's work, from every point of view, was under consideration at tne mutual meet- ing of governors and subscribers of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary which wag held at the Mies Hall on Thursday afternoon .Sir Gilbert G reenall, Bart., presided and amongst others present were The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph). Mr.. Rylande, Min Morris. Mr. W. P. Rylands (chairman of the Board of Management), Mr. George (akin (lion. ,reasurer), the Revs. Canon Willis. W. Bracecemp, H. V. l'ipot and John Yong., the Very Rev. Father Whittle. Captain Crosfield, Alderman Burton, Coon. cube H. Roberts, Items. Charles White, John Fairclough, Roger C. Parr. B. S. Townroe. J. E. Seeker, H. G. Roberts, J. G. With i nshaw. lA-A1b..., 11. Thornton and H. Collin., Drs. Elms., Burt, Tinsley and Mr. J. 11. J. Hampson (mecretary I.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Force itYLANDS, in untying the adoption of the report and accoents, rued the Board of Management made enormous Ilrafts upon the generoeity of the people of Warrington and district in undertaking the eaten- aloe of the institution. The magnificent atm of £16,000 was collected in the abort period of about eighteen months. It naturally followed that the special effort °net have tended to he reflected in a difficulty in obtain- ing that increese in their ordinary income which was neceseary to keep up the enlarged buildings. Con.- quently it was not a matter for surprile that fore time they were faced with heavy delicieucen year by yeer. The extetnion meant an increase in the expenditure of gomething like £1,500 a year—a gap which required greet efforts to bridge over. With great satisfaction he wan now able to draw attention to the fact that the anbscription Bet had exactly doubled since the rear before the extension woe made. , In 1901 it was £561, to day tt was a little over £I,IOO. and they had not yet reached rho limit. (Applause). He looked forward toe further increase,. they Raw a weedy advance in the interest taken in the institution by all their friends. The Workingmen's Committee had done magnificently. fheir'n wee. •ery hig teak to double their aubacription of £OOO n year, hut last year the amount raised was £1,200 and he had the ruisterince of the chairman that the committee were not going to rest till their contribution reached £l,OOO or £1,700. Having expressed apprecia- tion of the splendid work of the Million Penny Fund Committee, Mr. Hylands said he believed they had now nearly got to the end of the time when it would be necessary to make apecial appeals. Mr. Smethurst was, however, proposing to make one special and final effort in the near future to clear off the remain- ing debt of about £l7OO, and he had already received promises to the amount of £3OO. , Applause). Mr. J. H. SMETHURST, in seconding, pointed out that the children of Warrington suhscribed £ll9 193 9d. last year and the endowment of the cot which they had pledged tbergaelve, to maintain amounted to £4OO. With regard to the Million Penny Fund the funds of the institution had already benefited by £2,300 and the treasurer had nearly £2OO in hand. They hoped by one last effort to place the Inflrmary in finch a position as to give the people of NVarrington a rest from the constant appeals that had to be made. Ile had received orients°s of donations of £lOO each from Sir Gilbert Greanall and Mr. W. I'. Hyland*. Applause,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>-YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE -YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>In their 101 at annual report the Board of Manage. meet, after a reference to the celebration of the centeoary of the inei i twine in April, stated :—The average number of beds occupied (564 wee the name an that for 1909. and the average cost per bed amounted to .283 13e id. RS compared with £O3 Ils for the previous year. There was again • subetnntial increase in the number of in-patiente, outratiente, and accident, treated, se is shown in the following summary of the work td the Institution during the peer four years 1007. 1900. 1909. 1910 I logpital Inpatient, 517 753 1119 972 Horne Patient, 1,322 1,3311 1,285 993 Outpntients Acci,lents</p>
        <p>9,296 9,527 10,449 10,69(1</p>
        <p>Operationn 2;3 366 473</p>
        <p>Attention nury he directed to the number of in- patient. treated In the years 1907 and 1910 respectively, as giving some indication of the incalculable benefit afforded by the exteneion. The ordinary expenditure was £4,725 3. Ild. a eectesse upon that of the previous year of £llO 16e 7d. The extraordinary expenditure for painting and alteration%) wan 1:274 13. 9d, being an increase of £123 15a 9d. The ordinary income was £4.272 16e Id, an increese of £205 Ss lid upon that of the preview. year. The amount received to date from the Centenary Million Penny Fund Committee is £2.500. The year began with a debit balance due to the expenditure and income eccoont, of £2.x72 00 104. which. vrith the help of the shove contribution from the Centenary Million Penny Fund Committee,has been reduced to £10092. 541. Inaddition to this deficit there ion sum of VS. 12e I Id etill due to the Endowment Fund on account of the extension. An increase is shown in the annual sub- scyiptions ef £173 ISs 24. It is encouraging to note that the nontlal enbecription list alio,eu the hendeome increase of £520 :le 21, as compared with 1908, when the extension WWI opened. A ateady and increasing anbecrlptioe list in one of the most reliable source* of income. and the Boned earnestly appeal to tbe residents of the town and district for additional help In thin direction.Congresational reflect inn ham decreased by £22. :XI. from 1:273 IS. Hai in 1909. to £271 13. Id, in 1910. The Workingmen'. Infirmary Committee contri- ' lotted the sum of £1.221 101 3,1, an increase of .£l5O 17s 4d. The continued increase in the amount received from this committee is chiefly due to the larger number of sy.tematic collectione at the works. The Board hope that the special effort, which will he made during the awaiting year, will result in a still further improvement in the income from this source. With n view to curtailine expenditure the Board decided late in the year to close temporarily the male medionl ward as soon es possible after the Ist January, 1911. The Board sincerely trust that a brighter financial outlook will goon enable teem to re-consider this question. It is with deep regret that the Board record the death of Messrs. James Dodds, Henry Nield and William Owen, who were Life Governors of the Institution. The Warrington Infirmary end lhapeneary require. an income of at least £5,000 a peer, and, ea the Institution ham only a comparatively small endowment, the Board very earnestly appeal to the public of Warrington and neighbourhood for incyeesed financial support to enable thou to meet efficiently the unfortunately increasing requirements of the town and district. , THE PRESIDENT'S SPEECH. Stir Gomm, GREENAI.I. expressed his gratification St the increasing interest that W. being fahsu in the institution by the inhabitant. of Warrington and district. The neceesity and benefit of the extension were evidenced by the fact that 9 in- Wien ta were treated in 1910 as against 547 in 1907 —an inert*. •ince the new building wa• erected, of .5 per rent. Although the number of bade occupied in 11110-50- wee the sante es in the previous year, a larger number of _patiehts received attention with a shorter stay in the Infirmary. It was</p>
        <p>..... . encouraging to observe that the ordioary eipenditu.; for 1910 was only £452 ci excess of the income—a great improvement on previous year.. The annual subscription list, which had not yet reached it. limit, would bear very favourable comparison with that of ;similar institatione is other towns and be had every hope that in a comparatively short time their income would approach within reesocable distal.* of the expense. It would be nothing shcrt of a calamity if the great work which the institution carried on were or ippied tor the want of funds. They were looking forward with every confidence to the increasing help that would be given by the employers of the town sod Ii strict who bad done etch, great deal for the institu- ti on is the put.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart., wan re.elected prenident of the Infirmary nod the vice-president. were re-elected with the addition of Mr. F. A. Front. Other officers elected were : len. treasurer, Mr George Dakin; hon. auditor, Mr C Jackson Dolmen ; hon. consulting nurgerion.Drs E A Fox and G W Joseph : bon surgeons, Drn F F Flowden,EJ Fon,G 11.1onephand A Flodgkinnon: hoe amistant aurgeone, Dm G Ilion, C I) low nod C Robinson ; he denial nurgeon, Mr II It Taylor ; Board of Management re-elected, with Mr J 0 Withinnhaw in place of the late kev. C. Harvey Cook and Mr. Seminal Allen as a new representatiue of the Working- men's Committee.</p>
        <p>THANKS. THE EFFORTS OF THE WORKINGMEN.</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thinks were prawn] to the officials and to all the committees who bad assirted in raising f mute.</p>
        <p>(iirts.VNaht.,in moving the vote of thanks to the Workingmen's Committee, said that since its formation in 1974 that organization had raised the mon of £20,170. lie believed thst had been done with- out pressing too heavily upon anyone, and it only I CI owed what • aaaaa deal could tie accomplished when , body helped. liCotancillor 11. HOOOOT.I, in seconding, said the hand- some eubscription of the committee pointed to the confidence which the workingmen of Wirrington had in the adminiattation of that excellent institution. (Appian.%</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MAY RS TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>THE MAY RS TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in ressonding to a vote of thanks to the hon. surgeons, raid the work of the institution was done in the best possible manner and there were cases of which any hospital might be proud, as regarded their results. tie believed the most oifficolt case., requiring very special skill in medicine and surgery, could be dealt with at ibis Warrington Inlirms, as well as at the hospitals of our large citim.. Applause).</p>
