      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MORE LAND FOR THE PEOPLF,I</head>
        <p>MORE LAND FOR THE PEOPLF,I</p>
        <p>Lord Vernon has informed his tenants of his inten- tion to dispose of the greater portion of his Stiditury (Derbtshire, estate, amounting to 51`00 acres. lie gives the holders of farms the option of purchase on generous terms. The reason for breaking up the estate is not a question of personal finance. but the present instability of Isnded property. ' ' • " " "</p>
        <p>Sir Henry Hoare hen Instructed M . Knight Frank, and hutley to offer his Stourton Caundle and Purse Caundle estates ty auction in July neat. They consist of some 2.nt10 acres, situated in the centre of the Wedmore Vale Hunt, about three miles from Sherlwrne, and include the greatt•r pert of the •illages of Stourton Cautidle and Purse Caundle, as well as tho principle droned of the itlackmore Vale Polo Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SERMONS ON LORD'S-DAY OBSERVANCE.</head>
        <p>SERMONS ON LORD'S-DAY OBSERVANCE.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Lord'e-day Observance Society barn sent to the clergy and Nonconformist ministate throughout the country their ennnal suggestion that end, sermons should he preached on the nist Sunday niter Fester (April 23), or on some other convenient Sunday. They point to the opportunities for reforms. tiou presented at tbe present moment by (I The recent judicial decision which conflro • the London County Council's claim to close cinematograph shows on th• Lord's Day ; (21 The growing opposition tt the exten- sion of legalized Sunday trading which is proposed in Shops (No. 2) Bill and (3) The manifest readiness of all classes of the community for a considerable diminu- tion in the hours of permissible Sunday Labour and Sunday Trading is Penne Houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SENSATION AT LYMM. YOUTH'S ALLEGED OFFENCE AGAINST HIS SISTER Carbolic Add In the Tea.</head>
        <p>SENSATION AT LYMM. YOUTH'S ALLEGED OFFENCE AGAINST HIS SISTER Carbolic Add In the Tea.</p>
        <p>The usually placid township of Lynn» has been placed in a ferment ibis week by a charge against is local youth of attempting to poison hi• sister-a charge which wm eventually modi- Mir. as the matins of the clue an dincloned. The court- room of the police station wns melt 151,1 or. Wednesdny when the cape was inquired into. The accused %BR Fred Jennings, a youth of lb years, living with his parent. at Moston-ntreet, Newfield., 1,3 MU, and lie was ohs rged uitb feloniously causing to he taken by Edna Jennings !his sister) on March l7th a certain poison, to it, bout noid, with intention to kid tel murder thereby. Mr. A. Watkin occupied the chair and his Mother odigistrate man Mr. W. I'. Rylands. Mr. 1.0. Stud, orho represented prisoner, put in a Ors of tot guilty to the charge. The youth's rather, ' mother, brother and sister occupied seat. inside Mei aren of the court. SISTER'S EVIDFNCE. !Ina Jr nnings. eged shout 20, the sister of the prim-et. I.lllltPll that tin Friday morning last. atout seven &amp;elm k, she ansi ailed by her brother, who said it N. tine to get up. (icing downstairs shonly after- wards, she found the prisoner had gone to work. Some tea was in the pot, sod after pouring a quentity into.. cup she sipped some of the liquid. She noticed a tmell of something which the thought to be cartmlic acid. After swallochng a little she called to her mother, who came downsraim and gave bur auras malt nod renter in order to make her vomit. However, nho only heaved. She hit no pain and did not feel poorly. She did not krmw they had carbolic i.cid lathe house. Later in the morning she went to work ss tonal. Superintendent (Moll ) Did 3‹n hone your brother admit tlcit he had put this inlu the pot 3—Yen, in the prawn. of Sergeants Washington and Knott. Further questioned, she mid I hnt Sergeant Washiug• ton was asking her mother about it when the lad began to wry and said un did it. Mr. Steel ) There was nothing unusual in your brother calling you in the morning? Witness</p>
        <p>Your mother had, I understand, hers out all night and when she earns back at 5 o'clock she had a cup of tea out of the Name pot along with your father?</p>
        <p>There WIIS a strong smell of carbolic acid in the house ?—Yvs. .</p>
        <p>Knowing the smell of carbolic acid, cad that the arid was poison, you smelt at the tea in the cup, and then sipped Had you always been on friendly terms with your broiher Y—Yee.</p>
        <p>TU;t.e. tvel;—no reason üby he should attempt to murder you ?No..</p>
        <p>rrorn'your knowledge of yroir brother, do you think he.i.ntended to murder you ?—I don't think ko. . . . " ' " "' •• '</p>
        <p>Ile hes always been n good boy at home ?—Well, be has not done anything like that before. Mr. Harris (the Clerk) : Did you know where this carbolic was kept?—! did not know there was soy in the house. NOT SUFFICIENT POISON TO KILL.</p>
        <p>Dr. -.l.—dar.ier.liell-,-c—oti.n.ty-aSt.tl;et-fo; —Clreeliiie, said he had examined the contents of the bottle end teapot. The bottle minutiae,' a crude solittion of dieinfecting fluid, consulting of creosote not embolic acid. The amount of disinfectant in the Mild wee about eight grains The teapot conteined week tea menetiring about It, pints of liquid. It smelt of disinf want, and contained ten meinn of carbolio acid. The Clerk (Mr. fl. L. Harris) t Wee there euflicient left in the teapot to Id P ?—No It would not kill. I have taken sortie myself, simply to M. the effect it would have, and there wee not the slightest effect. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Ryland. (a megiatrate) What would be the effect in a cup of tee ?—I should not be afraid to take. cup ; I should only have a peculiar feeling in the mouth. The Clerk Would it ceuse' stomach ?—No</p>
        <p>T-h-e- Clerk Would it cause any irritation of the h?—No. I don't think so.</p>
        <p>. . . Mr. Hylands : How much do you think was put into the teapot?—Only few drops. Mr. Steel jt would have no ill effects at all ?—:Co: it would seem liken gargle. In answer to the clerk, Dr. Bell said ten grains would represent about two or three drape. Mrs. Maria Jennings (mother of the prisoner) who gave information to the police said she had been out nursing a sick person on Thursday night March 16. She returned about live o'clock on Friday morning. Her husband was then having his breakfast. The prisoner came downstairs, and had some of the tea which was then all right. Later her daughter called upstairs to her. On coming downstairs she noticed a smell of carbolic acid, and her daughter mid she had " sipped" some of the tea. Witness gave her some salt, and water. The bottle of carbolic acid was kept at the top of the cuptaard behind a box. The prisoner got it far her lust summer. She could not account for her eon doing this act. Mr. Ste : Your boy left school alien he was II year. of age? Yes. He was then in the 4th standard ? Yes.</p>
        <p>Did you from time to time have compb,nta that the schoolmaster could not make any impression npou him in the may of learning ?--Yee. In other word was your boy thill they could not knock any education into him. Even since be hal left school he has not been able to keep a situation ?—lle has been in several. Haven't you always found him a dull and harmless sort of boy I—Very hermits", He I lid everything I asked him. PRISONER'S ADMISSION AND COUNSEL'S 0 MI ECTION.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Washington said he in company with Sergeant Knott, went to the house of the prisoner, where he no Edna Jennings and her brother. tie inter- viewed both of them, and the prisoner denied very strongly that he had put poison in the ton, lie after- wards admitted having done Co. Mr. T. S. Steel objected to this statement, as the boy had not been cautioned. The Chairman.—The admission was made to hie sister.</p>
        <p>---Jll;..Steail.—lt was in responee to inquiries being made by the sergeant. There was ne direct evidence, he added, except the admiosion by the primmer, and he hoped the magistrates would take notice of his objection. The objection race accordingly recorded. The Clerk then called upon Syr-cont I.ott to give evidence hut Inspector 0.11 said it was unnecessary and the Clerk agreed. The officer's evidence on the first hearing on Saturday was to the effect that prisoner on the way to the punoe station gave as an explanation that he bad read in the papers a few weeks ago about a man putting poison in coffee up in Scotland. In his speech for the defence, Mr. Steel submitted that on the evidence of Dr. Carter Bell the ones against the prisoner must fail. They had it stated in the evidence that the liquid was harmless. The fact that the poison would not kill the girl absolved the boy from criminal liability. Mr. W. P. Itylands But the court can bring in • verdict of an inferior offence. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said it would be quite proper to bring in a lesser charge, as assault or misdemeanour, but he would challenge such a barge. He was of the opinion that a jury, having regard to all the circumstances, would not convict the boy, and he contended they were entitled to dismiss him.</p>
        <p>After retiring for about 15 minute., The magietrates decided there was a prinitt feria to go for trial for miscienieanour, and the boy was accord- ingly committed to the Cheater Assize.. After being committed the accused said, I don't know what made me do it. I had no intention to iojure or annoy anyone." . . - .</p>
        <p>An a-p'plicn'tion'for bail by Mr. Steel was granted in two puretien of £lO.</p>
        <p>Legal aid was granted prisoner under the Pr augers' Defence Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Master of the Mill."</p>
        <p>.4 meloilriume piece of exceptional merit is " The Master of the Mill" which ix appearing at the Royal Court Theatre this week. Mr. Henry St. John Cooper the author, has provided some telling lines affording excellent opportunities for powerful acting, and the accomplished caste take full advantage of them. A welcome relief, however, from the "heavy " part of the p'uy is found in some really aniusing comedy. As I the wealthy obi man John Greeniel,l. the owner of the mill, Mr. Algernon Syms, in addition too sur- prisingly perfect make-up is remarkably true to life in his representation of the old gentleman. Ile aurpa.sea himself in the scene, where, whilst the old 'nen lies trying on the couch in his room, his nephew , whom he has wronged in years peat, comes to him in the nick of time and forgives him. Mr. Herbert Young makes an admirable portrayal of the hero of the piece, Tom Bolt-n, and wins the heart of the audience by his frankness and open-hsartedness. The part of the polished and plausible 'alien, Julian Croft, is taken by Mr. Ernest E. 'meson, who inns 'Minnows as be cold reasonably be expected to be. At any rate he commits one murder. the RP-meg]e preceding the dastardly act being followed with breathless interest by some motions of the house. Mi 99 Enid Wilde, as the mill girl, who afterwards marries Tom, the hero, gives a pleasing portrayal. The other parts are : Isabel Ware, Miss Valerie Russell ; Kate, Miss Polly DblibilSoo Dykes and Collins, two mill hands, Messrs. Carlisle and Stevens ; a broken-alown adventurer, Mr. A. Byrne ; and the bibulous Braithwaite, Mr. Ewart Drake.</p>
        <p>KLINS,' an Inorgsnic Cement, Bind*, Pilling, LC., withstands water. 6d. post !res.—Advt.</p>
