      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>It is scarcely an exaggeration to say that the first spring Hower i a not store eagerly watched for or more heartily welcomed by women than the first spring bat. We are feeling somewhat distremed by dull foggy dare, or pinched by the cold ; the for turbans, long- haired 'teasers, and felts have ceased to have any charm for us. slbeit comfort still finde them necessary, when suddenly inn linrtit of pale sunshine we catch sight of brightly tinted straws and realistic dower.. Spring is here, and though the chill winde and cold showers may be vet in front of on, we are undaunted. A etr.i.w hat merits a day in fashion's calendar just as surely as doe, a cellandine in that of the nature lover.</p>
        <p>IlEr.obllNC STYLES Fort All..</p>
        <p>lie first question a woman asks herself, on the ' appearance of the new spring hats, is " Are they going to suit me ?" and mindful of sundry none too happy experiences she waits the answer with some trepidation. This year no one. he she tall or short. fair or dark, serious or piquant of expression, need have any fear about finding a becoming hat. For those who are heft set-off by the wide brim can still near it ; they may even allow them- selves to choose the slightly drooping model such an we have delighted in for a good many months, they tnay, if preferred, pronounce in favour of an upward toll in front, or to the left or finally they may turn the brim up sharply against the crown and catch it in place with a tall aigrette that springs from a mount of soft feathers. The woman who Its patiently submitted for some time to the mushroom and cloche styles. conscious all the while that they did not show her to advantage, may take heart of grace. for this spring sees a return to favour of the raised hrim.</p>
        <p>Some of the very newest toques, bell shaped and swathed entirely in folds of satin show a turned back brim all across the front. A charming model of this kind is made of grey straw, the fairly wide brim coming flown closely over the hair at the back, but turned off the fats. The high crown is covered with is very wide soft satin ribbon in grey, shot ; with orange, and gathered up into a huge flower-like rosette on the left side toward the front. Other really smart twines there are made entirely in straw, but no pliable as to resemble a satin that can be draped and twisted at will without displaying a single angle. That liking for Paisley effects which has been so pronounced ol late may be traced in ninny of these, for the strands of straw are of several different colours, reddish brown, dark green, mauve, white, maize. pink, and dark bine, all soft tints harmonising with each other. and mingled no deftly as to give the effect of Paisley minted silk or oriental embroideries. On such a toque the most effective trimming is a full bow of black velvet, some of the loops standing up high in aigrette style at the side.</p>
