        <p>He stated in reply to a ques- sion will be made during the ' for the payment of members payment of election expenses I a storm of supplementary What sum would members *. ASHLEY. Will the proposal form of a Bill? queried Mr. (BERLAIN. Will the proposal lie meaning of a Bill as defined lent Bill? inquired Lord HUGH RIME MINISTER added nothing information he had already ;lined to say even whether the lemplated will be by means of pen to conjecture, therefore, sion contemplated will simply moposals of the Budget.</p>
        <p>vie measure of pathos in the :rested by Mr. GEORGE LANS- nember compels us to think of were an otherwise inoffensive was being infuriated by pin- is reason to think that he accustomed to the House of cl that he will in due course humours in some more effective way than he does at present ot another VICTOR GRAYSON, tormentors may have been led t he has been accustomed to whose members have not sing as an incentive to dis- iour, and where he could be nest without appearing to be e will become a really useful he has beer. tamed, and he ame himself when he discovers at a member must suppress hie wee to a considerable extent lecorne at home in the House</p>
        <p>DOND (Aniseed, &amp;mem ssiels--, To, and Colds. Isiluents etc.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Public and private life I'HE REV. C. in Warrington are both . . . .</p>
        <p>navgr COOL poorer by the untimely death of the Rey. C. Harvey Cook at the age of:50. During his all too brief pastorate of the Unitarian Church here Mr. Cook had taken a . prominent part in all the local activities which have spelt progress with a capital P, and aimed at, making life an infinitely brighter and better thing. Physical infirmity only appeared to spur Mr. Cook to greater endeavours in the hope, no doubt, of achiev- ing as much good as one man may in the brief span allotted him here below. The rev. gentleman was altrustic in thought and ' deed, and it will be some time before the townspeople of Warrington can arrive at a proper appreciation of the senerous,unselfish spirit which underlay all the actions of the ' man whose place will be difficult to fill, not only at Cairo-street, but in the councils of many organisations which aim in different ways at the greatest good for the greatest number.</p>
        <p>In Tuesday's debate on A REPUDtATED the Civil Service esti- CIRCULAR. mates, Mr. Hoare, the young Tory member for Chelsea, drew attention to a circular issued to Inspectors of Elementary Schools by the former Chief Inspector. This circular urged the Inspectors to use their influence to ensure the appointment as inspectors under, the local education authorities of University, graduates and Public school men to the ' exclusion ot elementary school teachers. , With such views, it is needless to say, Mr. ' Runciman has no sympathy whatever. The circular, he emphatically declared, did not represent the policy of the Board of Educa- tion, and never would so long as he was responsible for it. As Mr. Charles Trevelyan, Under-Secretary to the Board of Education, pointed out, Mr. Runcimaii has already appointed seven inspectors who have been masters or assistant masters of elementary schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bonar Law hasl • Ma. BONAR LAW been given the oppor- AT BOOTLE. tunity of contesting what the Tory head- ' quarters regard as a perfectly safe seat owing to the retirement of Colonel Sandys. Colonel Sandys will live in history as the victim of a glaring" fiscal " hoax. A con- stittent wrote to ask whether he would introduce a short Bill to prohibit the importa- tion of Gerrnan machinery for smoothing out the wrinkles in tripe, tripe being a staple' article of the people's food. Poor Colonel Sandys fell into the trap, and replied to the effect that, though there was no chance of such a Bill being carried while the present Government was in power, it would be all right when we got a Tariff Reform Govern- ment. Mr. Sonar Law does not include "wrinkles in tripe" in his election pro- gramme, but he has made a brave show of the Tariff Reform label that he had to swallow when he went to Manchester at the General Election.</p>
        <p>An unpleasant feature THE ARMY of the Army debates DEBATE AND THE has been the inclination VOLUNTARY shown by the Oppoßi- SYSTEM. ton, or perhaps it would</p>
        <p>. .- belfairer to say certain sections of the Opposition, to try to make capital out of unwarrantable attacks upon a distinguished soldier, because he has written a. book in favour of that voluntary system which is the basis of the whole fabric of our national defences. The responsible leaders ol the Opposition know better than to pledge the Tory Party to the principle of Conscrip- tion. But' prancing pro-consuls" like Lord Milner,and famous soldiers like Lord Roberts, have been going up and down the country denouncing the voluntary system. One result of such manceuvres, whether inten- tional or not, is to " crab " the Territorial movement. Mr. Haldane very rightly and properly asked Sir lan Hamilton to embody his views on national defence in a memorandum, which has been published in order to clear up the misconception about the problems of national defence that the Con- scriptionist school have sedulously fostered, and to put an end to this "crabbing" process.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ED'-i °,-:ITINS.</head>
        <p>ED'-i °,-:ITINS.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the local branch of the Tariff Reform League the other evening was distingUishecrby an attendance of about twenty people, and the resignation of a couple of officers.</p>
        <p>A youngster's definition of Christianity as related by Mr. A. Porritt at a lecture in Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Thursday : " Telling the truth, not ` mucking ' about on Sundays, and playing games fair."</p>
        <p>Mr. John Redmond,in a speech at Glasgow on Sunday, said the Irish party supported the limitation of the Lords' Veto, not only because they desired to get Home Rule, but also because they wanted justice for the mass of English, Scotch and Welsh people.</p>
        <p>The Borough Bill has 'passed the Rouse of Lords Committee and it is expected that the Royal assent will be given next week. Steps will then immediately be taken for the building of the new bridge—tu feet the engineer (Mr. Webster) will in all probability be in Warrington for the purpose of laying out the ground in the course of a few days. Mr. Little's story of the Ayrshire man who, on his return from his first order- hunting visit to London, when asked by his father what he thought of the Englishman. replied, " I canna tell ye : I've been seem' the heads o' departments," is a good example of that" dry," suggestive humour which is characteristic of the Scot.</p>
        <p>Next week we shall publish the first of a series of three special articles on the Peace Question by Professor Graham of Man- chester. The articles will be entitled " Trade, Empire and War," and the pro- nouncement of this distinguished Peace advocate should be specially interesting and suggestive in view of the rapprochement between this country and the United States.</p>
        <p>A feature of the peace movement is the way in which the Free Churches have welcomed the Taft-Grey proposals. The Rev. F. B. Meyer, secretary of the National Council, is now in communication with the leaders of the churches and of the peace movement in America to ascertain how far they will respond to the popular feeling that has sprung into life amongst the churches on this side. It is suggested that if the churches in America are prepared to support the proposals as readily as their British brethren, arrangements should be Eltirrefor great demonstrations to be held simultaneously in America and in England.l The Albert Hall has been provisionally booked for Ics*dlet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>• Co&amp;gramme Advt Cosset ter Olios, Criss., 14. Itt, alai kpoef Tres.—</head>
        <p>• Co&amp;gramme Advt Cosset ter Olios, Criss., 14. Itt, alai kpoef Tres.—</p>
        <p>M. feignlies) ldeinbee for •Ythuger illard,bas follow ad the examle of the Member for Witiringtith and indulged in a little brush with the Speaker. On Wednesday he asked a terribly serious question about the weight'of the men who were to form part of the new Air Battalion, and was gently but ' firmly ruled out of order. Thus, another promising maiden effort was nipped in the bud! • • In some quarters it •is anticipated that large as ig the increase of the naval estimates, Mr. Lloyd George, when he introduces his third Budget, will be in a position to announce some reduction of taxation. The rurnohr battedjovonstderable 2pecßittritn One section say that the tax on tea will be lowered, anothex. the tax oil tobaccoes thind the income tax. Others again are of opinion' that the declining trade carried on in licensed houses will feel the benefit of a remission of some of Its heavy impoits. Meanwhile Mr. Lloyd George is lying low and saying nothing.</p>
        <p>Last Friday Mr. Winstol Churchill got the second reading, unopposed, for a Bill which embraces a large number of provisions for I the greater safety of workers in mines. The past year has produced an unusually heavy list of casualties, and Mr. Winston Churchill's measure commanded universal sympathy. His administration of the Home Office recalls Mr. Asquith's great achievements in that Department, which first demonstrated that what the man in the street had come to regard as a rather humdrum post, chiefly associated with the revision of death-sen- tences on criminals, could be made a powerful lever of social amelioration.</p>
        <p>The Land Union is a comparatively young organisation in Warrington, but it already appears to have fallen on evil days, if the very sparse attendance at Monday's meeting is to be taken as an index of local interest in its doings. The thrifty workingman and the small owner are strangely ungrateful for the tender regard for their interests displayed by landlords who do not relish he new taxes,and they persistently decline to be drawn into an agitation against a Govern- ment which has been courageous enough to insist that land shall bear its fair share of the national burdens.</p>
        <p>It is very diverting (says the " West- minster Gazette") to see the efforts of the Unionist Press to prove that the Parliament Bill must not be considered at all about Coronation time. In one Unionist journal we read : Can the Lord, be expected to deal with the measure in a white hest, during Coronation month ? The accepted belief in that they cannot, and that the constitutional settlement will have to await a later period of the year. . . . . .</p>
        <p>It is pleasing tt; know wSat the weather this June is to be. We shall be able to make our preparations accordingly. But there is such a thing as blue funk as well as white heat.</p>
        <p>Bootle is much in the public eye just now. Mr. Gladstone once referred to the Kirkdale and Bootle district as he knew it: " I have seen" said Mr. Gladstone," wild roses grow- ing upon the very ground that is now the centre of the borough of Bootle. All that land is now partly covered with residences and partly with places of business and industry; but in my time but one single house stood upon the space between Rimrose Brook and the town of Liverpool." So vast has been the growth of the Bootle population that to-day the numbers would almost justify a claim to become a two-member constituency. • •</p>
        <p>Within the confuies of Warrington and before his own constituents Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., is quite a courteous gentleman, but when on his native sod the native vulgarity seems to crop out inevitably. Mr. Smith has been pursuing Mr. Max Muspratt in Bootle with that rancour which marked the family attentions to his candidature at the general election. On Thursday the Member for Warrington told the electors of Aintree in his pleasant way that "No con- stituency, municipal or parliamentary, had ever elected Mr. Max Muspratt twice," that he was "a puny gladiator,' that" his election address showed a rudimentary knowledge neither of politics nor of grammar," and that the contest was one of bread and blather," in which of course, Mr. Muspratt was responsible for the "blather." If the electors of Bootle have any regard for the amenities of political life, Mr. Muspratt should have a good chance on Monday. - .</p>
        <p>One of the most'ing;nious questions yet submitted to the Parliamentary Debating Society was that drafted by the irrepressible member for Burton-on-Trent (Mr. J. Jarman) with reference to the proposed" All-British" IShopping Week. He asked the Conservative Government to inform the House (V) whether the term "All British Shopping" means that no foreigner is to be served; (2) whether English cloth manufactured from Argentine wool and dyed with German dyes, standard ' bread made from a mixturill of foreign and English wheat, and Dutch yeast by machinery run by foreign motors, boots made in England by American machinery, from foreign hides tanned in Warrington,l are included in the term "All British ;" (3) whether traders are justified in serving customers during the All British week by the ,aid of German made electric and incan- des&amp;nt lamps. The only answer which the Prime Minister vouchsafed to this poser was that a member who had been to such mental pains to compose the question ought to have brains of sufficient calibre to understand the meaning of the term "All British." The Government, he added, could not compel anybody to sell any goods.</p>
        <p>In a very entertaining lecture on "Carlisle the Border City," our Deputy rown Clerk, Mr. A T. Hallawax, brought to light a few "standing orders of mediawal municipal life which should open the eyes of present- day Councillors. Two or three hundred years years ago, the Mayor of Carlisle used to preside over the local Council meetings with the door-key at his side, and full power to either" punish extremely" an obstinate councillor or put him out. With a majority behind Lim the Mayor could put the minority in prison if they became too contumacious. Similarly strict orders governed the conduct of the townspeople. For instance "no man's sons," were allowed to walk the streets after 10 p.m., and the nocturnal excursions of daughters were sever even thought of. We agree with Mr. Hallaway that a return to the rules and practices of the middle ages would be no had thing in some respects. Sankey-street and Bridge-street could, at any rate, be kept clear from crowds of juvenile roysterers after dusk—a problem which has proved too much for present-day civic authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Weeetei Circle.</head>
        <p>Weeetei Circle.</p>
        <p>A small but %MIT suceeartful "Spring Cleaning Sale" was held in the *omen's Circle Club Roues, Bridge- atrset, on Monday last. The four stalls were an follows : Stall No. 1, Rummage." Stall Na. 2, soap, brushes. and other spring cleaning requieites. Stall No. 3, new goods. Stall No. 4, tea and tefreshmenta. A substantial stun for the Circle Fund woe realized by the ' sale.'</p>
