        <p>Patel, and the Chreeatlee.</p>
        <p>'At in:meting of tbe Penketh Pariah Conucil last nyebing it WI. decided to hold parish meeting on Wednesday, April roh, to discuss the be. means of locally celebrating the Coronation. Scalp-street School. —-•— •</p>
        <p>The Sitee and Buildings Committee of the Educa- tion Committee have accepted the tender of Weer.. Jofteph Dolan and 8 o, amounting to £516 lh. 2d. for the ereciion of douche bathe at the Evelyn-street Council echool. Coronation Holidays. . - • • • •</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee baa resolved that the variou, schools maintained hy the Committee be closed for the " Walking Day' arid Coronation celebration holidays from noon on Wednesday, the 21st, to the morning of Monday, the Nal June, Inn. Warrington Corporation Bill. . .</p>
        <p>The frrnillions Bill of the Warrington Corporation, which, was considered by the Incal Legialstmn Com- mittee of the House of Commons on Tuesday came before the Chairman of Committees in the House of Lords as an unopposed measure, and we* ordered to be reported for third reading.—The Bill among other provision authoriaea the construction of a pew bridge scenes the Mersey and regularises certain financial proceedings of the Corporation Royal Court Theatre. An attraction of exceptional merit will be presented at the Royal Court Theatre next week when Mr J C Lodge Percy, Miss Henrietta Schrier and company will nppe.ir in the following repertoire Monday and Wednesday, " Moths" ; Tuesday, "Mr. Barnes ef Hew York"; Thnred ly, "The &amp;wind Miss Th*pteray" ; Friday, "Camille"; Saturilsy, Snell we forgive her 2"—the following week "Miss Hook or Holland " will pay a return visit. Adult Schools Social .fr• 'On Monday about 120 members of the combined Adult Schools had an enjoyable social in Aciplemy- , street Schools. The chair was taken by Mies Paul (President of the %warty Women'a School) and sincere tbahka were accorded to the following ladies and vomtlemon for their kind and willing help —The I Mies.* Wilson, Wood. Hankey, Taylor and Hamer, and Messrs. Hannan, Wood, Leyland, Noden, Howl and Webb. Light refreshments were nerved in the interval and chocolates and cigsreites distributed to the artistes. The Misses Wood, Willson and flankey weee able accompanists. School Accommodation Difficulty. . • • •• • - •</p>
        <p>The Board of Educatcm having coiled the attention of the Education Committee to the overcrowded con- dition of Ramilton-street and St. Benedicia schools, the School Attendance Committee has reaolved that the attention of the Sites and Buildings Committee be called to the presaure which hi being brcsight to beer e 4 the local authority by the Board of Education in reOrd to the school accommodation of the district in which these schools are situated, sod that they be requested to facilitate an much as possible, the provi- sion of the additional accommodation PO urgently needed to meet the requirements of the locality. Ste' ,Patrick's. Day Celebrations. •</p>
        <p>Ireland'. Nationr(festival was again the occasion ofutech rejoicing,and a pleasant gathering took place among the many eons and daughters of Erin resident in Warringt, n. In addition to tonne excellent concerto, Ac., the Warrington Branch of the United Irish Laggflama Great Brikain provided the venal dance,and this took place on Friday night in the Co-operative Hal', Sankey-al reet. Over 200 persons were present and an excellent programme, including several National danCes, was provided. The hall had been very tastefully decorated and Mr. Blake's band dim- noosed sweet music, The MC.'. were Messrs. P. Regan, P. Archer, and J. T. New.. Great credit is due to all concerned. and especially the Lsdies' Corn- mauve and the hon. secretary, Mr. Joseph McHale. Tlim proceedings were excellently carried out and the dame woo !Pry nocceesful in _every way.</p>
        <p>Lege! Order of Ancleat Shepherds, 10. The quarterly meeting of members connected with the Late Spring Rose Lodga, No. 828, was held at the him&amp; of Bro. James Gleave, Barley Mow H del, on Saturday. A very creditable report of the past quarter working was given by the Secretary, W E. Clegg. The amount paid as rick benefit wee £lO2, the sum of £l9 i.. was carried forward, making the total worth of the Lodge £2,481 10s.After the election of Lodge and District officers, P.P.C.S. Frederick S. Mamey presented P.ll. William H Beswick with a flatbed emblem of merit for services rendered whilst going through the different offices in the Lodge. EPA'S. William Taylor, P.P.C.S T. C. Duckworth and P P.C.S Jame. Parr, added testimony to the good work and kindly feeling shown by Bro.Beevrick during hieterm of office. P.M. Beawick in replying thanked I the officers and members for their assistance it having been &amp;pleasure to him and he strongly advised 'others to go through office of the Order. VACUUM CLEANERS an hire, ss. per day; MACRO LAME CUTTERS, Is. per S. GARLAND, 51.55 Bridge to., Warringtoo.—Advt. Warrington Garden Suburbs, Limited. . .</p>
        <p>We are glad to see that the Warrington Garden Suburbs 'scheme continues to make satisfactory progress: A second Tenants Society has recently been registered under the Industrial and Provident Sgrjety's Acts, to assist in the development of the Morris Brook estate at Grappenhall. The Society Jigs already secured a site for twelve houses, and an interesting ceremony will take place at half past three this afternoon, when bricks are to be ' laid by Mr. C. E. Parker, the Chairman of " Warring- ton Garden Suburb., Liniiied," Alderman Bennett, the secretary, and Mr. J. A. Ransome, the Managing Director of The Richmond Gas Strive and Meter Company, Limited, the rein/oyes of which are largely interested in the new society. Other developments are contemplated in the near future, and the part which Warrington iv taking in the solution of the Housing Problem is being attentively followed all over the country. We understand that the plan., of the Garden Suburbs at Great Sankey and G rappen- hall are now being exhibited at an important Housing and Town Planning Exhibition in Edinburgh. and that they will be sent on to other large and influential centres in due course. The fullest information SA to the local ,cheme will he gladly given to all inquirers by Alderman Bennett, Mr. G. 11. Smith, the Secretory of " Warrington Tenants, Limited," or by Mr. W. McLeod Johnstone, the Secretary of " Grappenhall Tenants. J imited."</p>
        <p>iiirri--iiiiii-kettottraphic Society. " The Land of the Royal Fern."—This was the title of • lecture illustrated by lantern slides by Mr. C. Milcockson, the hon. treasurer of the Society, on Tugs- dey evening. Mr. Brown (the president i occupied the chair, and a good audience wan present. Tbe Lecturer cycled with his companion last SUMMOi for 500 miles from Cork through the counties Cork and Kerry, the most conseqed districts of Ireland. Be said that the results of such a tour must snake anyciee a Home Ruler, whatever politica he owned before, for Ireland ha. been shamefully ill-treated, especially in local govern- ment. It has been handed over in chains to the brewer and the publican, and is now a country ',matted with drink. In Princes-street, Cork, 11 shops Out of 1-1 are publics. In Kenmore 35 out of about 75. in Drimoleagne 11 out of 24, with a popolatioo of : lees than 2011 Sueem a while ago had a licence for every shop. "Surely,' stid the lecturer, "Irishmen could not govern their country worse than that. The condition of house property is deplorable." Many slides were pat through showing hovels that Englishmen could not live an, except in the worst London slums, but pretty cottara cheaper than these dene are being rapidly put up in their place be the Congested Districts Boards. Killarney was found too uninterenting and too iniiri- orm err ruract thew two visitor., but in she country districts were found beauty apots unrivalled in these inland's. The vegetation is wonderful. Bamboos, palms, arbutus, erealooia. fucheiae, all grow in pro- fusion, whilst London pride, white wswrlilies, end the Royal and other ferns are merely weeds.</p>
