      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Next Week at the Mast.</head>
        <p>Next Week at the Mast.</p>
        <p>There is amther excellent programme at the Palace. next week. The top "turn" will be the American Illusionint and Merteue-eu-srenc,Sears,who will pre-ent what is termed • mystical pantomimic Indian phantasy, entitled " The Blue Pearl' Other attractions will be J. P. Ling, comedian and mimic ; Ida Kessanly, and her troupe of roynl fox tetrien ; Oewald and Locke, burle•que artistes ; and Joe Humphreys, vocal comedian.</p>
        <p>1:1•RDILN ROLLERS, lIROCIAI 11011111 a 27s GARLAND, ironmonger, Bridge it, Warringtoo.—Adyt Sledding at kid-street. A quiet but pretty wedding was solemnized at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Monday, the con- tracting part,ea being Ifr,.Robert Shaw, of Gladstone- , street, second son of the late Mr. Robert Shaw, of Warrington. and than May Talbot, only dauahter of Mr. Joseph Talbot, of Orford lane. The bride, who wine prettily attired in a dregs of cream bilk taffetta and carried a bouquet of white lilies and tulip., was given away by her father. Her bridearoaid, Miss Florrie Aepey, wore a dress of navy blue silk eolienne and carried • bouquet of Missend tulips, Mr. Arthur Cleave carried out the dutiia of best man. The , reception wan held at the home of the bride's parent.. , CUTLERY, Joupb Rodgers It Sons' celebrated knives and forks, me.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, lromnonger, Bridge at., Warrington. —Met. ' Death of a Veteran Friendly Society Worker. Warrington has lost one of it. oldest friendly I society workers by the death which took place on the 17th int. at his halm, 25, Mill-lane, of illr. Thomas Fogg. The deceased, who was 79 years of age. had been • member of the Constitution Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddlellows (Manch“ter Unity) for nearly rift years and a great number of years was one of the trustees. He was •Iso a very old member ,of the Morning Star Lodge of the ;rand Coked Order ,of Shepherds, and at the time of his death wu a trustee of that society. The funeral took place at the Cerneb .7 on Sunday, the service being conducted by the Rev. J. H. Henkel', one of the assistant clergy at St.Patil's Church The mourners included the following friendly aocietv representative...—P.P.G.M. Thomas S. Steel, PO. Thomas Wadsworth, district treasurer; PO. Henry Taylor fassistint secretary of the Con- stitution Lodge), P.ll. William Watson (treasurer), P.G. Et-I/stn.! Steel, 1' li. Ellirion and P.G. WllllOmn Houghton: _ _ .</p>
        <p>Academy-street Adult School. Mr. J. H. J. Hampeon took the members of the above school on an imaginary trip to the West Indies, last Sunday morning. Except for the absence of the journey, one might have considered himself there, no lucid and graphic did the speaker paint the picture of the natives, of the fame, of the vegetation, of the ineect life, and of the flab that abound in the aurround. big water. Owing to the oistorns which prevail is the t ropics, white people do not as rule like to undertake manual work. Scarcely any meat is eaten —as these islands are in the Torrid Zone—the inhabit into subeisting on fish and fruits, both of which are extremely plentiful. There is no winter, no that the trees are ever green. Bathing in the sea every morning all the year round is the custom. notwithstanding the risks of sharks and other fish. Unfortunately, however, the sugar plantations (where greater opportunities in management were afforded whites' are on the decline, owing to beet superseding the sugar cane; conetsinently there is less demand for the services of e Lite pe ,ple to these salubriona and fertile islands. LAWN MoWEIIS ground by special mschinerl: 'nod your machines now before the rush.-4. S. OARLAVAN Ironmonger, Bridge at,. Warrington.—Advt. Pummel of Mr. Jobe Berry</p>
