        <p>On Thum:ray evening in the Awes— biy—Roo; of the work. of M 05.19. Crosneld and Sone, Ltd., the Bey. and Hon. K. Yarde Bullernf the Navy House,Chatharn,pve an interesting lecture, profusely illustrated by lantern elides specially taken by himaelf, on Life in the Navy." The hall wee well filled by ?demos. Croefield's employees. Dr. Markel presided and amount throes preeent I•Pre Mr. J. J. Croeflokl, Csiitain George K. CrosOeld, Miss Markel, Mrs. Broadbent, Mr. an.l Mr.. Holman Mogdoo and Mr. at. B. Layton. The lecture was the loot appearance of the Chairman in publics before his depvrture for the 000th England. . . .</p>
        <p>In introducing the lecturer Or. %trite' remarked that the Rev. It. J. Terri Haller wee not. stranger to War- rington, as he was the nephew of Lord Winmarleigb, who used to live in the present Warrington Town Hall. Tbe LE. TI:ItER cOrOnlencel his lecture by I picture of the on., saying that in the old rye the... we.. greet divider of nation. but now It bel become the great link between the countries of the world. and he sincerely hoped that the time would arrive whir universal disarmament would be poesible. He would welcome any properls far arbitration. But that titre had Dot COMO mod we needed a strong eery both to protect our commerce end to defend our imperial right.. (Applause.) Many other slide, of a early interesting character were shown, illustratine v•riou. of rips frJm the gigantic Dreadnaught dyers to the deetroyer, and slides depicting It. blonjackets' life at sea. The dangers of naval life at sen were illustrated by sll4cs showing armor's's, torpedoes, aunken wrecks and the feudist of collisions. Rut there nue also, rjej the lecture, danger, to the blanjacketa oa roes aa well aeon sea It was well said that Jack was never so moth at see se when he was on red. Laughter.) It Ins here that the citiur of the country were orate ,willing to help the bluejackets by providing thus with !plane' of recreation, and be appealed to the retiree to eaglet in the work of the Naval /louse at Mather, which last year provided for 27,000 men. The /enter wu clueely followed sad was punctuated with &amp;opium. Mr J CRO9IIFLI., in 1130,0( • vol. of thulis to the lecturer, remarked bow instructive and amusine the lecture had been, especially from the tern, point of view, for after coaling a great deal of material would be enquired to welsh things clean. (Laughter., Mr }lom.rtoo K INGDoN seconded, speaking of the blue jackets an both our guardians and uur heroes.</p>
