      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>PEASANT PROPRIETARY BILL</p>
        <p>PASSED.</p>
        <p>Rev. E. C. E. Carleton Twits the</p>
        <p>Socialists.</p>
        <p>After a fairly interesting debate the Pensant Pro- prietary Bill introduced by tbe inister for Agriculture Mr. C. PlinstoM was passed without a division. though not unanimously, at the meeting of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening. The diwusaion was re-opened by Mr. W. MACKEY (C., Fermanagh, N.) who void small ownership wan one of the finest plunks in the Conserva- tive programme. The system had been tried in Ireland , and in five years 250,000 tenants purchued or began to • acquire holding.. The poorest farm labourer in Ireland had an equal opportunity with the farmer of larger means to purchase his farm from the landlord to whom lie bad been pay ing rent. The policy of the Radical party appeared to he the creation of perpetual tenancy, he State to be the landlord. That, however, did not always operate to the advantage of the tenant, as wu evidenced by the fact Putt when the Liberal Government offered to take over one of the Duke of Bedford's estates they made it a condition that the rents should first be rebut 50 per cent. Small holdings had not proved a great bleasing on the Delaware Estate,. for a large occupying former whose holding hod been , broken up did not receive any compensation when he . had to take his notice from the Crown.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. ENGLAND (1., IVarrington) said the measure was one of the most practical which bed ever been pl.ed before the House. The cry " Back to the land " me in everybody's mouth arra it was therefore surprising that the Bill should he opposed from any .drarter of the Douse. The Liberal party tawnys pro.. leered to be keen on the land question, but now they were devoting their time to quibbling about questions of tenancy Fold owneohip. It reminded him of the story of the fox end the grape. If the Bill had come from the Liberal side of the House every member of that party would have welcomed it warmly and been almost ready to embrace the introducer. (Laughter.) "Their opposition was nothing more or leas than case of aro. grapes. The better the Bill that came from the Conservatives, the more they objected to it. !Laughter.) They were all prepared to acknowledge that agriculture in this couotry wan in a most unsatisfactory condition. Fifty years ago 2,000,000 people were engaged in that indtmtry end if it had been fostered end mimed instead of being hampered, 5,000,000 should have been employed in it to-day, inntaad of which—according to the " Daily New. " which appeared to be the Nero Testament of the Liberal parry—there were less than ' 1,000,000. The Bill vrould flonoce nnybody vrho wanted to acquire a reasonable quantity of land and it allowed for repayment on very sensible Bees. The benefits which would accrue from people working on the land would not be limited to mere monetary grin, for the pystem would help to build up a healthier racer of people, stronger in mind and body. The best brains, they were told, were possessed not by town dwellers but hymen whose forbears had for generations dwelt upon the land. The Ite. E. C. E. CARLEWN (President of the linard of Education), said the opposition to the Bill last week was largely on Socialistic lines and he intended to view the matter in that light. It was now perfectly clear that the Liberal party did not believe no much in a peasant proprietorship as people thoright. Everyone knew, of course, that they did not believe in landlord proprietorship, bet DOW they did not believe in the proprietorship of the workingman. It had been stated by opponents of the measure that the land belonged to the nation—he supposed to everyone in the nation, equally no much to those who had paid for land they pomessed and to those who hail pot. There Were ponibly members on the other side who bad bought land and built houses on it, or perhaps their fathers bad done no in the past. They must have been rather precious fools—(laughter)—becausv if they had only waited a little while they might have got it for nothing on Socialistic principle.. (Laughter). lie feared some of them were deeply committed to the buying and selling of land, and Meowing as they did that the lend belonged to mornebody else—to the nation—it was nor- pricing that they should give themeelves away in that fashion, when there was e Bridowell not far from the spot on which they stood. (Laughter). Some, he supposed, were in actual possession of that kind of property, just like ordinary individualists, while preaching collectivism. They had as keen i ns eye to a bargain in land as an)hoily else, and were quite willing to lend money on it arid to MOOD]. mend others to ori so. Vet they professed to regard ' with holy horror the ayatem in which they were no deeply implicated, which they were encouraging and for obich they were just as responsible re the land- , lords. I" Question.") If they really believed what they professed, there was hardly one of them who could , not be convicted of something like fraudulent intention —having a hand in transactions which they did nut ' hesitate to declare were a Hr.od upon the people. "Your Socialist" continued the rev. gentleman ...moos to be a man who does not practise what he preaches sod if I lived alongside a Socialist and were led to thiek from the speeches, he made in favour of collectivism that I might take down his boundary wall and Mare his hack garden, I am afraid I be would proceed against me exactly like an ordinary individualist. (Laughter.) Contiottiog the speeker said land did Dot lend itself very *roily to be monopolised. Oh.") They could not boors combination of owners to keep up prices; they could not have • corner in land. (Laughter.) They could not export or iniport it. Land was one of those commodities upon which the people could rely for protecting themselves from foreign competition. The Socialists had forml that oat and, as the foreigner could not tan the land, they had conceived the bright idea of taxing it for bin;. (laughter.) That was very patriotic. !Laughter.) The Leader of the Opponition said all taxes ought robe put OD land. That meant that the party opposite were really food taxers arid taxers of the raw material from which we got food and 'Melter. Ha supposed that even the brewers would get off when they had placed all the taxes on land. (Laughter). Mr. 0. I).kV EV SMITH L., tleinshorough) supported the Bill which he described se a floe democratic meas- ure. It seemed to mark a cleavage in the ranks of the Conservative party,for It was the first time they had wades definite attempt to solve the 1.1 (portion. The Bill did not go far enough hut it was an instalment sod Le was perfectly willing to vote for it, in the hope that they would get the remainder very shortly. It. Was the spirit of the age that the people should wish to acquiro the booms in which they lived and the land on which they worked. By giving tenantn and skilled workmen the opportunity of purchasing buildings they mould not</p>
        <p>enly develop the physione of the race but build up that staunch, hardy middle class which had been the backbone ninny country.Morever instead of sending the children of rural tenanta too compete with the thousands who were already striving for work there, they would give thorn the opportunity of staying at borne and working on the farm with a clew to becoming ita ultimate owner. _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. F. A" natty IL,, Dover), said he hail been somewhat baffled to uoderstand the attitude of the , Lender of the Opposition towards the meaoure. If it v.. the Intention of that gentleman to see nothing good in the measures advanced by the Conservative Government and to smother him conncience In order that political prejudices might have top may, he the opeaker) did not envy Lint the unfortunate predicament in which be found himself on the present occanion. (laughter.) The longer they lived the more they realized that there were good points in all concern onus and he believed the present nreesur• bore evidence of great sineerim. It narked one of Moire great eocial edvanc'en which would do a areat deal to arreot the growth and progrem of Socialism—he meant the unadulterated Socialism expounded by Mr. leir Hardie end Mr. Philip Soirwden. If them was one gondola of the House that ortaht to give the measure whole-hearted oupport it wan the Nate-modiste. He congratuinted the Government on the complete revereal of their attitude on the land leen- tier. When tend measures elorely akin to the present one in principle had been introduced into the Imperial Parliament they had hail to encounter stern end hitter opposition. If there was one thing the Lihernl early must take closely to hmrt, it was the fedi.] miateke of attempting to legiolate rhea(' nf the people, ond the majority of those who fat on the Liberal benches were not prepared to give a reedy cement to n ocheme of land not ionalisetinn. which would logically be the foremaner of the maim, Hon of all the rElialle of production. ("Ni..') One of the danger, of the prement dey wart the tendency to avoid the responsibilitiee and Inird,re of individnaliorn, and tem too mech upon the State. If in the future it won found that the industriea of the ponsant proprietors were hampered by unfair tariffs. the Government of the day woold have to consider! the quention of readjusting taxation so vs to militate algebra the foreigner end encourage the home producer. (Government cheers,. Mr. El a WGIORNe. '(C., E. Belfaot) arid that it wee not the fault of the landlord that no ranch oericultural lend hod gone out of cultivation. They would compete very favourably in their treatment o• tenants with the owners of ordinary holm property. Mr. J. JARMAN (1.., Burton-on-Trent) onpported the rejection of the measure. He twitted the Oppnait ion on the inconsiotencies and contradictions of their various speakers. For instance the President of the Board of Agriculture had said that more profit arose from the tenancy of land than from its ownership. Therefore, in his Bill he said • Let ea have more owners and leen profit." (Laughter.) Thera was no justification for the otatement that the Liberal policy of small holdinga was not r succese, for it hod Dever had e fair chance and it would not have so long as the Conoervatioes had their way. The Government had pledged themselves to resist food rakation,but how they could do that and tinker with tariffs in the, interests of the farmer he failed to tanderntend. With regard to ,the taxation of lend or land valees, he ohserved that neighbouring landowner hod apparently been cont. polled by the Liberel Government's iniquitous Budget. to diopose of his land to a brewer who lad been ruined by the ammo measure. (laughter.) Havina criticised several clauseo of the Bill, the Spanker said the serf' cultural question could not be properly dealt with until the meano of tronsP, rock rents, &amp;c.. were also trebled. The Primo Minioter lurid that ft tenant who did not work his land properly ought to he deprived of it. Why should there not he • haw to take land away from lan3lordo who wort/d not farm it all ? • The PRASIDENT or 111 it BOARD or /1,14.1,r-urt•Re (Mr. C.Plineton) in replying to the discussion maid the Member for Limehoese (Mr. Harry New), would soon learn that tub thumbing would not do in Warrington end that sound arornent wog looked for in that Hone.. (laughter and epplouse.) The Liberal opposition to the present Bill was merely their old trolley of denouncing the landlords. Thu Conservatives were a party of progress hut not of collectivism, which would sink everyone to the sane dead level. On a vete being taken the " Ayes " appeared to be in nconsiderable majority and the Bill was passeJ with- out s divioion.</p>
        <p>Nett I'ewl/1y evening the (-henceilor of the Exchequer (Mr. F CL Taylor) eill introduce the Budget.</p>
