        <p>ASTON V. DARrSEURY.</p>
        <p>A friendly game- between Auton and Dareabury ti place on U. ground of the latter on Saturday rota ing in a victory far the visitors who scored live goals Daresbury'a one. T. Hotter had hard lines in the ft few minutes of the game striking the crossbar with hall. Although the Daresbury players played on g genie saying that it was the first snatch this season lacked that combination which the Aston players played, hence their downfall. A. Depret scored Deresbury. Mr. I'. Booth act.] u referee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. I. OUTR,nM MAlistl•LL, in the course of his lecture, vigorously denounced the Divorce Act and the DeceasedWit,'. Sister's Act. The !Icily Scriptures, he said, required a little study, sod they, as children of the Church, simply heard and obeyed. That was their position with regard to the cpteation of the Law of Marriage, and they had to obey. They believed that Lb. Church anon Kingdom, and that it hail the power of making laws for its own subjects. And so it was with the law -1 Christian Marriage. Their law. were : 'Marriage for life ; no divorce, but separation under I regain circnmstance&amp; Ton law of the lend for some reason, continued the lecturer, supported divorce, and the Law also allowed a man to marry his deceased</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN mod that what the Sunday schools had accompliabed in the peat had been marvelloua, hut be was convinced that with a little new method and if new life were intuited into the Sunday school people g renter I mults would come of theirefforta. Applasee; they welcomed Mr. l'orritt gladly and they were glad to think that one who received his each training in Wycliffe Sunday school was doing such a grand mirk for Christianity not only through the paper hut by means of the Sunday school. (Applaumi. Mr. Poenirr genes graphic account of bow he ran a Sunday school in London on modern linee.He ccii thet there they got men and women of ability to work and make the Sunday schools efficient whereas in Lei...hire and other places them was •n idea flint anybody who was pious might become a teacher. Ile considered that the ruin:ion of the Sunday school ea. Evmgelimic. By that he did not mean Torrey..klesander or Moody. Sankey type; be meant that the method met be neither emotions!, nor •IHDFIBIit,OI.I nor sentimental ; It must be educational having behitid it the purpose of evangelism. It was necessary in the school to endeavour to makeJeatis Christ present to thischildren—they must try to visualise Him Co that the children would realise Hie Preesece. lie thought that the teedking should be embodied</p>
        <p>Mrs. Woalhamis 10, Jubilee Street. North •I Brighton, writes : " Ily little girl wan very bad birth With a shocking rough and difricult breat and although .he had the beet treatment was decl Honsaroptive. I wee at • loge what to do on friend said, • Try Veno's Lightning C.rig:, Cure,' after two doses her c ugh was eased. and very oh !ehe was a new child. If I had not hes° able to the cough I should have lost her." Vertetl4 Light Cough Cure was awarded the Grand Prix and Medal for safety and efficacy at the recent Pane He Exhibitir,n, and is a perfect remedy for all chest, I and throat troubles. Price Is. lid. and 21 of all chemists.</p>
