      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FAIRFIELD WARD LIBERAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD LIBERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. New's Interesting Address. A meeting of the Fairfield Ward Committee in con- nection with the Liberal organization, was held at the Liberal Chili, on Monday evening, Mr. George Walker presiding Amongst those present were Messrs. G. T. Rushton, S. H. Walker, B. Hall, J. Seed, E. Jolley. it. T. Parkes, J. Pbomix, W. E. Marsden, H. Howard, J. Allen, W. Butterworth, J. Jarman, W. Birch, J. Ainsworth, D. Houghton, J. Gatley, J. Patton ( White- cross Ward) A. Jones (roan Hall Ward), J. Wilkes and J. Critehley (St. Austin's), W. Clegg, (Howley) and Mr. C. H. Parker (President of the Association).</p>
        <p>Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from Messrs. W. Haigh, and J. W. Jeffery. After Mr. Walker had given a report from the Finance Committee which had just been held, Mr. 11. Nxw gave an address on the reversmn duty. He pointed out that the reversion duty was one of the most important taxes contained in the Budget. He proceeded to illustrate by various instances, from the rent of land had been put up by landlords. In 1907 the Duke of Norfolk's agent proposed to raise die rent of a tenant, who had spent £3,000 on the property from £IS4J to £550 per yearand of another tenant from £O3O to £6BO. Of course, said Mr. New, the tenants were helpless for if they. went elsewhere they would have to begin to build again. That kind of thing was not fair and just, and they as a nation of democrats had a right to demand from the leisured dowses, a larger proportion of the nation's taxation than hitherto had been the cage. (Hear, hear.) It was time that the poor did not have to bear the whole of the country'm burden of taxation. 'the consequence of the higher rents had been lower wages, owing to competition, and slum property as a result of the low wages. The land was the people's and to the people it must go. (Applauee.) The time was coming when the rich man would have to pay his fair share of the country's burdens. "lam cousinced." concluded Mr. New, " that the only reason Tariff Reform ham been expounded at such great length by the divided Tory party is became they are determined to divert the people's attention from the burning question of the hour- the taxation of land values. Mr. Lloyd-George certainly deserves the warm support of everyone who realises that the land hitherto has not borne its fair share of taxation—but in the future it will do so. Thereby means will be found to get people on the land to prevent the rush to the cities and towns. Avoid competition with the town man, and prosperity in • much greater degree will come to this country of which we are so proud." (Applauae). A discussion was afterwards taken part in by Messrs. C. E. Parker, B. Hall, J. Seed, M. Howarth, J. Ainsworth, G. T. Rushton, anti the chairman. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. New for his address, and he was asked to give, at the next meeting, a further address mn " State insurance and du relation to friendly societies.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ONE OF MANY IN NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</head>
        <p>ONE OF MANY IN NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</p>
        <p>In every town in the kingdom there are many who suffer from kidney disease, too many of them not knowing the cause of their trouble. Among the easily recognised signs of the kidneys being weak or diseased are backache, urinary troubles, flerVOUSIle., tiredness dizziness. and watery swellings. In every town there are many ll - o have been cured Inc Doan's Pills ; it is one of then, in Newton-le- Willows who narrates the followingesperience. "For wane time I Buffered with backache," says Mr. Henry Shaw, Southworth-road, Newton-le-Willows pains in the beck which I could hardly describe. It NVns very difficult for me to bi.mil down, and when down I could scarcely get up again. I suffered agony at such times, and it was equally as bad to sit down This Wan especially bad after walking too far. " Donna pills were wonderfully effective in thi complaint, in fact I have not required to use th remedy since it took my pains away. I would aim'all kidney sufferers to try thin kidney medic' (Signed) Henry Shaw." Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillir ninepence per box six boxes for thirteen an, —. ~ , •</p>
        <p>E. E. PETERS.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Church is one of the moot important and flourishing centres of religions work in Warrington and never in its history has its influence and prosperity been greeter than at the preeent time. Since the church was built in Is3o the pariah has grown considerably, and although St.Parnehas' parish has been carved mit of it, the district served by the church has to-day a population of /4,000. The clergy have the loyal and enthusiastic help of a large body of lay workers ; the number of communicants and the general congregation have for some years shown a steady increase, and the finances of the church are in a thoroughly sound condition. The lies. William Pracecamp, who has been vicar of St. Paul's since May, 1902, is a Devonshire man, hie native place being a village neer Ilfracombe. He attended the village day school and by dint of hard work qualified himself for the teaching profession. ultimately taking up a post as mister.' master at a large boys' who'd in Peterborough. Enrly, however. he ahowed a decided inclination for Church work and , when he was invited to engnge in miesion effort in: Sheffield under the late Archdeacon Mokeney he. readily midertoolt it and gave up teaching For fourteen years he laboured most euccesafully in the poorest pelt of Sheffield, conducting varktis clams and ruisaion services. His congregation on Sunday evenings grew front very email beginnings to nearly Loterand he held a Bible class with • membership of several hundreds. He war the recipient of several token,' of esteem and affection from the people amongst whom he worked so devotedly. At the lage of twenty-one he was presented witit a</p>
        <p>1:2121Na</p>
        <p>REV. W. BRA'7ECANIP.</p>
        <p>THE CLERGY OF ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>beautiful set of books ; later he received a gold watch from the Bible Class, and on the occasion of hi, marriage he had a further gift of • crow of cutlery and silver miser. Twice during the time he was in Sheffield he acted as hoe secretary of the Relief Committee, and for six months during the coal strike he organized soup kitchens, Ac. On the draih of Archdeacon Blakeney he derided to take Holy Orders. Having successfully passed through a theological courts, at the London College of Divinity he watt ordained deacon by the late Dr ltyle, Bitthop of Liver- pool, at (thristmaa ISttS. His fret curacy wan at St. Peter's, Warrington, and in that thickly populated and very poor parish he found abirnilant scope for his energy and euthusiarrn. It fell to his lot to take control of the 'wish during the lengthened period that him Vicar was away ill, and Iris ministrations were no highly appreeetted that the congregation presented him with a purse containing .f. 30 with nu illuminated address. At the end of three years Mr. Braceramp returned to Sheffield and hecame curate of St. Itarnabate Church. He had warcely taken up his duties there when he was ~flared the living of St. Paul's, Warrington,which had become vacant by the preferment of the Rev. H. V. Pigot to St. Thome', Stockton Heath. Mr. Itraceciimp'a preach- ing arid parochial acti•itics are on distinctly evangelical lines and he makes a special feature of the Bible class for mein which is largely attended and is a very valuable force in the manly Church enter- neins, which claim attention and .11 for hard work.. During Mr. Itracecamp's incumbency the church has</p>
        <p>been beautified and the sanctuary enriched by the introduction of a new rerralos, an improved heating apparatus has been installrnl,and a large vestry erected. Puling the Mayoralty of Alderman Smethurst, Mr. Brio...camp acted as his chaplain, and he at present holda the offices of chaplain to the Warrington , Territorials and chaplain at the Union Workhoime. Mr. Braceesinp has an efficient, painstaking and energetic colleague at St. Nulls in the person of the Rev. E. E. Peters who him held the curacy since Elf ill. Mr. Peters received his University tiaining at Corpus Christi College. Cambridge, arid took the degree of If A.. as well sia thirst clam in the Theological Tripos. Ile was ordained deacon inn 1099 and priest in 19011 by the llinhop of Chichester, his finnt curacy being at Newhaven. Since then he has worked in the parishes of St. Ann* Aigliorth and Bedworth. The minsion of All Saints, which is doing a splendid I work in one of the poorest di.tricts nsf N'arrugton. is in charge of the Rev ..f. If, Nukes who has proved himself to he eminently fitted for thin somewhat difficult 'sett and has rallied to his aupport a united and vigorous body of lay worker.. The congregations are out-growing the accommodation of the mission and a acheme is shortly to be placed in loud for the ' erection of a new church. Quite recently the mission was provided with a new organ and the outlook in in every respect most promising. Mr. Bankes wan edu- cated at Windham College, Oxford, and obtained hie ILA. (with honours in history) in 191/5. He Vtil ordained deacon in hOist and primrt in 1909 by the Bishop of Liverpool am! Ram is his first curacy.</p>
