      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Rev. W. Braceeamp's Return.</head>
        <p>The Rev. W. Braceeamp's Return.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W.Brececamp preached at St. Paola Church on Sunday morning and evening and made a strong appeal for contributions to the local Infirmary. Before com- mencing his sermon in the morning he said be felt it to be his bounden elute, in the presence of God and in the sight of the congregation, to thank God for all His benefits to him during hi. travels. Ile also thanked the members of the congregation for the loyal rapport they had given to the church and clergy during his absence, God's work not having been allowed to suffer in any way. Ile had seen and learnt a great msny things which, he Liped, would be for their mutual benefit and help from time to time. Vhen he read the Ott oration "to render thanks for the great benefit we have received," he could not help realizing that the words appealed to him perhaps more than over they had dune before.</p>
        <p>The Church Missionary Society. There was a fair attend,nce at the normal meeting of the Medical Mission Auxiliary of the Church Mission- ary Society which woe held on Tuesday evening in St. Ann's school. Dannett-street. The Vicar of the parish, the Rev..lames Daties,presided and a arrnly commended the work of the Auxiliary. The Rev. W. R. Cole, medical ^rg..iYing secretary, cane an interesting address entitled "Among the Pathans—N.W. It was well illustrated a ith lantern slides. Allusion ! was made to the great benefits which mankind had I derived from the introduction of Christianity, nod Mr.! Cole contended that if people would only carefully ! consider the question there wrodd be no need to plead to thorn to support missionary work. The Medical Mission was an effort through hospital work to raise men's minds to higher and better things, to give them a true conception of what Christianity was, not to take a mean advantage of them but to heal as part of the message of Chriatianity.</p>
        <p>All Saints Mission. Preaching at All Saints' klission, Bewsey. on Sunday evening the Bev. .I.lf. Bashes, 8.A., dealt mainly with the work of hospitals fle said that there were those who like the Phase.e of old gate gifts to hospitals and snob like last itotions not because they felt much eympathy for the sufferers, but because they wished to be known en philanthropists and have their names pub- lished in the list of subscribes. He believed this class was fast diminishing. The Christian spirit of love and sympathy had lasrmested all classes of society that most people gave now cot of a real desire to help. Then there wail the selflab class, like the Scribes, who never gave at all, who did not believe in India- criminate gi•ing—es time worn phrase which was often used as a cloak for the moat unspeakable meanness. Then there W. the so-called Socialist who pointed to the liiishops and clergy and beautiful churches and cathedrals and said" Look at the groes extravagance there—the waste of money. What care can such a relig ion base for the poor cripple at its gate ? " But they moat judge the Christian religion by what it did, not by what its enemies said about it. the more true their religion, the greater was the humanity of the ' people. Those who cut of love for God gave their mosey for the building of beautiful churches. out of love for their brethren recognised the public responsibility in ministering to the needa of sufferitig humanity. True philanthropby and human sympathy went hand in hand with genuine religious faith. The Christian faith, he concluded, brought hospitals into being, and he believed that if that faith were to die all hoapitala would be inevitably closed. And where did the monis• for the hospital fonds come from? he asked. It was sot from the secular Socialist solo despised the Ch faith, for when people lost heart for God they lost heart for such philanthropy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>English Church Union Address on The Marriage Law:</head>
        <p>English Church Union Address on The Marriage Law:</p>
        <p>The Rector and Rome.</p>
        <p>An interesting address on • The Christian law of marriage " was delivered at the Church i 10100, Bridge- street, on Momlay evening, by the Rev. T. (Jamul Nfardwill, of Teddingion. The meeting wee under the aupices of the English Church Union of which Mr. Marrhall is the organi,ing secretary. There was a fair attendance.</p>
        <p>Ma C. S. lit ROESS, who presided, refethecl to the contyoyeril which is going on between the Rector of Wamington and the Rev. Father Whittle. Ile Aid 'd3l2:lrotP:d'en rboeves.rsiida) to deal ith which . bed o f betehne rsZercli Whilst they of the Colon did not seek controveny ivth the Roman Catholic. they did not fear them. Their duty west° defend the diwipline and doctrin,of the Church of England, and it did not matter who opposed them. they would take up the cudgels and fight as earnestly as they could for the faith once delivered to tbe Sainte. (Home, beer). .We claim to be the representatives of the Catholic faith in England," be continued. -I do not say this in any spirit of bitterness to the Roman Catholics, but we must make it clear tutul with whom we come into cos, tact, that we do represent the Catholic faith here. The Churcha teaching on this point is perfectly plain. The word ' Protestant ' is never heard in the Prayer Book, but the word • Catholic' can be traced over and over again. The Protestant religion is nnything we hke to make it, but thethstbolic faith is clearly defined for,us in the. Creeds of the Church." Hear, bear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <p>wife'a sister. Plen, therefore, was allowed to ciefy Clod. The only course pen to the Church was to make it. own laws. Althoeigh by law, to marry again after divorce was not a crime, it was, nevertheless, a win egallOt God. There acre, he admitted, hard cases. For instance the husband or wife might be a hopeles• lunatic, a drunkard, or perhaps, a gembler, and under thyme circimistancee it wan very herd indeed. But hard cases had to be borne end if they taunted in Urid they would find consoletion. Hard cases meet not be met by the breaking it God's lair, hut by the power of Christ end the grace of the Sacrament,. God would revee let any case be too hard for them, for it. bed add " Ply grace is oullicient fur you." Before the passing of the Divorce Act the heppy home Woof England ease house- hold word in foreign countries, but there wean,, doribt that divorce had injured Englund's domestic life. The Divorce Act had taken all the holiness out of the marriage ceremony. ,5 hen people worried noweday• they would rep," Well, if we don't get on together we can easily obtain a divorce." It has ruined family life," the rev, gentlemen went on," and are we, ea members of the Church of England,going to countenance this divorce law ? No. We may not be able to get the Divorce Act repealed, but we cao have nothing to do with those who live in legalized sin. I will give you a proof of shut the Church Ezion can do. When the Di•orce Act was permed there via. no Union, bid it took forty-nine years to pect the Decermed IVife's Sister's Bill, owing to the exertions of the Union. We have to cleave to that which ie right and to atm that our priests ref one Communion in the mon of divorced pen mine, and those who have married under the becalmed Itife's Sister's Act, (Hest., hear.) Mn, NIA 11,11A1.1. concluded by moving the following resolution That this meeting holds strongly that the law of the Church no to Chrintinn marriage cannot be altered by any Ant of Parliament, rind that it is the duty of the clergy end laity of the Church of England, to °enthuse to meintain and obey the law of the church as declared in the table of prohibited degrees which is ordered to be hung up in all our churches."" That it lathe duty of all loyal menibers of the Church of England, not only to oppose rigorously any extension of the facilities for divorce, but also to agitate for the repeal of the Divorce Acts, as being contrary to the Lew of God, an declared in holy Scripture end taught by the Universal Church from the beginning." (Applause). 'the Its, TOR in seconding referred to the controversy between Father Whittle and himself. He pointed out that lowa. not of kin own seeking but arose from the publictt ion in epee( the local papers of photographs of the Parish Church clergy and with biographical notices in which he the Rector) wee deocribed as a member of the Catholic or High Chorch party. In consequence he waa inundeted with pernplilete and he ileenied it his duty to refer to the Emitter at a meeting of the ChurcESociety. An to the motive he had in his mind, he wished to any that he did not want to attack Father Whittle. A point was involved of utincet importance and it was lathe interests of truth that he had spoken. in had no intention to write lurther,but, after consideration, he had written what, perhaps, might be his flonl letter and it would appear in the local papers neat Saterday. Ile wanted them to look carefully at what he had said on two points—one on the subject of coneecratioa and the other upon the subject of transubstantiation. The Cri•r HAL, said it was •secred dirty for members of the church to preeerve the sanctity of the marriage tie. Whilst he was afraid that it was almost neelens to endeevour to obtain the repeal of the Divorce Act, they must certainly insist on the right of the clergy at all times to refuse to re-marry divorced pers.a whether innocent or guilty. 'they should al SO strongly endeavour to preveut any additional facilities being grented to make divorce easier and cheaper. (Hear, hear). The molution was agreed to. .</p>
        <p>The Rev. 3.11. G. 1101.1.1N0 WoltTli, hon. sec. of the local branch of the E.C.U. proposed a voteof thanks to the lecturer for his address, and also to the Chairmen for presiding. Ile repressed regret that there ens not larger attendance at the discussion of each an important question, and particularly that only teo of th.2,local,Flergy had seen tit to he present.</p>
        <p>Mr. Co,rso seconded the [cotton which was unanimously carried.</p>
