      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>St. Sambas' Church.</head>
        <p>St. Sambas' Church.</p>
        <p>Daring Lent the Vicar (Rev. J. IL IC Hollingwertb; has been delivering an interesting cod instructive .ries of addresses on the ammd and symbolical significance of the various parts of our churches and the reason for the special architectural arrangements and the orna- ment, used. Preparations have already begun for the erection of the new organ which it is hoped will be lustelled by Easter. On Sunday evenirg last (Hospital Sunday) the preacher was the Rev. 11. It. Protberoe, of Ashton-in-Ma her field, shone subject was . Temptation.' , St. Patrick's Day Concert. Twelve months ago a most ancomsful concert— the first of it. kind for a long time in NVarrington— watt held in the Parr Hell on St. Patrick's Day, and on Friday evening last, to celebrate the mime occasion, a second Irish concert WAR held which proved even a greater success than the feat. It was organized aa before by the Rev. Father Howley and the trectors of Sacred Heart Catholic schootand the crowded house on St. Patrick's Day showed its appreciation of the varied sod well-balanced programme in interest andenthusiesm from t he commencement to the concluding chorus song. The singing, dancing, and character sketches given by the Sacred Heart Schottl children reflected the highest credit on their teachers. The Gipsim Chorus" and " Caller Herrin" song and Irish dance. wore capitally done by the senior school children, and the song., lullabis, and choruses given by the infant , children noted the huge audience to enthusiasm, , hearty laughter, cad gmerom applause. Rarely have we aeon children of such tender years acting and singing in such a plowing and most interesting manner. The programme, of which the children', part was the life and soul, wan varied by Irish songs and 1 dances by excellent artiste from Liverpool, Manchester I</p>
        <p>gad Warrington.The Rector,Father Rocktiff contributed a very pleasing item in Sylvin," arid an Irish story told by Father Dowley on hearty laughter and applause. The humid,ue songs sod patter by Mn. Willie Farrington added to the mirth of tho occasion, while the •tirring Irish ballad songs elven by Miss May Murphy end Miss Minnie Madden, Miss Fastens and Miss Lily McCarthy were warmly received. A pleasing wing in Irish was rendered by Miss Sheridan. An exhibition of Irish step-dancing by Mr trkfahony and Mn Sheridan and the four-hand Irish . reel, danced in the old style by children from , Seacointie, proved moat interesting items during the! night. On all sidee it was egreed that the concert was the finest of ita kind ever seen in Parr Hell, and n eon. siderable portion of the audience although not Irish , were as pleased ria the Irish themselves. No doubt many hundreds in Warrinettin will look forward with plewitire to the next Dish celebration of St. Patrick'. • Day. We offer our hearty congratulations to the teachers, Mies !hum:We, Mice Forney, Mien McAdem Miss Ilaney, end the dial! of the Sacred Heart Khoo!' on the well-merited success; of their pupils in the feast of Irish music, mirth end song.</p>
      </div>
