      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SHIP ON FIRE IN THE THAMES.</head>
        <p>SHIP ON FIRE IN THE THAMES.</p>
        <p>Our picture shows the extraordinary spectacle of a:hip on fire in the Thamic ne steamer, "North Point," was bound for Philadelphia, and discovered en fire shortly after leaving the deck. The crew, which numbered forty, were aroused by the captain, and all saved by tugs. Five sailors, unable to jcin their comrades, owing to the heat of the decks, lowered themselves by ropes over the side of the vessel, and were then rescued. The vessel in a short time was a floating furnace, the iron plates on her sides being red-hot to the water's edge. Eventually the "North Point" was beached,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GOSSIP ABOUT THE CENSUS.</head>
        <p>A GOSSIP ABOUT THE CENSUS.</p>
        <p>The Commons decided upon it by a large majority; the Lords rejected it without courtesy. . .</p>
        <p>Such is the first chapter in the history of the British census, a beginning that is most modern in sound and flavour.' This takes U 9 back to 1753, and if the Lords had not thus failed in enlightenment England might have boasted a notable pioneership in a matter which all the world now regards as of the first importance. After forty-seven years of consideration their lordships got above their prejudices, and when in 1800 the patient Commons sent up another Bill it was accepted. It is hardly likely that the Overseers of the Poor gave the Act a welcome, for it was upon them that the burden of "numbering tho people" fell, but they muddled through it and revealed to the world that on March 10th, 1801, there appeared to be 8,892,536 men, women and children living in England, Wales and Scotland and sharing among them a million and a half of houses.</p>
        <p>Ireland was not included in this survey and when in 1811 the attempt was made the results were highly unsatisfactory. The country had not been properly surveyed and much of it was as remote from the official ken as Central Africa. The cynic may also relish the knowledge that while in England and Wales the task was left with the Overseers of the Poor, in Scotland it fell mostly to the village school- masters and in Ireland was relegated to the police.</p>
        <p>THE WORD CENSUS.</p>
        <p>But a gossip about the census should begin much further back than this. What of the name?</p>
        <p>--rdio-ping back four hundred years B.C. We light upon the word in the archives of ancient Rome. As early as this the city had dealt with the delicate question of assessment and had appointed a small but very powerful committee to value and tax every man's estate and property. These two (for the num- ber was as small as that) were called Censors or Estimators, and when their list of citizens was made up it was called the Census. It was not, however, for statistical purposes that they did their work. Their return was an "ownership list" pure and simple and was for taxation only. If anyone appealed the case was heard and decided by an assembly of the people, a democratic practice from which many lively scenes would result. It was these Censors who began the bad habit of " farming " or letting out the taxes to sub- agents—the publicans of the New Testament whose name was anathema in every Roman colony. .</p>
        <p>We are only concerned with these ancient officials at present as having given the title Census to a list of names. Once conferred the word was handed on along with a lot of other things, good, bad, and indifferent to the nations of the newer world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CENSUS CURIOSITIES.</head>
        <p>CENSUS CURIOSITIES.</p>
        <p>Possibly our readers will like to know how these varied nations have used the idea and to what extravagancies they have run. It rested, of course, with the more progressive peoples to recognize the value of the census and among them the United States holds a place of honour. Twelve times, beginning in 1790 and extending to 1910, a deceminal survey has been made, the results of the last one filling twenty-four massive volumes. Those huge tomes contain much more than a mass of figures, for the census is a masterly inventory of personal and public estate which makes England's simple survey appear modest indeed. In every respect the States claim to be ahead of us as census takers. They have electric sorting machines which handle 12,500 papers per day and while the enumerating is in progress 50,000 men and women are hard at work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CENSUS NOVELTY.</head>
        <p>A CENSUS NOVELTY.</p>
        <p>Dry enough, in all conscience, the job must be, and it would be a pleasant relief at the last census to get away from the millions of uniform sheets and cards and make a study of the Indian returns. These came to hand in the extraordinary form of bundles of willow wands—whereby bangs an interesting .tale.</p>
        <p>Namana was the chief of the Pinto Indians and the Nevada Government committed to him the task of getting returns from his own people. _</p>
        <p>• gam—antis brain had probably made many revolutions before he hit upon a suitable plan. When it came it was ingenious. He distributed to the sachems of all the villages acknowledging his authority large sheets of! white paper upon which each of his subordinates drew as many lines as corres- ponded to the number of Indians coming ' under his cognisance, distinguishing squaws from bucks by curves, as contrasted with straight lines. The marks representing children varied in length according to their respective ages. Namana having collected these primitive returns, proceeded to transfer them to willow wands by the rudimentary process of carving notches thereupon. Each wand represented the head of a family, the notches his wives or other females dependent upon him. Tiny twigs neatly tied up in miniature faggots stood for those legally described as infants. By the employment of this simple mechanism an accurate census of the Nevada Pintes was obtained and received official recognition at the hands of the United States authorities. THE INDIAN CENSUS OF 1881. In contrast from this patient and intelligent achievement the conduct of the Eastern Indian stands out curiously and painfully. The talk of a Census in India in 1881 seemed to afflict the natives as with a ghastly night- mare and the epidemic of fear was remark- able. In Calcutta on the eventful night, the streets were deserted from early in the even- ing, as it was firmly believed that anyone perumbulating the Queen's highway after dark would he decapitated. Moreover, a rumour had been spread that at 9 p.m. all the gas lamps were to be extinguished, and every inhabitant must then keep burning a , lamp or candle. Consequently quantities of oil were bought up, and on the census night not a candle was to be purchased. In one quarter of the city it was rumoured that a male and female European would enter every house and decapitate the inhabitants, while a more amusing report was that the Government wanted to provide wives for the solders returned from Afghanistan, and were going to count the available brides. The coolies in one district bolted in a mass to the jungle, dreading some unknown horror ; while in another district they believed that a child would be taken 'from each house and its head put under the foundations of a neighbouring bridge, the trunk being placed on the tramway for the engine to run over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FRENCH CHATECHRHI.</head>
        <p>THE FRENCH CHATECHRHI.</p>
        <p>France has a census every five years, and like us, has fixed upon April as the best month. Its scope is great and it aims at elaborate results. Most folks would call it a family catechism.</p>
        <p>It is not enough to state whether married or single. It is asked whether the marriage was civil or religious, what is the duration of married life, how many children and in how many years. These queries are intended</p>
        <p>to throw light on the vagaries of the popula- tion, and to indicate the comparative fruitful- ness of the classes. Minute inquiries are made concerning foreigners in residence. The water supply to each house is also a point to be noted, whether it is derived from well, pump, or city mains.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GERMAN DETAILS</head>
        <p>GERMAN DETAILS</p>
        <p>It would not do for Germany to lag behind her neighbour in the matter of frivolous questions and so the German official brain has made a careful study in the art of annoy- ance. The point upon which it scores is that of the rearing of infants, and having thus scored we pr-sume the national status is regarded as having been vindicated.</p>
        <p>~ .., .... —.. A lady in Berlin some time ago sent for publication a copy of her census-paper, the following being the words in front of the blank spaces to be filled up:— Christian and surname ; masculine or feminine ; married, widow or single • divorced. By married People :—Year of • (last) wedlock ; born on ; in the year............; birth- place , in what neighbourhood For Those Born Out of the Phssian Native Land :—Since when in Berlin;</p>
        <p>occupation,profession,position.,re. Ration ..... .; mother tongue • under German or what law Any defects, blind in both eyes, deaf and dumb, &amp;c ; was the defect at birth or later ; with regard to children in the first year of life, with what is the child at present (December let) nourished, if with mother's or nurse's mint, or animal's milk, or other food ..... . ... I</p>
        <p>THE AUSTRIAN INQUISITION. But Austria easily leads. There every- body receives a bundle of papers the size of which would terrify an English workingman. After a first reading a Vienna lady actually committed suicide and public sympathy was so strong that it was generally felt that she deserved a public funeral. These are some of the more innocent of the details:</p>
        <p>How much rent do you pay? Is gas or electricity used in your house?</p>
        <p>Have.you a bathrOom?</p>
        <p>Descrilx;eaci-s-e-pa—r-a.te—room in your house„ whether with two windows or one; whether hall or kitchen or scullery? Give the number and species of your domestic animals?</p>
        <p>State whether you belong to the class of the unemployed. If not, do you follow your trade in a fixed place, workshop or shop, or by hawking? Are you a sweater, and for whom do you work?</p>
        <p>6i):n- you read and write? State your religion. This is merely the beginning. Along with the schedule is distributed instructions filling six pages of print, with 31 divisions each containing several paragraphs, and in addi- tion a somewhat smaller schedule, with supplementary questions, probably for a separate purpose.</p>
        <p>Next comes the demand for a complete personal history. Everybody must remember where they have lived and for how long, the place, date, parish, district and province of birth, the principal and collateral occupations, the principal and secondary language used in the family.</p>
        <p>This would be bad enough if it were done once for the whole family, but it has to be done separately for every member of the household and for every workman apprentice or domestic employed. . . .</p>
        <p>Compared with this the filling of the English schedule is hardly a thing•to be mentioned. We have reason to be grateful too that the papers are not collected which the point of bayonet. When England set herself for the great census effort of 1871 it was gravely announced that 32,543 enumera- tors were required—" persons likely to conduct themselves with strict propriety and civility." The wits asked whether the land contained so many. We presume that they were found, and that the present greater demand for "persons likely. etc., etc." will not exhaust the resources of our civilization. The "proper and civil" gentleman has not always had a smooth pathway, and if he had made in time past a list of objections it would he a charming bundle of curiosities. At first it I was urged that the Government was repeat- ing David's sin of numbering the people and the family Bible was brought out to show Ithat the Census was a National sin. The latest novelty comes from the suffragettes who propose to dodge the Census 88 a picturesque way of declaring that they don't count."</p>
        <p>_ Most of us will be rather glad to feel that for once we are going to count, and will receive the civil gentleman with a smile. As for the trouble, having studied Austria, we shall be glad it is no worse. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Bowdon College sprang a surprise at Latchford last week, winning a close game by 4-3. Travis scored for the home team early on but, by dint of superior all- round play the visitors were leading 3-I at half -tin.e. After this Latchford palled the game round by giving a much improved display and the scorea a few minutes from time were made 3-3. A stiff struggle then ensued for a winning goal and this was obtained by Bowdon. The •isitors owed their victory to a general smartness by their forwards and sterling play by their centre half, who proved a great ntombling.hlock to the home attack. For latchford,Tole and Knight at half heck and Travis at forward were in good form, the latter scoring all three grads.. . . .</p>
        <p>The second eleven had n warm reception at Knots- ford and did well to win by 4.3 after being down 2 0 at half-time, to the first half the Knotsford forwards got In Nome effective combination and the home goal- keeper made • series of splendid save, from herd shots, no that Koutsford woo the better team. After half-time, however, Sfinards reduced their lead end Hanks equalised. Then the Knoteford outside left made a good um and put his side anted again. Starkey once more equalized and just on time shot a centre from the right into the net. The Latchford halve, and lull hacks played ripely in the second half and had the Knoteford attack well In hand.</p>
        <p>.-7Pb—";fl;;t -eleven v. Bowdon College at Bowdon this afternoon is :—A Hill, goal ; P Allaneon, T Dion becks ; T Tole, E F CBEs., A Knight, halve. ; R Dole, J II Wilkinson, D Travie, H Parker, E Bank., forward. 'Hos from Bridge Foot at 1.45 p.s. The wooed eleven v. Ashley at Latchford Is :—W II Pickle.; A N Other, R Waleh, foil back. ; A Bradahaw. F Isherwood, R Parkinson, halves • II Fletcher, J Pearson, A Starkey, C Hackney, T 'Banks, forwards. Belly-off 2.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HOW PLEASANT IS A CHANGE</head>
        <p>HOW PLEASANT IS A CHANGE</p>
        <p>Whether in regard to scenery, occupation, or ! recreation, a change is always welcome. BO it is with the palate and the digestive organs, To achieve the mach-desired change in the" sweet "course it is not neceeriery to adopt an elaborate style of cooking, nor to incur great expense. There is a little fruit, full of pure nutriment, which in itself provides variety, and that is the delicious little Currant of Greece. The Christmas Pudding and the Mince Pie do not taste alike, and the famous Eccles Cake differs from both. Yet all three derive their excellence and food value from Currants. That is a simple illustration of how Currants can be made to play many parts. There are hundrede of perfect dishes to be made by the use of Currants. All are dainty and attractive, nutritious, and inexpensive. Ask your get-rev for a useful little Currant Cookery Book, which he will gladly hand you on request,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
