      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P. LABOUR AND NATIONAL ECONOMY. The Labour and Socialist parties every- where stand for peace. The British and Parliamentary Labour Party last week brought forward a resolution against the proposed increase of Navy Estimates. Judg- ing by the small amount of Liberal support which this motion received, it looks as if the promotion of peace and retrenchment had now passed into the keeping of the new party. While the British Labour Party is main- taining the principles of internationalism by its Parliamentary work, their comrades in the German Reichstag are taking a similar step, and are moving in favour of inter- national action with a view to the limitation of armaments, and the establishment of an understanding for the immunity of mercan- tile ships during a time of war. In Austria and Italy there is also a great movement against militarism, and last Sunday vast demonstrations in favour of the reduction of armaments were held throughout these countries. In no continental Parliament are the Socialists yet strong enough to force the adoption of their policy, but in Germany at least, there is every prospect that after the next election they will have sufficient votes in the Reichstag togreatly influence German policy. This will be the greatest safeguard to the peace of Europe which could be erected.</p>
        <p>WHY LABOUR OPPOSES MILITARISM.</p>
        <p>The British Labour Party is opposed to increased expenditure upon armaments for two main reasons. The first is that they believe that enormous navies are not for the maintenance of peace, but are directly pro- vocative of war. They believe that the statesmen of the great nations should exert themselves unceasingly to bring about inter- national arrangements which will make the reference of all disputes to a court of arbitration the universal practice. The Labour Party welcome the possibility of such a treaty with the United States, and they hope that the continental countries may soon be brought into an international arbitration union, which will include all the great powers of the earth. But the Labour Party opposes naval and military expendi- ture, also, on the ground that it is an unnecessary waste of wealth, which should be spent upon objects which will be of physical and intellectual benefit. The present huge expenditure of the United Kingdom upon armaments is undoubtedly keeping back social reform. The money which should go to finance such measures as Unemployment and Infirmity Insurance goes to build Dreadnoughts. It was so last year; it seems likely to be the same this year. Since the introduction of Mr. Lloyd-George's Budget nine millions have been added to the annual expenditure upon the navy. This is about as much as the cost of Old Age Pensions. We had to fight for twenty years to get Old Age Pensions, and when they did come at all they came in an altogether inadequate form. The excuse by which every shortcoming of the Bill was justified by the Government was that it was impossible to find the money to make a better measure. But when it comes to wanting five millions more for war- ships there is never a word from the Govern- ment or the Unionists about the impossibility of getting these millions. Under a Liberal or Conservative administration, the more money we spend on armaments and the less there will be far social reform. This fact wants driving home with all possible force. The workers have the choice of Dreadnoughts or better social conditions.</p>
        <p>A FORMER EXPERIENCE. It will be remembered by those who were taking an interest in politics in 1894, that the Budget of Sir William Harcourt, like the recent Budget of Mr. Lloyd George, was hailed as a great democratic measure which was to open the way to new financial resources which were to provide the money for great schemes of social reform. The, Radicals began to see' the prospect of realis- ing their long-cherished desire to have a Free Breakfast Table. But what has happened? Food taxes are higher to-day than they were in 1894. The yield of Sir William Harcourt's new taxes has been more than swallowed up by the increased expenditure upon armaments. In 1894 the cost of the Navy was less than £17,000,000. For the coming year it is to be over £45,000,000. This year we arc spending £100,000,000 on war preparations and for the payment of past wars. There is every probability that the same thing as has happened with the increased revenue from the taxes imposed by Sir William Harcourt will happen with regard to the Lloyd George Budget. Indeed, this has happened. His surpluses are going wholly to meet the increased expenditure upon the navy. He imposed those new taxes on the distinct understanding that the money was to go for social reform. The peroration with which he concluded his six hours speech in intro- ducing his Budget will be remembered. This is a War Budget," he said, "but for a war on poverty. It is to make poverty as extinct as the wolf which once infested the forests of our land."</p>
        <p>A WASTE OF WEALTH. The Labour Party offers a strong opposition to this yearly increasing expenditure upon armaments, because they really do want to see the national revenue used for a war on poverty. The expenditure on armies and navies is economically wasteful. This expen- diture finds employment, it is true, just as the tcriminal finds work for detectives, judges, , lawyers and goalers. But it is a diversion of wealth from a beneficial and useful purpose to one which is unproductive and wasteful. There is also a specially Labour aspect of this question. These increases of expenditure upon armaments draw labour from useful and productive work to shipbuilding yards and arsenals. At the time of the South African War thousands of men were attracted to Woolwich from other engineering shops. After the extra demand fell off, thousands of tnese men were discharged, and they suffered great hardship. It will be remembered that it was brought forward as a serious charge against the government of the day that they discharged these men. The building of these warships is drawing men to the dockyards, , and they naturally will be opposed to any proposffi which will stop the work at which they are employed. This expenditure creates ' a vet9ed interest in the businesses on which I the money is expended. Capital is invested, ' labour is attracted, towns rise up which are , dependent upon this expenditure, and all ; these interests are always united in opposing , any reduction of such expenditure. Retrench- ment becomes difficult, if not impossible; and t t refore, the Labour Party are anxious to , vent the transfer of labour to such under- , ingn, seeking rather to keep it at p 1 e encouraged mowreithPermad advantage eantod ' w ci °hYcmanen be w e hi ay body. ot</p>
      </div>
