      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>(AM MOW, B.EAERVY `l,</p>
        <p>:heir fneeting hid'led eoere i tears in her reproachful eyes, which her lover gently iped away. "Dearest, I cannot explain, because the secret is not my own.. I cannot tell till I am perfectly sure of the full extent of Miriam's treachery. Above aU things, I must be just." Aide placed her hands on either of his shoulders, and murmured dreamily, "Mtn. am is terrifying and mystic. Some day I think she will kill me," and then she shud- dered.</p>
        <p>"But, he cried, "you have found your home, and need never return to her."</p>
        <p>At these words • joyous expression of relief sprung into the girl's face. A happy smile showed her dimples to perfection. "I would claim you openly," he went on muotdel y eI can ats k t hyeo roc t oi sir,ntutcoh mtyo the. T ns o be worthy of the girl I love, I must bear a name at which none can look askance. At present it is impossible to say who I am. Miriam may hold the key to the secret of my parentage, hut only fear will compel her to reveal the truth. Till I prove that Traf- ford Khan is really dead, necessity forces me to remain in what is practically conceal. meet."</p>
        <p>"Trafford Khan not dead?" ejaculated Aide in {wished tones. "Oh, Michael, what do yon mean? What new mystery is this? Don't look like that?"</p>
        <p>He thought she Ray going to break down, and said soothingly, "I let slip more than I intended to say, dearest. I beg you to guard the disclosure as you would your honour." His intense earnestnese impressed her. " Our whole well-being depends on my ens' melon not reaching Miriam, who is, I thoroughly believe, the concoctor of an in- genious conspiracy to dot rand you and me of our birthright. But proofs are wanting, and those proofs I am pledged to find." He spoke quietly, but stern resolve was on every feature, and Aide, scared at his de- nteanour, said pleadingly, "Why should you care so much for this particular thing if I ate indifferent to the slights of people who are no more than shadows in our existence? Have I not the name reason as you to ask: 'From whom, Aide, have you descended?' I do not care for wealth, ambition or dis- tinction. It will not hurt me if 'the cold finger of pride points ecornfully at us be- cause we cannot name our ancestors. Dear Michael, let ea live for ourselves, not for the world. No matter who or what your father or mine, I am not ashamed to declare that I love you." "And- I love you, joy of my life." Their vows of constancy were terminated by Simard coming from the house and touging discreetly. .Bonjour, mademoi- selle," he said, smiling benevolently at them. She welcomed the Frenchman in her soft, melodious foreign voice, so dear to his ears, her innocent eyes gating into his. Simard was enraptured.</p>
        <p>"Alia, you blush. You need not, I have known a great passion when I was young. Feiss! The years seem long since she was married to another. Iler husband was • brute. I would 'are killed him, but she acts afraid, and begged me to go, to leave her. So I go, leave her to suffer. as I suffer, but she was spared dis- grace, lived a martyr, and to the grave she went, glad of eternal rest. Hetes Life is sad, nothing but a broken 'curt memory to carry to the end, to the end, mee en- fants. "</p>
        <p>Simard brushed the back of his hand over Lis eyes, and stood regarding the girl with sympathetic fixity. At a single glance he appraised her dress, her charming features, her shining brown hair, her slender hands, her graceful and supple figure. Extending his band he took her fingers, respectfully kissing them. "Am pr-r-oud to be tliJ friend of mademoiselle !" _ .</p>
        <p>By this time Chinnery, who was bored t 3 death, lounged to the table. "I've evidently grown out of recognition. Don't you remem- ber me, Miss Morantr "Oh, yes, Captain Chinnery, I am not likely to forget you," said Aide, her eyes spa,r.kling • .welcome. .</p>
        <p>"We 'used to ho capital friends," and Chinnery laughed, causing the girl to cast down her eyes, and Lawson to flush angrily. Simard saw this by-play, and emitted his croneking little chuckle, which meant so much and yet revealed so little. He pursed his lips together in admiration, for the sun was lighting her face, and gilding, as it were, tier fine eyes. The official forgot his &amp;trims. lion, the exaction of duty, and remained heedless of those near by. He was sadly thinking of his own lost youth. "Are you sure be is genuine?" whispered Ctiinnery to Lawson. " Staunch," replied Lawson resolutely. Simard thrust sentimentality behind him, and wan • again the astute man of iron. "Miss Guthrie may be searching for you. She may ace your canoe, and where should I be in her estimation? I must keep in with the lady. Abe, my friend." to Lawson, "you hide. Ah, ah. you most. Ah, ah, ace, go," he al- most commanded, pointing to the house. "Will you, mademoiselle, give me leave to take you home?'' Aide looked at her lover and at Simard. The offer was unexpected, and she remained mute.</p>
        <p>—t:litiny I be your escort ?" repeated Simard, slightly impatient at the delay, and glancing apprehensively across the river to the tow- ia li'uastth. I go?" said Aide, tearfully, to her lover.</p>
        <p>eignal from Simard, he answered, Yes, dearest, for • little while it is impera- tive. "</p>
        <p>suspicion is aroused the fair tigress will tear our plans to tatters, see? Go, be brave. We will make things as you call them j jump. At! At!" "Living with Miriam poisons my happis Desk" she cried in distress.</p>
        <p>"The antidote will soon COMA in the person of Romeo. Then mademoiselle, your heart will Rutter, like a birl on the wing. You will sing—sing—to Heaven, and your voice will make melody sweet. Rh, bien! Non, non, mes enfants, don't doubt me; you shall not be brow-beaten or tortured by that beautiful tigress." Turning towards the ~‘muse, he paused, musing aloud. "Her 'strange ways are a constant fascination for me, and I try to guess what lies lurking in the depth, of those wondrous cruel cold eyes of hers. I will be a constant visitor. I will make love. You mill pretend to favour my suit; she will be deceived, and we will keep her in the dark. Timm! Together we make the lady smile, but on the oder side of her mouth." He laughed a short laugh of contented triumph, and patted the girl's hand in a fatherly fashion, which gave her courage and con&amp; deuce.</p>
        <p>--;iiefonßieur. mademoiselle will excuse us just von minute." He bowed gallantly to Aide, beckoning the men to join him in a private conoultation. Aide parted reluctantly from her lover, wihn pacified her with an assurance that be wodop ! rejoin her in a few minutes. Why did eyes linger on him till he had disappe from view within the house? A !poem at • heart, and she sank into a chair, thin 'ng that she would never are him again, he upbraided herself for her silly preeenti nt. Was her first day of liberty, her first .day from the espionage of the detestable Mre. Pridge, to be spoilt by morbid forehndingsf She would not allow a cloud to darken her exultation at meeting her lover. in spite of all Miriam'is elaborate precaution/ to keep them apart. She would be content and happy in the present ; she would reel in the joys of the hour, and think leas of the trouts. loin future. Her girlish spirits were rising rapidly, and she leaned back in h. chair, • smile of ineffable sweetness on her lip's, for her thrughts were of him, and she fell into a di, dream of felicitous blnui. The stillnesa of this languorous eene of peace was disturbed by the uneppi4g of twigs, some person was forcing apa. the hedge shutting the lawn from the skendnes beyond. Aide Rat petrified when she ieheld Mr.. Pelage and Leon Rodrigues ejepiog through the tangled branches. SI sat powerless to scream, powerless to eve, • pathetic figure of terror, a passive v tim in the coareer woman's remorseless clu .13. She was PO Mag, to helPleN and orl feu. that</p>
        <p>even Leon, whose base nature was rarely touched, ordered the woman to use no violence. He was a transgressor, but the girl's plight stirred some soft spot in bin heart to the utmost. The good and bad in his character battled tumultuously against the outrage, and he was about to leave when Miriam's vindictive face appeared between the gap in the hAlge. "Captain Chinnery, Monsieur Simard, and a man whom I cannot recognise at this distance, but whose form seems familiar, are approaching. Bring the pretty truant," she sneered. "Quick, or we shall be discovered." Aide had regained her presence of mind, of which their sudden appearance had de- prived her, and was about to call for assist. sure, when the woman gripped her delicate neck with a strong hand, and lifting her bodily from her seat, carried her through the hedge. As they hurried from the lawn, over the short grass of the field. Leon and Miriam lagged behind Mrs. Bridge and Aide.</p>
        <p>_ . Miriam whispered, "Should Captain Chinnery encounter me, he will inform Mon- sieur Simard that I am living here under a now de guerre. We must depart from Mar- low at once."</p>
        <p>"Luckily your motor is ready, and I can drive," observed liodrigues, looking in the direction from which they had come. There was not a sign of pursuit. "Is this Simsrd a Frenchman?" he asked. "Yes. Why?" "Describe him," he said, curtly. "Short, thick set, inscrutable features, al• ways a pleasant smile," she said promptly, "but those eyes of his are the most piercing I over saw."</p>
        <p>"Finish," said Leon, appreheneively. "Clow,ly cropped white hair, imperial, and moustache."</p>
        <p>Leon's hands closed on her arm. "Tell me, has this man a red scar below the right</p>
        <p>"Yes; do you know him?" "Aye, and dread him. My dear Miriam, unawares you have been entertaining Mon. hour Rafael Simeon, chief of the secret police of Paris ; a man before whom every wrongdoer in Europe shrinks. To him, Leon Rodrigues has no signification, but Signor Victor Paratti he wants badly. We shall hese to get away from this neighbourhood in a flash, or the game's up." They had reached the roadway now, and at the door of the Grange stood a throbbing 40 h.p. Panhard-Levaanor, hooded and ready to start. Rushing into the house. Leon seised emits and rugs from a bench in the hall. Pridge lifted Aide, almost fainting, into the back of the car, followed 'by Miriam. Leon. who had disguised his features under goggles, leapt into the driver's seat, ordered the attendant to stand clear, threw the en- gine throttle wide, and the motor bounded forward, flying along the road at such a speed that Leon, for a few minutes. had to bend low behind the dashboard to shelter himself from the malt of wind and the blinding dust. Meantime, the three men were sauntering back to the lawn where they had left Aide. "Our plan is good, heM?" said Simard to the men on either side of him, and he chuckled, his hard dry laugh grating on the ears of Chinnery. "Why, where is Mademoi- selle?" he asked, surprised at not seeing, Aide where they had left her. "She is gone. "Gone! Nonsenne." There was a want of confidesee in the intonation of Michael's voice. which chilled the others.</p>
        <p>"The canoe is still moored at the steps," exclaimed (Thinners% racing to the brink of the lawn. "Miss Morelli's sunshade is at the bottom; she can't bare gone," he shouted. The three men scoured the none too exten- sive grounds, and Michael called Aide by name, till reminded by Simard of the futility, not to nay danger, of being heard and seen by thome whom he most wished to avoid.</p>
        <p>The Frenchman's brain was cool, his eyes steadily roving here and there and every. where in search of a trace of the tont girl. Lawson's fervid imagination and hot lover's blood was aflame; he was rushing headlong in pursuit, he knew not where, no that he was mo.ing. Captain Chinnery was tactful, as became a soldier, and he scouted along the hedge, and in the narrow gap he picked from a bramble a few shreds of white silk. His cry brought Simard and Lawson to see his discovery. "Foul play," he said, short and sharp. "Stand back," ordered Simard to the men who were about to trample about on the grass near the table. His professional in- stincts were at work, and he knelt on the ground to search for footprints. He rose, his brows contracted, his eyes closed to a slit. "A man's narrow boot, Austrian; a woman's clumsy hand, of English manufac- ture. Adolph," he shouted. "What do you suspect," demanded Law- son in a fever of impatience. "Abduction," he answered promptly. "Adolph." "Hui, Weirs." "Unchain Caesar." "Hui. tastes," and Adolph vaulted over a low fence.</p>
        <p>A loud hark was heard, and the servant came round the corner of the house, with a splendid specimen of a bloodhound, togging at the end of his chain. The animal fawned on his master, licked bin hand and bayed, but a warning finger held up and there was instant silence. Simard unfastened the leather brass-bound collar, patted the dog on the head, leading him by the ear to the table. where he saw a little handkerchief which Aide hod left there. The well-trained ani- mal sniffed at it, going round and round. get- ting more and more excited. Then he caught the scent and clashed through the hushes, the three men following closely. There was not • trace of footsteps on the brown, short sun. burnt grass, but the dog went on unerringly to the road. A motor whirled pest amidst the cloud of dust. Simard was quick enough to distinguish a pale-faced girl seated by the side of a beautiful and seornful woman. _ _</p>
        <p>"Too late, my friends—she ham gone," he erelaimed, pulling Lawson from the road Into the meadow, his heart the while hot with raze.</p>
        <p>"Gone, Aide." said Lawson, struggling to free himself from the Frenchman's hold. "N'importe. we will find her." A man, hatless and coatless. was seen sprinting across the field. and stopping when he reached the group, be delivered A plain- coloured envelope to Chinnery, who tore it open and read aloud t Blandford to Chinnery. Enemy hoc moved, we have scored, a regular sporting event. Come to town."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MYSTERY OE kAYFAIR,</head>
        <p>A MYSTERY OE kAYFAIR,</p>
        <p>JOHN STRANGE WINTER.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
        <p>When Michael Lawson realised that the girl of whom Simard had spoken was Aide, whom he had 90 long sought, he clasped her in his arms and tried to stem the torrent of her tears, but though she wept her tears were the tears of an exquisite joy. ".Dearest," he murmured, tenderly, " it is rach a meeting as this that I have a thou- sand times pictured to myself. Where did Miriam hide you? My beloved, I sought you everywhere, but• only to know that I had failed miserably. Thank heaven for this wonderful chance which has brought us togethei• at last." She put her two hands into his, smiling and saying : "Dear Michael, are you not glad to see me?" "Glad does not express my feelings, dear," he replied. "Glad and happy as I am?" she asked, shyly, conscious that they were not alone. "I mourned you as use dead, dead to me, for I never expected we should meet again," said she, wistfully. •.. .</p>
        <p>"Oh, child, I, Coo, thought we were parted for over. But I am grateful to God that He has granted this meeting. Miriam owes on much for the misery she has caused us. I will eoropel her to cancel the debt she has incurred," he said, sternly. " Michael," said the •girl in forgiving tones. "In the midst of our rejoicing ought we to bear animosity to anyone? Try to for- get. Iler punishment will fall soon enough. She cannot rob us of our love, though she may even try to do that." she added, tear- fully. There shone in her eves a light which man seen but once In a lifetime, and he binned her willing lips with a convincing adoration such as assured the excited girl that his love wan as true an steel.</p>
        <p>Chinnery appeared to be absorbed in con- templation of several dragonflies busily flut- tering on the surface of the water. Aide gave a terrified glance around and whispered. "If we should be seen by Miriam or Miss Pridge." Who is Mrs. Pridge?" asked Lawson, cal:easing her slim hand. - .</p>
        <p>" Thenwoman who never leaves me. I have escaped her to-day and found you." "My precious one, don't tremble, you are safe hero from any harm." She gave a little smothered cry. " I am afraid," she cried. "Tears! My darling, remember all the weary months that we have been apart," maid he huskily. . .</p>
        <p>"You have thought of me?" She seemed hardly able to realise that he wan with her, and that he was still hers, only hers. " I have not a single thought that does not belong to you, my beloved," he an- swered, gently stroking her shining hair. "Michael, your eyes cannot deceive, can- not speak falsely. Oh, if they did, the thought would kill me, for you are all that I have in the whole world." There was a tremuloua quiver in her voice, which touched him to the quick, and he drew her to him with passionate tender- ness, showering kisses on her fair face and assuring hes of his undying devotion. " Darling, you hold my heart in your hand to cast away or to cherish. I exist only for you, for you alone my own." Her heart beat violently at the passionate flow of love that was on his lips, her bosom sinking and rising no she listened. "Has Miriam been kind to you?" he asked. " Harsh, but not positively unkind, nave that she absolutely denied me liberty, and instructed that detestable Mrs. Pridge to spy y" my every and report tvilt to even Do tried you coerce me 'into ma' rry!,ng ;dnonsiseeL Simard?" "Simard ! The scoundrel, I'll thrash him."</p>
        <p>_ "No, no," she laughed. "lb not look so fierce, you jealous creature," rho cried, free- ing herself from his clasp and stepping farther away, for at that particular moment Chinnery did a half turn towards them, but again became concerned in the Miming in- sects hovering above the waterlilies. " Mon- moor Simard is my confidant. Ho appeared to fall in a ith plane so as to be near me and that he might continue on visit- ing terms with her. You must not quarrel with him ; he is my friend." "You told him that we were betrothed?" he demanded, anxiously. "I told him there was soma one in whom I was interested," she answered coquettishly. "You are bewitching," he raid, fondly. " "</p>
        <p>"Your actual name was never mentioned between us, for I was cautious; he asked many strange questions and seemed to sus- pect Miriam, though el what I cannot say. Its tried to glean f ro. her whether she had lived in Turkey, Armenia, •or l'ersia, but to these questions Miriam was silent. I could not understand his hints or his conversation; he talked much of precious stones belonging to the royal houses of India and I'ersia, re- lated their history, how they had been stolen and after years regained. Very dull and prosy this to me, Michael, when all the time I was thinking of you."</p>
        <p>"Did Miriam account to you in any way for my disappearance?" "Yea. she did, and moreover she charges you with having robbed your benefactor of money and of important documents. But she is a wicked woman to accuse you either of ingratitude or theft." Aide, feeling the start he gave, bruise off suddenly, a sob choking her speech. She clung to him, saying imploringly: "Why don't you deny—" He bit his lips, not wishing to tell of the thing he had done in the ,hamber of death, and said, quietly: "I do deny it. You are right, Miriam is a wicked woman." He kissed her forehead, and she nestled to his breast.</p>
        <p>Do you know, dear Michael, you must not think me childish, but when Monsieur Simard fixes his eyes on me I feel he is slowly 'compelling me to reveal the secrets he wishes to learn." Then impulsively she asked: " Who is he? What is he?'' "An exceedingly clever, brave, and honest ,an, Aide. A man whom you and I may ve in implicitly." Ha been placed in a peculiarly isolated position during `the best years of her young fife, Aide had always relied upon herself rather than asking advice from others. What she had seen of oriental society had taught her caution, and her first instinct was always to mistrust professions of friendship from those she met. A superficial student of human character would have pronounced her to be guileless in the ways of the world. To a certain extent the student would have been correct in his opinion, for she was really in- nocent of the by-ways of life, she knew no- thing of its laxities, its vanity, its sordid greed, and its utter falseness. "I wonder if you will ever grow weary of ma?" The girl's lip quivered while she _spoke. .._ . .. .</p>
        <p>"I shall love you, dearest," he said, "to the last beat of my heart." Her coyness was gone, and she held up her face to 'him. After a delicious pause she asked him a question. Michael," she said, " how did you make the acquaintance of Monsieur Simard?" At once he related the incident of their meeting on the river, but she did not seem convinced, and said: "Be careful, and do not confide too much till you know him better.".</p>
        <p>"He is to be trusted, and has joined me in an important undertaking," he maid in reply to her look of inquiry. "An undertaking?" she repeated, opening wide her eyee. "My dear little girl, forgive me if I do mot tell you the exact details of the affair. I am tongue-tied for the moment," he said, Inciting and hesitating. His reticence offended her, and she drew back to the length id hie outstretched arum. Her. :admit woo dimmed, the glamour. of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A *FAIR .DERIJTANTE,</p>
        <p>tread that the Hon. aula Legh, second daughter if Lord and Lady Newton, is shot tly to be preeente I at Court. The item, conveyed to local renders through the columns of this paper a week ago, sent me to my scrapbook to een afredo Lady (leered' piquant account of such a function. I may not quote in erleneo, yet I cannot but feel that at this juncture lady readers will be interested in the following brief extract, bearing exactly as it does upon the supreme incident in one of those memorable events in which the Hon. Hilda Legh is to play a conspicuous part during the forthcoming season. Here it in, then The room nearest to the Throne having been emptied of it, favoured occupants an usher appear. in the archway to announce to those seated in the furtheet room that "their time is at hand." There is an instantaneous and general rieing, and rustling of ekirte. " Kindly keep your vets, ladies," is cool- ' mended in stentorian t men, and thereafter in quiet, orderly procession, one by one and row by row, the ladiee,but not the gentlernen,pass before the King and Queen. As your train is deftly unfolded and spread by the attendant gentlemen, and an you prom into the presence chamber with your four yard. of bait) weep- mg proudly behind your, your name is pronounced with startling and Lonorons distinctness by the Lord- Chamberlain, in the case of presentations with the words "to be presented " called mit before the lady's name. With two profound obeisaocea, one to the King and one to the Queen, the ordeal is over. 'the train is swiftly and deftly gethered together, replaced over its owner's arm, and von are free—to enjoy the excellent supper provided by your Royal boats, the convereation of your friends, soil in due course your well-earned rest. Tully "Royal " hoaritality is stately, unhurried, ample. No crush, no crowd, no endue waiting for your carrisge, and a sense pervading all that if these things are to be, this in also how they should be done. YE MUNICIPAL MOBLTS.</p>
        <p>The amusing parody on "If I bad a donkey and it would not go," immired by a spectator of a broken- down motor-bus belonging to • Corporation not far from Mancheater and reprinted in the " Examiner" week ago, remiuded me of a costly experience of the kind which not very long ago befel another Lancashire Town Council with which I happened at the time to be in close touch. While sorely lacking reliable data, to say nothing of experimental knowledge respecting such matters, this otherwise commendably enterpris- ing b. &lt;ly said, "Go to, let us have a municipal motor- bus service." And (of course) it was so—in a sense. The one et ipelation insisted upon from the inception of the scheme was that the entire staff of the new department ehould be compreed of strictly local men. So the responsible committee chose no manager a young fellow whose chief recommendation was that he was a native, and certain other citizens eith the like qualification were sent off to get a few lessons in &amp;lying and no &lt;a. The result of entrusting such expensive machinery into raw hands was disastrous. The time-table promptly issued by the promoters proved a delusion and a snare. In the absence of skilled mechanics and with an utterly inadeipmte supply of repairing tools, no reliance whatever could be placed upon the 'bus service, intending patrons were disgusted, indignant ratepayern were spin arms against their chagrined and bewildered representa- tives, and the whole liteiiness became a laughing stock and a byword among neighbouring autholities. Eventually, the originators of the scheme did as young David Copperfield did with his stenography— they turned back and recommenced et the beginning ; and now they have • wonderfully reliable and in other respects successful 'bus service. The hint may' lie useful elat(where. MILL ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Reverting for one moment to what Mr. Phi/lip Snowden, M P., bad to nay last Saturday as to the , cleaning of machinery in motion beings prolific canoe of section's, I may just state that during quite a short period of residence in • cotton weaving diatrict of not more than six-and-twenty thousand inlmbitants all told, I had personal knowledge of many such mishap, the effect of two being to entirely scalp the young women victim., involving the most horrible form of Optical torture, prolonged hoopital treatment and disfigurement for life. "A GENTLEMAN."</p>
        <p>I take this significant quotation flour a paragraph in la,t week's issue of this journal, " What is a gentleman ?" Thus runs the title of a popular recita- tion purporting to answer the Tamar:re categorically. Let me first deal with it negatively, as it were, to the extent, that is, of illustratiug what is a snob. A so-called man of the world found in his morning's correspondence a letter signed' G. W. Jones "the writer of which made a strong attack upon him. " Say to hits," he instructed his secretary, " that I have received his letter and that while quite tinder- standing how, under the ritcumstances, appearances may have led him to form a perfectly excusable opinion, I venture to thank that if he will consider the eerions chargen his words imply in due relation with the general tenour of my speech upon the ones- Finn (full report of which I enclose) he will realise that his attack upon me is not only unjustifiable, but that the statements he has made use of in it aro not in accordance with strict veracity. As I explained to his father, Lord Bowowli--" " But this h.tter isn't from the Hon. Gerald Willoughby Jeers," hastily interposed the secretary, "it'. from George William Jones the village tailor. "Oh it's fronr Jones, the tailor, is it ? " responded the man of the world. "All right. Jost write and tell biro he's a liar." An Eastern exchange thus define, a real gentleman ; " A man that', clean inside sod out ; who neither looks up to the rich nor down on the poor ; who can lose without squealing and who can win without brag- ging ; who is considerate to women, children and old people; who is too brave to lie, too generous to cheat and who takes his share of the world and lets other people have their,." Some months ago I heard Nurse Andrews, who was then, if idle be not new, on the staff of the Royal Eye Envital, Oxford-road, Man- chester, give a like admirable definition by way of iebuke of a male patient who had behaved like a cad to another inmate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PROSPECTIVE METHODIST CHANGES.</head>
        <p>PROSPECTIVE METHODIST CHANGES.</p>
        <p>I note that no fewer than three local Wesleyan ministers will next conference complete the triennial term in such calms made and provided—as the legal phase goes; so that on the first Sunday in September their old pulpits hereabout will be occupied by others from circuits more or leen distant, while they will themselves in like manner be entering upon another' short lease in "fresh woods and Natures new." The I feature of that particular IltBBoo IN the transfer of I something like 200 Wesleyan ministers from one sphere of labour to another. One of my many friends , among them writes of it to the effect that in a special , sense all these cases involve an utterly new beginning for the men, and often also for the circuit. The curiosity of novelty, the fresh Vie. aril fresh personality, with the incitement of a pleasurable miticipat;on of that new find Sunday, almost invari- ;day culminate in a capital congregation to hear the new preacher.; and there can be no doubt that with pest difficulties conquered, end pest difference</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>~0, . • 1</p>
        <p>, # 6 40,</p>
        <p>forgotten,that that first Suntbq in September has often probed a turning point in the experience of the Church. BEN TROVATO.</p>
        <p>Here —and with this I will conclude—is a story winch seems sotnehow t arise out of the immediately foregoing rekrence --A yr.ung tarried couple with a very young child were overtaken by the parson on the way to church one Sunday no rning. Instantly, having a horror r.f infant squalls during service, he realised the threatening terrors of the situation. " Ah,gord morning T.tk ng baly to church for the first time rhe inquired. " Yeast," " Fine baby very fine baby," he went on anxionely, " but I'm efraid —er —that is, isn't he rether too young yet to be brought to servica ?" " Oh, ye neweln tbe 'feared; sir," promptly came the reaseuring reply. 'hell Im quiet. We worst goon' to bring him to make say dietorbance for ye; rotor, took him to t' chapel last Sunday to try him n t' Methodist first !"</p>
        <p>0•.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CRAFTER XV.</head>
        <p>CRAFTER XV.</p>
        <p>Three days of insufferable heat culminated at midnight in a violent thunder storm, ac- companied by a deluge of rain. Blandford,instead of dining at his club, as was his custom when alone, had a cutlet at home, and was consequently bored by the length and dulnese of the evening. Ile was expecting Lawson and Chinnery to arrive at any moment, and not caring to desert hie poet at a critical time, eat reading, smoking, listening, and looking towards the staircase at-- the slightest noise. On this particular evening he happened to be in the house by himself, the landlord and the servants having gone to the Earl's Court Exhibition, and afterwards to a supper which was a yearly treat provided by the proprietor of the chambers.</p>
        <p>. Blandford was restless and wandered from room to room 'thinking of the mysterious 1113- pearance of Biggins outside the jeweller's shop. Since the crafty ruffian had been seen loitering on the opposite side of the street, Blandford had been constantly on the alert, and having while lodging in the Seven Dials, guaged the receiver's cunning character, he was prepared for any emergency. He yawned, stretched himself to his full height, and strolled slowly to the window with a view of investigating the state of the weather. The road and pavements were glistening under the street lamps, but not a single soul was to be seen. Me applied his eye to • small hole cut in the blind, quickly retreating and lowering the name of the reading lamp. Again lie re- turned to the window; the ambush, he named it, and surveyed the shops. There was • movement in a doorway opposite, and lie fell a-wondering whether the redoubtable Niggine was hiding in that gloomy entry. But as neither man or woman appeared, lie concluded that he must have been mistaken. Indistinctly he disoenied another movement, a band; while, in comparison to the dark clothes of the wearer, was unbuttoning hi. or her coat, for the oidht ,was too dark to distill.</p>
        <p>rush* the sex of the person in the OLorway. Blandford was accutely sensitive to the in- fluence of electric fluid; consequently, he was restlees, undecided, and imaginative. There being no further development, he re- tired to bed. Before donning his pyjamas he removed from the drawer of the toilet-table small, highly-polished revolver, placing the weapon under hie pillow ready at hand, should occasion require it. Throwing himself en the bed, he closed his eyes and en. it ,avoured to cease thinking. For the nest twenty minutes he tumbled and tossed from side to side courting the drowsy god in vain. The thunder became incessant •' then there was one terrific crash, followed by • cataract or rain. The fury of the summer storm being spent, all became quiet and still, and Bland- ford fell into • heavy dreamless slumber, from which he was aroused by • rasping at the door of his bedroom. . .</p>
        <p>Half awake, he rubbed his eyes, and for • minute or so the sudden awakening only s,emed to confuse his senses. He sat up, vaxuely wondering why he had been dis- turbed at such an hour, and by what? His powers of reasoning were not dormant for more than a few seconds; the dread which oppressed him deporting as quickly as it tame. Slowly. very slowly and quietly. he crept from the bed, and stole, barefooted, step by step, across the carpet to the door, and there his eyes alighted upon k finely-tempered saw. about an inch broad, cutting rapidly round the wood to which the night bolt wss screwed.</p>
        <p>Blandford fairly chuckled, and instead of feeling the grip of fear, rejoiced that the moment for action had come. Laughing in- audibly, he said to himself that the plot wan beginning in downright good earnest, He had arranged a method by which he would de- stroy the trustfulness of the burglar, whom he shrewdly guessed to be his former land. lord, Niggins Blandford was on thorns, and chafed at the slowness of the saw revolving round in • circle. Returning to his bed, he grasped the revolver in his right hand and lay on the bed, pretending to sleep. He was not kept long in suspense; the wood crackled, the bolt was removed, and it burly man, masked, clad in a velveteen coat, a fur cap, moleskin trot, s-rs, and rubber chase appeared in the door- way. His first act was to fieer into the dark room, then noiselessly to close the door. Blandford opened his eyes, but could dis- tinguish nothing. The breathing of the man was the only indication of his whereabouts. The darkness was tantalising and trying to his nerves. A flash from the burglar's len- t rn &amp;melt caused him to disclose Use fact that his sleep was not real. The rays from the Inntern darted into various parts of tha room, resting last of all on the bed. "flume !" muttered the burglar; "a cove gnonsin'. I'd jist see if the josser's a-sham- in'." Crawling on hands and knees to the side of the bed, he flashed the lantern full on tlv, sleeper's face. There way not the quiver of an eyelid, not a muscle stirred, and Nig- pins rose, standing looking craftily at the form on the bed.</p>
        <p>''rho magher! Mister Go-as-yer-please. Is e playing weasel. I'll bloomin soon slit 'is ni.rrn if '0 is, IT</p>
        <p>Niggins regarded Blandford, a vicious glint In his eyes. poising a life-preserver over his head. 'lbis tried Blandford's endurance, but by a mighty power of will he continued to breathe as though he was in a deep sleep. Niggina placed the dark lantern on the toilet. table, readjusting the crape, partly covering his face. In the mirror. 'Then he went and bent over the bed, his hot breath blowing on Blandford's cheeks.</p>
        <p>Nerving himself for an immense effort, Blandford clutched the ruffian by the nape of the neck, pressing the cold steel circle against his temple. (To be Continued).</p>
      </div>
