      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Frodsham.</head>
        <p>Frodsham.</p>
        <p>DAIRYMAN SIIMAIONLM.—At the Frodsham Sessions on Wednesday, John Bright White, dairyman, of Helsby was charged with having sold milk containing 9 per cent, of added water, at Ilelshy, on December 29th. The Casa mu dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Moore.</head>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>THE LAND or THE ROYAL FERN "—Under the above title, Mr. C. Wileockson of Stockton Heath, gave • very interesting lecture on Wednesday evening in connection with the Wesley Guild. The lecture was a description of a holiday Spent in counties Cork and Kerry in Ireland, and was illustrated with about a hundred beautiful lantern slides. Mr. Meldrurn presided over, very good attendance. At the close a very hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Wilcockson was propound by Mrs. Jackson, aecondnd by Mr. Cartwright and suppor- ted by Miss Dutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>- -- FARM AC, DENT.—A driver, Samuel Straw, met with an accident while following his employment on the I farm belonging to Mortimer and Co., Orford Tannery, on Wednesday. He mu working with • sowing machine, when the horses took fright, and Straw got his leg entangled with the pipes, and was dragged along the field fora considerable distance before they could be stopped. He vvas taken to the Warrington Infirmary, suffering with a broken leg. 1800002.—The County Coroner, Mr. Brighouse, held an inquest at the Longford Hotel, on Monday after- noon, on the body of Joseph Carpenter, 28 rears of age, ' who committed suicide at his brother's house during the early hours of Sunday morning.—The juty returned I • verdict of " Suicide whilst of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFS AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND Or I:loPE.—Ors Monday evening an entertainment was given in the Wesleyan schoolroom, and proved a great Incense. Mr.. James Dodds, President of the Warring- ton Band of Hope Caine, occupied the chair, and expressed her pleasure in assisting the Band of Hope work at Stockton Heath. Miss Eva Tanner sweetly sang "Bells of gold" and " Who is that sitting in the , old arm chair ?'' and Miss May Tanner made an ideal grandma. Miss Elsie Hewitt wan in good voice, and delighted the audience with "A May morning " and " Dear home songa," whilst Mini Minnie Mounfield contributed "Rosen" with Rood effect. Master Sydney 'Pemberton caused much amusement with his I humorous songs "Monte Carlo" and "Since I father joined the Territorials," as did alao Master Percy Bowers with "A coon song." Miss Ethel Houghton p eyed a pianoforte solo in a skilful manner. Miss , Maud Kirkland gave a recitation with pleasing effect. The Musses Leigh, Grounds and Ellison sang " Gipsies' laughing trio," with good effect. Alias Monk., Miss Turner, Miss Dutton, Mrs. Osborne, and Master 'l'. Peruse discharged the duties of accompaniats. The event of the evening was the performance of a sketch entitled " A Real ',airy," by members of Cairo-street Sunday school, trained by Miss Menke, the rayStio fairy being represented by Mien Amy Spencer the May Queen elect), woo showed careful training in a well-executed fairy dance. The humour of the evening was provided by the Hill Cliffs Dialogue Party, in a dialogue " Paddy's iniatake." Votes of thanks were paned to Mrs. Dodda for presiding and to all who had taken part in the meeting, on the propoeition of Mr. G. Whitheld, secretary, seconded by Mr. H. Brereton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>CONFERENCE OF ASYLUM WORKERS.—A Conference of the National Asylum Workers Union was held at the Swan Hotel, Winwick, on Saturday. Delegates were present from Rainbill, Lancsater, Winarick and Prest- wich, (lenceabire) Barnston Park, York City, Wakefield and 111snston (Yorkshire) Upton, (Cheshire end Exminster (Devon). The Secretary of the Union reported that they had 2,500 members and be had sent 850 membership cards out that morning. He also mentioned that anew registered office would be opened in Manchester, next "reek when the organising Secretary, the Rev. H. W. Banchard, would enter upon his ditties. The chief item on the agenda, was um threatened action by the A sy lam Workers Union, against the National Asylum Workers Union, for taking a title similar to theirs, whereby asylum staffs might be mi.- understood. It wee resolved theta well-known firm of litanchester Trade Union Solicitors be retained and if necessary, defend the notion. The other item. were of the ordinary Trade Union busineee. A Branch wanting was held after the conference, when there was a good attendance to listen to addressee by Mr. Isworth, secretary of the Rsinbill branch and Mr. Eagers, delegate from Exminster, Devon. At the close, a hearty vote of thank. leas acoorded the speakers for their very Intereatlng addreeses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>FARM FLOOD AND ITS SEQUEL. WINWIcE_FAEIkISR:S .J.ter!oN AGAINST</head>
        <p>FARM FLOOD AND ITS SEQUEL. WINWIcE_FAEIkISR:S .J.ter!oN AGAINST</p>
        <p>ASYLUM AUI.</p>
        <p>An anti on to recover damages for alleged negligence In the laying of drains waa opened before Mr. Justice Grantham at the Liverpool Anises on Wednesday, when Peter Loogton sued the Visiting Committee of the Winwica Asylum. Mr Rigby Swift, M P., and Mn 00 Morris (inatrucied by Mr H J Lon ton) appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr E Sutton and Mr J H Whitworth (instructed by Mr J Stanley Francomb) represented the defendents.</p>
        <p>Mr Rigby Swift explained that plaintiff had farmed the Lower Alder Root Farm, at Winvrick, for many yearn Up to 1894 he was occupier of several fields under the Ecclesiastical Commissioners, In 1894 the commissioners sold some of the fields to the Winwick Asylum Commiesionem, who also took possession of a brook. From 1894 to 1908 the brook took the water from Mr. Longtoiffir land as it had done for many years previously, but in 1908 the Asylum Committee filled up the brook and anbetituted for it a line of piping, which commenced with a 15-inch pipe, and which went into a 21-inch pipe further to the south. Up to thin time Mr. Longton had never had any difficulty with rain.water. The drainage into the brook had always kept his field, perfectly dry, and his house had never previously shown any sign of flood. In August, 1909, however--after the brook had been filled up—plaintiff's fields, to the extent of twoacrea became flooded, and the whole of his potato. crop was ruined. in December 1909, there wee a heavy fall of rain. and on the 3rd of that month plaintiff discovered that the surface water vim actually coming back up the pipes and grids. Ilie home wee also flooded to the extent of three inches under the floor. Ibis state of things continued nntil a date in January, and the process of drying the house and fields continued until March. The cause of the trouble was, first of all, that in laying their line of pipes they had done so on antiquated size. The pipes had been laid at irregular levels, and in many cage. they were higher than the old brook had been. Furthermore, it was alleged that the pipes had not been eatisfactorily joined. In November, 1908, the surveyor to the local authority had reuse to caution the Asylum Committee of certain dangers which were likely to he incurred if they did nre 'metros* the izelog of their pipe.. The plaintiff cow claimed £l4l 12s. for the logs of potatoes in 1908. O:10 for potatoes spoiled is Auguat, 1909, and £37 13s. 9d. for • crop of oats sown April, 1910. Counsel submitted that the defendants' scheme was badly con- ceive 4 and imperfectly executed. Mr. J. Thompson, a civil engineer and contractor, of wr.rriegton, spoke to having inspected the pipes laid down by the defendants. The 15-inch and 24-inch pipes were not, he said, a sufficient subetitute for the old brook.</p>
        <p>--fil;."11. S. Newcombe, surveyor to the Warrington Rural District Council, gave evidence of defective pipe, laying on the part of tbe defendants. The case for the plaintiff hod not concluded when the ' court rose, and the hearing was adjourned. On Thursday, the plaintiff in his evidence said that he had lived on the farm for fifty-one years and up to 1908, when the brook was filled in, be Dever had any trouble with rainwater. After the brook had been filled lent the end of 1908—he bad been very badly flooded not- withstanding that the rainfalls had not been an heavy no oc 'n sls. et- tturle oe fenr:e, Mr. Sutton, whilst admitting that the drain we, cut off from one of plaintiff's tields, said the damage to the potatoes could not all be attributed to the cutting off the drain. Thin particular season wss a very wet one, and the whole of the potato crop could not be got in without some damage. It was impossible that the water could have been backed up nto the houre in the may plaintiff alleged. The plaintiff's OWD drains had become choked up, or were not able to carry off the heavy rainfall. This, the defendants alleged, one the real cause of the flooding. John T. Woods, engineer BIM expert on drains, deduced that the brook was not at all eat isfactcry as a watercourse. The defendants' system of piper On, quite competent to carry off the drainage, and the floods could not be attributed to that tryetem. The bearing wee again adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>PRIMROSE Leant. it.--The annual meeting of the Primrose League was hell In the C.E. schoolroom on Wedoesday evening last, presided over by Alderman A Bennett,J.P. An addresa was also given to the mem- ber, by Councillor James Broadhurst. Views illus. tratinc the lecture were shown on the screen by Mr. P. J. P rith who afterwards gave a grand miscellaneous lantern entertainment. Mr. H. Chambers who is retiring from the secretaryship of the league was presented by Alderman Bennett with a silver rose bowl in recognition of the excellent services be has rendered to the league. WESLEY GI:MD.—The temperance meeting of the ! Wesley Guild wee held in the schoolroom oe Thursday ! evening last presided over by the Rev. T. Gledding when a lecture was given by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell ' clear of Burtonwood. The Rev, gentleman who had • I rousing reception riaid he waft no stranger to the Wesley ! Guild as he bad on several ocouions attended the Earlestown branch of the Wesley Guild and he wee delighted to have the opportunity of coming to speak at Farlgets. Ile node a very touching reference to the! late Mr. Harvey Cook whom he considered seas one I of Warrington's greatest temperance reformer.. He emphasised the necessity for union and concentration of moral and spiritual I u r We have no timoThe eaid.for 'engin.s and theological controversies, as the present age cannot be bound by the same things as those of oratories ago. What we event is more unity of Church and Dissenter, un social platforms. Why not an exchange of pulpits '?" Mr. Mitchell torn went on to say that the Licensing Beach ought to be amenable to public opinion. He also spoke of the necessity of a Housing and Town Planning Department in the Town !Council, the objects of which would be to bring the rich and poor nearer together, tel in his opinion wine town planning would do much to eliminate the lower type of public-houes. He also said the women should be better represented on the Board of Guardians, and in conch,. Ilion said that in the nation that remains true to sobriety Bee the future of the world.—A vote of thanke to the speaker wee moved by Mr. Duckworth and seconded by Mr. Newton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Lyaim.</head>
        <p>Lyaim.</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON NICTUAL IMPROVEMENT Erot,,ETy._ ch, Monday evening, an interesting paper was reed by Mr H Oxley, bin subject being The Adulteration of Foodstuffs," which was much appreciated by the , members. ,</p>
        <p>LOCAL DOG FANCIRR'S SUCCESSEs.—At the Man- chester Dog show held on Tuesday and Wednesday lent In Belle 'hue Gardens, ,Mr. J. Palmer, Holly Bank, Lymm, and Mr. James Gould, Crouchley Ferro, Lymm, won the championship of the show he.sides several prizes with their old English Sheepdogs.</p>
        <p>- LANTERN SRRvICR—One of the largest gatherings assembled on Sunday evening in the Prim itiveMethodist schoolroom Mrs Redford gave a splendid address during the evening,which wee highly appreciated. Mr Thomas Newton rendered a solo and Miss Edith Goodier presided. A very touching story was depicted on the ecreen entitled 'The Lighthouse Keeper," and several hymns alluding to the same subject Isere sung. The meeting closed with • collection.</p>
        <p>CONORROATION•L CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAND.—• axe weekly meeting was held on Monday evening in the school vestry, when a good number of young people attended. Mr. James Hewitt gave an interesting address on the" Bible " which was most helpful. Miss Fanny Sutton very nicely rendered a solo Simply trusting every day." Mr. P. Lowther presided. Miss Edith Sutton was the accompanist of the evening. The meeting cloned with ;Mort prayers from WINO of the members.</p>
        <p>EVANGELISTIC MISSION.—The services of the mission were continued during the week in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom. Mrs. Redford has given some splendid addressee during her stay in Lymm and will be ' long remembered by many who heard her. Much good has been wrought in the village by her labours. On Wednesday evening Mrs. Redford gave an address which was much enjoyed. On Thins&lt;lay evening the meetings concluded with a tea which wee a great success. After tea Mrs. Redford told the story of " Her father's life and conversion." The proceeds of the tee and collection amounted to over Z 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>KNOCKED DOWN BY A MOTOR.</p>
        <p>A peculiar motor-car accident occurred in the neighbourhood of Simm's Cross, Widnes, on Thursday. While a large our laden with people was journeying through Albert-road, one of the main thoroughfares, about noon, a child happened to be crossing the road to get into a side 'street. The driver of the caromeing the danger, endeavoured to avert the eccideut, and swerved into a aide atreet on the left, with the result that a youth named Samuel Worth,aged seventeen years of 13, Parsonage-rosd, Widnee, engaged at Mr. wrenson'a grocery stores, was knocked down and injured, He was taken on the oar to the Aocident Hospital, where he was found to be suffering from concussion of the brain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED BURGLARY AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occsaionel Court on Thursday, James Lllley was committed to the Liverpool Quarter Semiona on • charge of burglary at Haydock, on Feb- ruary 24th.—It was stated that the home of • bricklayer named William Ridley, of Ha}dock Park Lodge, Haydock, wee broken into, and too suits of clothes, two pairs of boots, and other articles, to the total value of £5, were stolen. An entrance had been effected through the gerlour window. Prisoner was later arrested at Chester on another charge, but was dis- charged. He then mode a statement to the Chester police, In which he admitted having broken into the : brume Cl. Haydock. The articles atolen were pledged at various pawnshop. in Cheater and Liverpool. When arrested he was wearing a pair of trousera belonging to one of the missing cuitn. 'rhe boots were sold to a boot repairer, named blocs, Prescott-road, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS RUFFIAN FINED.</p>
        <p>Michael Mulligan, a young collier, of 19, Raglan street, pleaded guilty at St. Helens on Monday to being drunk and disorderly and assaulting Constable Swain. The officer said that on Saturday afternoon he saw the defendant and another man fighting outside the Saddle Hotel. He separated them, but Mulligan went back, and had to he locked up. Ile fought like a mad- man all the way to the police station, and two civilians who went to the officer's assistance were knocked about. Mulligan was fined ss. on the first case and 50s. and costs, with 103. extra coats, for the assault.</p>
        <p>TRAGIC SEQUEL TO ST. HELENS CASE. WOMAN DIES IN CELL.</p>
        <p>A 'soy sad event took place on Wedneeday morning at St. Helene Town Hall in connection with a charge of theft by a schoolgirl named Join:18011. At half-past eight in the morning the girl's mother, Elisabeth John- son, of Is, Csitchley.strect, who had been locked op all night on a charge of I ecnivipg, was found dead no the police cells. . .</p>
        <p>• The girl was alleged to have stolen money and valuables from the teacher.' residence Merton Bank School, and on Tutwday elm, was remancied to the deten- tion home at the workhouse : the mother being remanded to the cells for one night. A doctor who was called in etpreased the opinion that she had died from heart diem.. She was a stout woman, end no doubt the fact that she was in the cells end her daughter the subject of a serious charge had acted detrimentally on her health. The Chief Constable reportrd the event to the bench, and said that tinder the circumstances he would with- draw the charges against the girl, and there would be an end of it.</p>
        <p>FARM FIRE NEAR ST. HELENS. Damage to the extent of about £h4.o was caused by a tire which was discovered shortly before one o'clock on Wednesday morning at Windle Ashes Farm, near St. Helena. The favm is occupied by Mrs. Hurst and her two sons, and at the time stated Police Constable Cuelige of the County Constabulary, found a large barn blazing furiously. heat once sruused the inmates of the house, and liberated six horses, fourteen or fifteen head of cattle, 5505, and litter of ten pigs, and a large number of fowls. The barn, shippone, stables, and implement shed were all under one roof, and the building was completely gutted. The farm belongs to Mr. Pilkinfton, ut Rainford Hall, and the brigade from Pilkington a wee soon on the spot to aimiat the borough brigade, which bad been immediately summoned. For- t...fel!, the strong East wind which woe blowing carried the flames away fi om the bowie, which almost touches the barn OD OOP side, and from four or five stacks of hay and straw. Shortly after arrieing on the ' scene the police constable found a drunken man wander- ing about the premises, reel from atatements be made it is believed he lied been sleeping in the barn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>had been putting the flre out, end then he sold he had teen there tore Ow. Heals° said he bad been sleep- ing in the paddy-house, but that was not true, for on Monday he was paid up and discharged horn his employment on the farm. He took hie clothes away on Tuesday morning, and was told he must not come back. He turned up again at night, but at eight o'clock was turned out of the paddy-house and ordered away, and wav never noon agela until the constable found him.</p>
        <p>Constable Conliffe gave evidence, and said that in the buildings, which were all under the same roof as the barn. were winos horses, thirteen cattle and calves. beside. the pigs and other live stock. He got them all safely out. . . .</p>
        <p>The bench ordered a remand until Tuesday neat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING. ---- WARRINGTON'S GOOD WIN.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING. ---- WARRINGTON'S GOOD WIN.</p>
        <p>Vose Again Successful</p>
        <p>A good number of Warringtonians maile the journey to Bebbington where the twentieth ennual races for the Novice and Senior Championships of the Liverpool sod Dietzlet C.C. Association,were held on Saturday at the well appointed Wirral Show and Athletic grounds. The novice race had attracted the attention of ten clubs and all ran teems of not lase than nicer more ' than 12 the conditions being that the first Mx of each club should count and the team with the emallest aggregate be declared the winner. The distance, about 61 miles, was made up of about 600 yerds round the enclosure and four circuits over theciountry comprining mixed going with plenty ef obetscles, passing through the grounds each time with about 400 yards round the encloeure to finish. Over 100 individuals started and those representing Ryland'? Workmen'. Recreation Club were the only team from Warrington. Minus some of their beat runners end badly away at the start the wiremen were soon fighting an uphill struggle in which C. A. Harding was prominent. At tbe start W Wharton St. Anstins H. and A.C. had the ill fortune to conie a cropper and s good winning chance was probably thrown away by stooping to argue the queetion, while the rest of the field took over quarter mile lead—Wharton eventually finished tenth of the 82 which completed the course. Result i-1, A (Hewn, Runcorn Holy Trinity, time 38min.11eec.; 2, AT Preece, Runcorn Holy Trinity ; 3, It Young, Wallasey AC.; 4, TJ Brookes, Walitisey AC.; 5, D Black, Richmond ii.; 6, P Scott, Wallasey A.C. ; 7, E Plant, Wallasey A.C. ; 6, E 6 Ball, Wallasey; 9, C H Spence, Wallasey ; 10, W Wharton, Si. Austin'a ; 11, J Illinehani, St. Helens Parish Church ; 12, It Scoins, Wall ist”. Club Oscines i-1, Wallseey, 37 point.; 2, Richmond 112 points . 3, St. Helens PC, 139 points; 4, Runcorn Holy Trinity, 140 p dais ; 5, Liverpool Oddiellotre, 203 pointa ; 6, Ryland.? W it C H, 238. °reason led al I the way an I won easily by over 150 yerds from his club mate Preece, who took the medal for first home of en unplaced club. Wallasey held a commanding position throughout and took the team honours in essy fashion aa expected. •^-</p>
        <p>THY. SENIOR RACE- Eleven teems supplied 112 starters for the Hedge Whitworth Cop. The holders, Sutton Harriers, had only to win this thaw to make the trophy their own property nod they entered the fray with a determina- tion to take the sting out of the live defeats sustained from Warrington A.C. during the present eenaon. An additional 2; miles of country, making the di.- tan about nine miles, was the only difference in the conditions as compared with the novice race. The West, Lancashire Champion, IL Holbrook, mode the pace n cracker over the first two miles with the Northern Champion C. Vose in close attendance sad followed by W. IL Begg and F. Antrobus. At end of forth miles the order was Vole, Item Holbrook, F. Antrobus, S. Welding, W. Antrobus with W. fioltoo, Francom and A.O.Roberts,followed by batch of runners whichlocluded Jepson arid two other Warringtonlana in Tinton and Wilcock. At six miles Volta had a lead of 100 yards and Welding had become second, which Was the only change in the leading positions, snd well as the ex-Northern Champion ran 00 was unable to make any impression on Volga until the latter eased in the lent 200 yards. Welding ran exceedingly well and did not belie the amnion of his friends that he was in his best form in getting within a 90 yards of the winner. POsITIoNS. Time. 51ins. Secs. 1 C Vose, Warrington O2 0 2 S Welding, Sutton Harriers 3 W H Hogg Sutton Harriers 52 93 -</p>
        <p>4 H ii-ort;74-, Warr i ogToAn7.C. ...W 53 5 5 5' Antrobus, Warrington A.C. 6 W Antrobus, Warrington A.O 53 14 7 W Bolton, Warrington A.O 53 15 8 hl Francorn, Sefton H 53 23 9 A G Roberts, West Cheshire 53 38 10 H K Jepson, Warrington A.C. 11 E Harrison, Sutton H. 12 T Melia, Sacred Heart H. 13 T Turton, Warrington A.C. 14 H H Herbert,Liverpool BoundaryH 54 40 15 A E Walker, West Cheshire 16 A Willcock, Warrington A.C. 17 C.l Strew, Sutton 11 18 J. Roberts, Sefton H. 19 C Holland, Sacred Heart H. 20 R. McCaughey, N. L'pool Gym. H. 55 8 21,2 Morris, Sutton ; 22, J T Rirnmer, Sefton ; 23, D Large, Sutton ; 21, E Barnes, Sacred Heart; 25, 1' Hill, Ravenhead .. 26, A Alec Master, West Cheshire; 27,1 Fallon, Liverpool H. ; 28, J Smart, Sutton ; 29, NI McGson, Sacred Heert ; 30, S K Hayward, West Cheshire ; 31, E A Curren, West Cheshire; 32, L Finley, West Cheshire; 33, W A Jeffrey, N. Liverpool Gym. ; 34, T Well, Warrington A.C. ; 35, A Hancock, West Cheshire ; 36, D Platt, Raveeheed : 37, .1 McGarry, Liverpool Boundary ; 38, A Siddall, Warrington AC. ! 39, J Gaysghan, Sacred Heart; 40, D Brown, Sutton. Club Plecings.-I, Werrington A.C. (1. 4,5, 6, 7.10) 33 solute; 2,Sutton H. (2.3, 11, 17, 21,23) 77 points ; 9, Went Cheshire, (9, 15. 50,30, 31, 32) 143 point. ; 4 St. Helens Sacred Heart H, 182; 5, North Liverpool Clymnuiurn 240; 6, Ravenheed H, 257; 7, Liverpool Boundary, 284 ; 8, Liverpool Gymossitun 427. Runcorn T.H. and Sefton failed to finish the necessary sit to count a team and were unplaced.</p>
        <p>110 W THE LOCALS FARED.</p>
        <p>. - C Vow ran a very pretty race fully earning th• plaudits of the spectatora and the congratulations of hie friends • many of the competitors wished him 100 k in his race for England in the international at Cawleo n, this afternoon, and it goes without saying that Ws r- rington sportsmen hope to we him occupy a promineutt. position. H lloll.rook in the closing stages of the race anted htv\ part an captain and held the team together. Antrobus ran in a style suggestive of better things to follow. Antrohlts and W Bolton stuck together like Siamese twins whilst doing some very fine running. H K Jepson ran well, and was a factor in the team's success. . .</p>
        <p>T Torten and A Wilcock were always handy in case of emergency, and the measure of improvement both have recently shown should meet with early reward, T Wall and A Siddall are both short of training, and took matters easy when the club's tizic,ess was assured. As a team they have this seas,n proved themselves easily the best in Lancashire and third best in the whole of the country, only ffrillamshire and Highgate have led them home. Thee fully merited the honours brought to Warrington on Saturday last.</p>
        <p>ST. lIELINS NOV I,•IT TEAM RACE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>Sutton Ire not a weakened force as some critics aver ; it is evidently overlooked that their positions of fourth in the National Championship, third in the Northern, second in the Warm Lancashire, and second In Liverpool district prove their inferiority only to Hallamshire, Highgate and WaKrington. The greet improvement of the latter probably supplies the reason for the cupposed deterioration of Simon who are a very line team indeed, as proved by their form when compared with the many individuals they have encountered [big season. Bonin, the Frenchman, is regarded as hot stuff for the international.</p>
        <p>Serpent O'Neill, the Irishinito, iR expected to worthily represent the dktressful country. Other (wicks include Voss end Welding, who should be thereabouts if the course suits thero.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH</p>
        <p>Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>A two-days' sale of work, In connection with the Legh-atreet Primitive Methodist Church, was opened in the schoolroom, Legh-street, on Wednesday after- noon. There was a large attendance, and the various stalls bore evidence of the care bestowed on them. Mrs. Fairhurst, Wigan, performed the opening ceremony on Wednesday, Mr. J. Rowlands occupying the chair. Amongst those present was the pastor (the Rev. W. Cooper). The CHAIRMAN (said he hoped that the sale of ['work would be quite as successful as the one held twelve months before. As regarded the preparations for the sale, everyone seemed to have had an idea, and all those ideas grouped together would help,be thought,to !make their sale &amp;complete success,. (Hettr,hear.) Te.y ' had a worthy object in view—that of building a new church. (Hear, hear.) He did not think there WY anybody present but wanted to see e more commodious building to worship in than they had at present. (Applause). Mrs. FAIRHURST, in declaring the sale open, remarked that although she wa.s a stranger to then she was not a stranger to bazaars. (Laughter). Neither,. she added, "am I a stranger to your Pastor, for I have known him for runny years." She hoped the bazaar would hen great success, and that their high ambition of a new church would soon be realised. (Applause). A little girl named Min Parkes then presented • bouquet to the opener. Mr. PARKES proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and Mrs. Fairhurat, and in doing so hoped that the sale of work would be an unqualified success, and that the procreede would put them well on to their way to • new church. They had had • good start that afternoon and he hoped the start would be kept up. (Hear, hear.) During the work of preparation for the sale of work everybody teemed to have had the welfare of the church at heart. He hoped before long that they would have • new building in which to train the children and to w..rehip God, if possible, in a nobler</p>
        <p>Mr. Oftrvp,Tluo, in secondiog, eald that last year's sale of work wiped off ell the debts and left them with a nice little now in the bank. (Hear, hear.; lie pointed oat that at the meeting of the Natiimal Free Churoh Council held at Portsmouth not hog ago, It was stated that Trade Unionism was started amongst the Primi- tive Methodists. That was a fact that was not generally known by people who were apt to take Primitive , Methodism too cheaply. (Hear, bear).—The sale than commenced.</p>
        <p>----------- and stall-holders are u follows: —Hugh Bourne stall, Mesdames Dutton, Atkiuson, Cook, Atiq, Allman, B T Parkas Cud dinC Price.—Will tam Clowes stall, Mesdames Cooper, Griffiths, Bennett, Elwell and Dodd.—Sarnnel Turner stall, Mesdames Spruov, Crompton, Swales, Bennett, Ashworth, Howarth, Rowland, Cresswell, and the Misses Bridge and Cotton.—James Cranford stall, Mesdames Mills, Smith and Mill E Ashworth. The eale was continued on Thursday. Councillor Halliwell presided over the opening ceremony. Mrs J. G. Withinshaw declared the sale open. The proceeds for the two days amounted to £7O, and the sale will be continued to-night (Ssturdar.</p>
      </div>
