        <p>" Is All Well with Us ?" Sir,—Your correspondent who has thought fit to shelter himself behind the nom de plume " Warringtonian " is evidently somewhat doubtful on statements made with regard to the question of unemployment in this country and Germany, and apparently needs further light on the subject. No doubt he will accept the following gentlemen's statements, form least he will not consider that they have any special bias in favour of mak'ng them. Mr. Lloyd George at the Colonial Conference on May fith I Dt': said " Employment in Germany .is undoubtedly re, good. There is as much work to do as they can tind people to do it." Mr. Winston Churchill on October 22nd 1908 in reply to a question is the House of Commons said, " According to my information there is at present more unemployment in this country than in : Germany." Mr. Arthur Ilenderson, M.P., in his report on his visit to Germany with Mr. Barnes, M.P. (nee 'Times " of January 14t6, 190,4) said : " German/ has its unemployed problem, though it appears to ciolonewtdyerallittly,.lessi. than this toettrnigi,, 19118, "In ratio to the total population I imagine</p>
        <p>_. nes net b. , ssages whiekorld , of 41fe. What is th, O 4 a .., -e,„ -,ltmpiie ___ pnor b 0,11.1314411 in - -.We of our • -E` ,Tybo Flog. kte!atitarrqui wocidoraiipt ao -re, As.or, . , r &amp;Opt 4paapliatltufmid:Waittla ell •trorkera we have • , "dm orb° see iliosoTyittroing to death in a Chri-dian 0 "`- , , otlntry lull of wealth plenty for all . fithoinoney to buy it ! tis not trade unions. . ployers we ,- . 1 ,4, ~ . , • cpntrthnu,nv , 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>0 . . . . •. • 0 I . . n</head>
        <p>0 . . . . •. • 0 I . . n</p>
        <p>there are fewer unemployed in Germany than in Britain."</p>
        <p>Of course, although these are well known statesmen Ind public men, yet there are many persons who would not be prepared to accept their declarations. They may not at the moment be suitable to the mental palate and sufficiently opt etising. But those statements I submit to " Warringtonian " are borne out by unmistakeable evidences and signs from another quarter. Unemployment and emigration are clearly and cloaely related to each other. They act and re-act upon one another. If he will study the emigration returns of the two countries he will find that there has been for some years -- ,fro-</p>
        <p>now quite six times as many persons emigrating from this country as there have been from Germany, and yet Germany has, as he is no doubt aware, one- third more of a population. Take the following recent three years is an ,example, although they are only a sample of what has been going on for a considerable 'period. Emigration from Emil/ration from ' United Kingdom. Germany. • 1907 19k 1 139,774 1909 Th* glues should male "Warringtonian" ponder on the matter and cause him to milt himself the question" Is all well with us ?'' We are losing a portion of the cream of our male population and female labour as a consequence is entering more into competition with male labour. Nations like individuals must be prepared to learn from each other. Germany has learns from us the Art of Commerce. Flom her we shall have to learn the Art of Organisation.—Yaurs faithfully, E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
