      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Rector and Rome</head>
        <p>The Rector and Rome</p>
        <p>Sir,—Once again in he interests of truth and for the information of all who would be true Catholics I . . . _ _</p>
        <p>reply to Father Whittle. closely as poaeible to the points on which I spoke at the amnia! meeting of the Warrington Church Society. 1. The Supremacy of Home.—The opposition of the four Eastern Patriarchates to his claim from the time of their foundation to the present and the schism between East and West, which has existed for the last thousand years ore sufficient proof of its unhistorical basis. No candid, impartial historian would now sup- port the. claim.</p>
        <p>2. fhe Filioque.— The Church of England, as pert of the Western Church, has received this article as a portion of her inheritance, and, interpreted aright, she gladly recite. it, but she sympathises with the Eastern Church in her objection to it. arbitrary introduction into the Greed against the ninon decrees of the Councils of Ephesus and Lhaleedon. 3. The Consecration—To the Word and to the Testimony. The Sacred Narratives, as have pointed out, do rot tell us what words our Lord used in con- secrating. They simply say "Be blessed,' 'He gave thanks.' Aware of this the Universal Church for • thousand Years recited what our Lord said and did at the Institution, and then prayed to God to send down the.H4ly Spirit to make the f3read and Wine the Body and Blood of Christ. This the Eastern Church still does, and though a direct invocation is not folnd in those Western Liturgies which love come down to us it is clean that they contained sonic equivalent, since St. Ambrose A.D.. 374, with knowledge of the rite in use at Milan, speaks of "the Sacraments which by means of the holy prayer are transfigured into flesh and blood." A thousand years ago the Church of Rome took upon herself to substitute Christ's Declaration, "This is My Body," "This is My Blood" for the Invocation of the Holy Ghost, and still persists in this erroneous teach- ing. NOW let on turn to the teaching°, the Churches of England, Scotland, and America. I said in mv speech at we are indebted to the Church of Rome. 'through her we derived from the East our Faith, our Orders, Our Liturgy. But with our advantages we received great disadvantages, for we have suffered through our connection from her later additions and corruptions, let no see. The chief Use which obtained inthis land before the Reformatioantas the Sarum, which was based upon theitoroan In neither is there any Invocation. When Archbishop Cranmer drew up our find English . Prayer Book in the year 1549 he used. as the basis of the Order for the Holy Communion the Sarum [se, and he inserted a direct Invocation of the Holy Ghost REF no the recital of our Lord's Words and Acts. In t 5512 the Foreign Reformers weakened this beautiful -. add in the subsequent revision. in 1559, 101, 112 the Church was never strong enough to re- or place it in its proper position Arne the . The Scotch and the American Churches, with knowledge and in happier times, in 1764 and ave done so, ant we, pray God, at our next 1, shall without doubt do the same. Meanwhile, I all that is essential in the prayer, "Hear us, NI Father, we most horribly beseech Thee,and let we receiving these Thy creatures of Bread ne, according to Thy Son our Saviour Jesus holy institution in remenbrance of His Death [Sion may be partakers of Ili, most blessed id Blood." This its cur Invocation, and we be. at God hears and answers, l'ransubstantiation.—We all own that our Lord fis is My Body," "This is My Blood." and if we „nese words alone we might perforce be driven to etieve in Transubstantiation contrary to the evidence of our own erases; but we are bound to give equal weight to other words, and when He says after Conse- , °ration "I will not drink of TON FRUIT OF TUE VINE." And when Paul says after the Consecration " This BREAD," we are sure that Traesubstantiation is wron; So, in like manner, and here I beg my readers' u careful attention for Father Whittle dare not base argument upon Holy Scripture; but turns to t' Fathers. So, may, in like manner, when the Fathe I as they do the strongest possible language with to one aspect, but qualify it with regard to,. are quite sore they did not believe in Tr . .</p>
