      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ii‘,.,o,eni Oct halt I sunrise</head>
        <p>ii‘,.,o,eni Oct halt I sunrise</p>
        <p>of ale,da oscsaWMEthe elear</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday</p>
        <p>b.'; and tam into this, that i. the Divine Subetance, by the operstioilkilf the Holy I Ghost„yet remain in the peculiarity 411 their own nature (Calming lie Ousbus Naturie its ('hriato).,' Would he have said this if he had believed in Traneub- I *satiation? It is needless to ..1,0 further. Let all impertitl, unprejudiced readers decide. 5. Holy Orders.— So far as the truth is concerned it matters not in the least whether the EasteraChurch or the (lurch of Rome acknowledge the validity of oar Ordtre, but as regards Unity and the fulfilment of Christ's Prayer 'That we all might he one," it does indeed natter. We know that the proofs of the soundness of our position will stand the clomet investi- gation. The Eastern Church has never studied the , question. The Church of Rome has. :She is wilfully blind, and we understand well the reasan of her refusal. She know, that the acknowledgment of our Orders , would be the condemnation of heryirn action in in- truding into this land, after,tbrllefonnation, and sending here as she did in MO the prevent Italian Mission, which claims to be the Cathoho Church of the laud. Rut instructed Churchmen brow better, they know what constitutes a true Catholic, they know "the faith once for all delivered to the Saints," they snow the history of the Church in the past ages, they kaow the history of the Roman Church, and they know the history of the Church of England, and while they thank Rome for all she has done in the past, they feel that their only course is to stand apart and teach the faith, anginal that truth is, great and will prevail—Yours,</p>
        <p>mission consieaing of all tile bishops of the English Church, eight archdeacons, and seventeen doctors of; divinity, making forty-six in all. Hugh Latimer,' then bishop of Worceeter, was one of the number. One result of this great meeting was to produce a care'uland elaborate summary of Christian doctrine published by the authority of Convocation in the year 1543 (ten years attrr the rejection of Papal supremacy) under h, title of" The Necessary Doctrine and Ermlitn, for any Christian man." Therein we mad</p>
        <p>" Forasmuch as doe order of charity requireth, end the Book of hlsoortbees and divers ancient doctors plainly show, that it in • very good and charitable deed to pray for soul. departed ; and foranmuch as such usage hulk continued in the Chinch for no many yearn, Creft front the beginning, men ought to lodge and think the same to be well done. And it truly standeth with the very order of obesity, a Christian roan to pray for another, both quick sad dead, and to commend one another in their prayers to God's mercy. . . Notwith- standing they have departed this present life, yet remain they still members of the same mystical body of Christ whereunto we pertain." The book went on to condemn the " fond and great abuses " associated with the " Itomish doctrine of Purgatory," but, as the foregoing extract shows, it ' unmistakably enjoined the dote of praying for the departed. I maintain that the Church of England has never receded from that position and it is worthy of note that " The neceasary doctrine and Erudition for any Christian than " has not been withdrawn or repudiated by the Church. Moreover, Bishop Latimer declared, in a sermon preached before Convocation, that the doctrine of prayers for the dead woo nerer loot. Mr. Jaye is right when he states that prayers for the dead were retained in the Burial Service in the Prayer Book of Edward VI. issued in 150. He strives to make much of their disappearance (due, I coatend. to foreign and Calvinistic pressure) from the second Prayer Book of 1552, but he is guilty of inaccuracy when he mates that they were totally excluded. One such petition remained in the burial service in 1552, prsyer being offered "that we with thie our brother and all others departed in the true faith of God's holy Name, may have our consummation and bliss." • .</p>
        <p>, But whstever argument Mr. Jaye appears to derive from the excision of most of die provers to which be takes exception from the second Prayer Book of 15.12. is nullified by the fof•t that the very authority which published and sanctioned the second book namelypthe Act of U...iformily—declared explicitly and emphatically that it was not intended as a con- demnauon or censure of anything contained in the first book. The Art by which the second book of King Edward was ratified, states that there was nothing in the first book but what was "agreeable to the Word of God and the primitive Church, end very comfortable to all good people desiring to live in Christian conversation." The Act goes on to explain " that such doubts as had been raised in the nee and exercise thereof proceeded rather from the curioaity of the minister and mistaken than from any other worthy cause." Still stronger testimony to the excellency and orthodoxy of the first book is the declaration in the same Act of Uniformity that" by the aid of the Rely Ghost it was with one uniforMagreernent concluded." I therefore submit that the second Prayer Book of Edward VI. cannot be quoted as evidence that the Church of England does not sanction prayers for the dead. More light is thrown on the feelings and intentions of the English Reformers by the fact —to which I called attention in any first letter and which Mr. Jaye floee not question—that in the first draft of the twenty-second article, after the word " Pardons," die Puritans had inserted " Pruyers for the dead," but after discussion, it was struck out, the Reformers thus deliberately declining to condemn lunch prayers. Not only does the Church of England not prohibit prayers for the departed but she uses them --some- what sparingly, it is true, but none the lees definitely —in her public services. In the prayer of Oblation in the Holy Communion WO pray that " we and all Thy ',hole Churrh may obtain remission of our sins and all other benefits of His Passion." Now the Prayer Book as we have it to-day is the outcome of the revision of 1662. One of the principal reviews wan Bishop eosin, and he has placed it on record in the clearest possible terms that the words were ! intended to be a prayer both for the living and the departed. These are his words on the point : " Where by 'All Thy whole Church' is to be understood as well those that have been heretofore and those that shell be hereafter, as those that We DOR the present members of it. . . . . The virtue of this Sacrifice (which is here in this prayer of oblation commemorated and represented) doth not only extend itself to the living sod those that are present, but likewise to them that are absent and them that have already departed or shall in time to come live and die is the faith of Christ." . _</p>
        <p>In our Burial Service there is a prayer for the departed quite as definite as it is direct, not !ma no because of its brevity. "That we, with all those that sre departed in the true faith—may have our perfect consummation and bliss." In face of this Collect it is impossible to argue that the teaching of the Church is that the departed are in the enjoyment of their final consummation and bliss in heaven. Why pray for it, if, as Mr. Jaye and his fellow.Proteri- tants teach, they have already attained it? Bishop Colin makes an illuminating comment on the Burial Collect as it came before the revisers in 1662. " The Puritans "lie wrote "think that here is prayer for the dead allowed and practised by the Church of England and an think I. But we are not both of one mind in censuring the Church for so doing. They say it is Popish and superstitious ; I for my part esteem it pious and Christian," The only teaching against Prayers for the Dead is found in the Homilies, a strange compound of truth and heresy which can be quoted both for and against Catholic doctrine. Most writers, however, admit that the doctrines taught in the books of Homilies are not necessarily the doctrines of the Church of ! England. Nor are the practices with which fault is found therein necessarily condemned by the Church of England, for while article 35 declares that they " contain a godly and wholesome doctrine, and neces- sary for these times," it does not assert that all their contents are equally valuable and the limiting phrase " necessary for these times" implies that their use- fulness was hut temporary. On this aspect of the controversy, the came of Hawke v. Woolfrey, tried before the Arches Court in IS3S —and which expressly affirmed the legality of prayers for the dead—has an important bearing. The action arose through the refusal of an incumbent to admit into his churchyard an inscription : " Pray for the soul of J. Woolfrey. ' It is a holy and whole- some thought to pray for the dead.' " The Court over-ruled his objection and sanctioned the inscrip- tion, on the ground that "it was not illegal, as by no Canon or authority of the Church in these realms bad the practice of praying for the dead been expremly prohibited." In delivering judgment, the ['resident of the Court, Sir Herbert Jenner Font, thus referred to the Homily by which Mr. Jaye sets such store —"la: looking to the Homily it must be considered what was the purpose for which it was composed—via., to dis- courage the practice of praying for the dead as con- nected with the doctrine of Purgatory, bet in no part of the homily le it declared that the practice is unlawful. . . . Blit supposing he (the writer) had been of opinion that such provers were unlawful, it is not necesearily to be inferred that the Church of England adopted every part of the doctrines contained in the Homilies. If it had been the opinion of the framers of the Articles and Canons of the Church that prayers for the dead were opposed to the Scriptures, they would have expressly declared their illegality." As indicated in my first letter, there is the strongest ground for believing that prayer for the dead was a Jewish custom, for many years before our I.ord's coming, and that it was incorporated by apostles and inspired teachers into the practical system of the Christian Cburch. Many great writers and divinee might be cited in proof of the assertion that such prayers were in use in the time of our Lord, but the following quotation from Bishop Jeremy Taylor will *lace : " We land by the history of the Afaccaberw, that the Jews did pray and make offerings for the dead, which also appears by other testimonies, and by their form of prayers still extant, which they used a in the captivity. Now it is very considerable that, given our Blessed Saviour did reprove sit the evil doctrines and traditions of the Scribes and Pharisees, and did argue, concerning the deed and the Resurrection against the Saddoceee, yet He snake no word against this public practice, but left it as He found it; which lie, who came to declare too. all the will of the Father, would not have done if it had not been innocent, pious and full of charity." Dean Farrar tells us that "As regards prayers for the dead, it is unanimously admitted that they existed in the Jewish Church, and were noreproved by Our While there is no direct command in Scripture con- cerning prayers for the departed. I submit we have indirect evidence that the use of them was probably habitual to the Christians of the Apostolic age, as it had been to the Jews, and as it was to the Christians of the Psimitive Church after the Apostbie. Mr. Jaye makes tbs dogmatic assertion (without the slightest attempt at justification) that St. Paul's petition "The Lord grant unto him that be may find mercy ofThe Lord</p>
        <p>in that day " applied to a living, not to a dead man. I contend that a careful and impartial study of the context goes to show that Onissipherous, for whom the prayer was offered, had departed this life. This is the opinion of many of the ablest modern commentators. In eupport of this view I quote the following reasons from Dr. J. H. Blunt's " Annotated !took of Common Prayer' That Ooripherous was not then living seems to be proved (I) by the °minion of his name from ordination, which shows that he waa neither at Rome nor at Ephesus ; (2) by the manner 113 which St. Pau) speaks of his association with him, as belonging to that which was loog past and gone by: (3) by the salutation meat to the bonsehold of onesipherous, es if he ware not now one of that boueehold ; II) by the direction of the prayer towards the day of judgment and not to the time of grace and probation." Dr. Blunt also points out " In another Epistle St. Paul enjoins on the Ephesians that the), should offer Intercessory prayer as well as prayer for themaelv. : 'Praying always, with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit and watching therenoto with all perseveranos and supplication for all mints' (Eph. vi., IS). This inclusive phrase is one which brings to mind the sense in which it is used on All Saints' Day of the departed in Christ and also the passage of Scripture reepenting our Lord's Resurrection in which it is mid al.' that many bodies of the saints which slept arose.' " The literary remains of the first century are so, scanty that actual evidence of the custom of praying for the dead in the Primitive Church is dillicult to trace until the second. The inscriptions in the Roman Catacombs, however, show that the practice in at leant almost as old as Christianity, and a moot striking and important fact is that all the liturgies of the Primitive Church contlin such prayers. They make it clear that beginning noon after the ,irne of Christ, all Christians everywhere were accustomed to intercede for deported ionic.</p>
        <p>-TO atm op I maintain :—(l) That the Church of England has not condemned or refused her sanction to prayers for the dead ; (2) That such prayers have been used by the Christian Church from the earliest ages ; (3) That it is a reasonable ustutytion that the Christian Church inherited them, with the tacit sanction of our Lord, from the Jewish Church.— Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>LAYMAN.</p>
        <p>Wr.rrinoWn, March 220 d, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>FREDERICK W. WILLIS.</head>
        <p>FREDERICK W. WILLIS.</p>
        <p>The Rectory, March 23rd, 1911</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the midst of the contr.dtersy between Canon Willis and Father Whittle, may I be allowed to raise the point as to whether the Roman Church is truly Catholic ? After readirg 'logien&amp; of the history of Papism, one can hardly be expected to think so. When one reads of the horrible murders and persecu- tions meted out to those, who century upon century ago "broke their bonds asunder " and nobly stood forth in the clearer light of Biblical Christianity, a noble army composed of such martyrs as Latimer, Rogers, Ridley, Wycliffe, &amp;c., no wonder one fails to see any catholicity of spirit. A Papist book, recently published (oat on the Index), likens Luther to Lucifer " arch-liar and arch:rogue," Calvin as the biggest liar who ever stepped on French soil and our own John Wesley is described as • confounded remobate. It is remarkable how the Vatican, the most intoler- ant body under the sun, attempts to juggle with the word intolerance. Let them take as example of true Catholic spirit the United States Cbristian'Congress, or nearer to um, the mighty " Edinburgh " Confer- ence, or the Free Church Council; or within our own town the " Orient " Exhibition.</p>
        <p>But why so much discussion over a chnrch that is dying at heart ? With the exodus of her most illustrious priests and professors the power of I Papism is gone for ever, and it requires no prophet's eye to eee the final result. We hear of such nonsense : as the Pope weeding his garden and throwing the Modernist " weeds " on to Protestant ground. Where are the flowers ? The various Paphit organs treat these matters lightly offering each consolation to the effect that for every priest who leaves the Church hundreds more will join her. What balderdash ! In every part the Modernists abound, in the colleges and in the obscure country parishes, with a legion of followers. Did the editor of the " Civilta Catholics" poseless no followers' No followers to thoae priests, who, duriutt the few years have joined the Italian Wesleyan Church. which they testify, is more in accordance with dictates of conscience? And hundreds more are leaving that which is steadily going the way of all error. JEUNESSE DE WESLEY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your columns have for the last week or two been occupied by a controversy cat ried on by thelocal heads of the two leading Churches in our town in regard to the respective merits of the doctrine, pre- eminence, and government of their respective branches of the Christian Church. In regard to the Rector's last letter, I quite agree with its argument in the main, but am constrained to disagree with his previous efforts to enlarge the number of the Sacraments of the Church and entirely disapprove of the various Ewer- dotal practices in the Parish Church. In regard to Father Whittle's letter I note the old Romanist argu- ment in favour of his Church ; but I re no poesilsle justification of the claims of that Church in the arguments he uses or the doctrines and dogmas he</p>
        <p>propounds. Whatever may be thought of modern ideas in their application to past history, both political and religious, man must be allowed to investigate the plan of his Redemption for himself, and we shall find that even in the days when some of the Apostles still trod the earth error and false doctrine began to creep into the Church. St. Peter warned his contemporary against " false teachers" and "damnable heresies ' and St. Jade tells of "ungodly men turning the grace of our God into lasciviousness and denying the only Lord God and our Lord Jesus Christ." Allow me to point out that the Christian Faith does not centre in • dogma or in a book but in a Person and that is the cause and pledge of its essential unity. Its one answer to all is that made to the Philippian odor:—" Relieve on the Lord Jesus Christ and -thou shalt be saved, and thy house." The writer of the epistle to the liebvews very succinctly sketched Chris- tian theology, with the pregnant words, " God, Who fragmentarily and multifariously of old spoke to our ' fathers by the Phrophets, at the end of these days spoke unto us by His Son." But unity does not exclude diversity —nay, more, without diversity there can be no true unity. Where there is no unity there is no distraction, but where there is no diversity there is death. The diversity of the Prophets of the Old Dispensation and the diversity of the Evangelist and Apostles of the new demonstrably prove the correct- ness of the Christian policy but the added traditions of egotistical followers have almost obliterated the plan of Redemption from men's minds. Father Whittle, though quoting a lot of eo-callerl authorities does not bring forward any absolute evidence to prove that the Pope or St. Peter ever was appointed head of the Church. We have absolute evidence that Christ negatived any idea of P_ pea ri or ity among the Apostles or their successors. (Matt. xx. 25-27 ; Luke xxii. 24-26.) The promise of the Keys was given to all the Apostles alike. (Matt. xviii. 17„ 18 ;• John on. 21-23). To the</p>
        <p>'Apocal ipso no- distinction'n is made between Kephas and the rest (Rev. 'xi. 14). Origin referring to Peter's confession in Matthew's Gospel says that ill who make Peter's confession with Peter's faithfulness shall have Peter's blessing. (Matt. xvi. 13-20). We know that as time progressed and the life and death of our Blessed Redeemer receded into the dim past men's minds became darkened and the dark or Middle ages supervened and men became enveloped tn darknem, ignorance and miery. The Papacy during this period developed its power over the earth, and assumed 101 sway over kingdoms. principalities and powers. Rome having lost its title to be political capital of the secular world, aspired to become its religious capitaL It even assumed the right to carve out the colonial world among its proteges—Spain and Portugal—and where are these two bnlliant countries of meval days at the present moment ? Read the history of these two countries for the past two hundred years and compare them with our own dear land of liberty and freedom to worship God according to the dictates of our conscience, the land of the open Bible. Then turn to the condition of the Papal States before the fight fcr Italian freedom, or if you want a com- pari,on in government look at what a few Englishmen have dove in India, and compare it with priest-ridden Goa. in Portuguese India. . . . .</p>
        <p>The Roman—Church is not content with ruling in gpiritual matters, but ever claims to dictate sad direct in members in all their secular dealings. A Papist is not at liberty to use his own private judgment, but !must mbmit himself in all things to his spiritual mentor. In the Roman Catholic Church the canon law claims to overrule the State laws, and each individual parishioner is taught that he must render implicit obedience to his ecclesiastical superiors who rule over him absolutely by divine right.—! rema:n..Fc .</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Church of England and Prayers For the Dead.</head>
        <p>The Church of England and Prayers For the Dead.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. T. Jaye would have done well to familiarise himself with the views of the English Reformers before committing himself to the emphatic 'declaration that they"did condemn prayers for the dead, no being contrary to the doctrines of the Reformed Church of England." He would probably agree with , me that in the ranks of the Reformers none held a ...in higher place or made a stronger appeal to the religions conscience of England than the famous bishops say Creamer and Latimer. Do we find them condemning rine prayers for the departed? No. On the conlrar7 they it are both directly associated with no authontstive .s pronouncement in favour of such prayers, ite being* * primitive aril wholly commendable practice. Archbishop Creamer presided over a *corn.</p>
        <p>of time. d in the tive it as lieved in /describe things an 1 in it ? do not :ration,</p>
        <p>iahion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. C. HARVEY COOK.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. C. HARVEY COOK.</p>
        <p>It is with sincere regret that we announce the death of the it.,. Charles Harvey Cook, minister of the Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel, who passed away at four o'clock on Tuesday afternoon in a private nursing home in Manchester. 'The sad news created profouad sorrow in the town, for the rev. !gentleman was esteemed by all sect ions of the community, irrespective of creed or political opinion. For a considerable period he had been subject to lung troubles and laryngitis and it was chiefly to recruit his health that three years ago he visited the United States and attended the great Unitarian Conference at Boston. The trip appeared to benefit him considerably, but of late he had been in is very delirste state and it became a severe strain upon hint to discharge his ministerial ditties. He preached for the last time at Cairo-street Chapel an Sunday, February 2fith,which was observed " Hospital Sunday," and made strudel sppeals for liberal contributions to the local Infirmary. At the end of the day's work it was noticed that he was quite exhausted and a few days later his health com- pletely broke down and he had to take to his bed. Yesterday week he was conveyed in a motor ambu- lance to Mancheater,where an operation was performed by Dr. Burgess on Saturday morning. It was then found that he was !suffering, in addition to another internal complaint, from cancer, and when pneumonia developed his case was regarded as hopeless, and he panned peacefully away at the time tasted, in the presence of his wife, son and daughter. . . .</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. Harvey Cook, who Ls fifty years of age, was a native of the Isle of Wight. He was originally deatined for, commercial career bat felt a strong call to definite religious work and, persevering with his etndies, outdated himself for the Congregational ministry. Ultimately, boo , he threw in his lot with the Unitarian denomination and held pastorates in Yorkshire. He came to Warrington from Holbeck. Leeds, In 1903, in succeesion to the Rev. E. W. Lunsmis and quickly earned the confidence and regard of hi. congregation. He bad a winning penonality—frank, genial, straightforward, outspoken—nod was always ready to take op the cause of be poor and helpleu. A. • preacher, he had an attractive delivery and • splendid command of language, and hie monsoon showed hint to be porreemed of superior Intellectual attainments A special feature of hie ministry wen his work amongst the young men to whom he endeared himself by • gym. pathetic, and helpful spirit of comradeship and by the high tone of his addreeses at the weekly Sunday afternoon class which he conducted v ith great regularit: mud conscientiousneu. Notwithstanding physical drawbacks he was never known to shirk any teak end often on Sundays he would conduct an afternoon service at the Unitarian Chive! at Croft, after preschieg at Cairo- street in the morning. Mr. Cook was •Iso a most accept. able lecturer on e variety of topica and ODO addresa which he had delivered in many towns was en account of the chnrches of hintorical interest in Lancashire and Cbeehire. . .</p>
        <p>sou x.0a50,,•. Mr. Harvey Cook had no exceptionally broad out- look on life and he never allowed sectarian or political barriers to Mecum his judgment of hie fellow men. He was a democrat in deed, as well., in opinion, and be could boast of very large oircle of friends of all shades of thought and of all chums of the comnionity. His interests were many—i• fact he never hesitated to identify himself with all organi/ations for the I amelioration of suffering, the improvement of the environment of the working classes, and the enconrage- meat of education. He was a member of the Board of Menagement of the Infirmary and DM. pensery and also took a prominent part in the work of the Warringtoo Citizens' Guild of Help, the Literary and Philosophical Society, the Warrington Society, the Citizens' Terneersoce League, the Beautiful Warrington society, the Field Club, and the Parliamentary Debating Society, Ile we.. Radical of the staunoheet type sod was always. welcome and oonvinsisg spanker at the meetings in support of XI,. C•ogield's candidature at the last three Parliamentary contests in Warrington. lie was a arm supporter of Women's Suffrage and had presided at meetinge organized by the local society, pleading the cause of the ladies with logical directness and gr.Tit!picerit3, . . . . .</p>
        <p>By Mr. Harvey Cook v death many of the people of , Warrington lon.. friend to whom they 000ld always turn for advice nod help. To the poor he way generous almost to a fault and he never turned a deaf ear to nay deserving appeal. The deepest sympathy is felt with the widow and two children in their be eeeee ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>A striking tribute to the respect entertitioell for the deceased gentleman and the widespread regret aroused by his death was fortbooming at the funeral which took place at the Warrington Cemetery yesterday after- noon. The attendance was large and very represent.- tire, and showed what a large number of ftiendshipe Mr. Harvey Cook had formed not only amongst his own people but in • much wider field. The first part of the service was held in Cairo-street Chapel, where : the body had reposed since Thursday afternoon. Around the coffin w” • profusion of beautiful floral tribute. and the pulpit and reeding desk were draped in purple and black. The chapel was filled to overflowing, and the tier-vice was deeply impreesive. The officiating minister wes the Ft.-. Charles Hargrove, of Leeds, en old friend of Mr. Harvey Cook. Mr. J. M. Moir mu at the organ, and played '0 Rest in the Lord " and Mendelmohn's " March Fonebte." The service was choral, the hymns song being " 0 find our kit!p in Ages Poet" Ind 0 Peace, Perfect Pence."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Cil ARLEN HAAG., s delivered en eloquent address. lie amid they were met in tbe solemn presence of death—that mystery which overshadowed their lives. and which they were all called upon some day to meet. "Here line" continued the preacher " what was our brother—the pastor of so many, and friend of so many more; for he was a man who easily attached people to him. He was kind-hearted, genial, intelligent—a man whom many loved, and who loved back again. Ile is not there. Not that body which we commit to the earth is he. Out of our sight he hen passed and we cannot follow him. But surely, though to us is not given thst belief which so many hold in • beakyen above, and the company of the angel., and the eternal song, we, look- ing tack upon the past, can take good hope for the future. Think of the fifty years that he lived on earth. Thiak of the days when he was • belplese Infant who had perished but for what love provided him—how he grew up to boyhood, how he wrestled with the difficulties in which he was placed, how he yearned to make himself rim in human lore. And no ha enters npon the ministry of religion and then attaches to himself one who for the rest of his life was to him a companion whom he loved and trusted, sharing all his labours, brightening many a bard time with the sweet sunshine of love. But ash, had been anxious to learn us boy, be was anxiou to learn on a man—as teacher, teaching others the highest things. Ile then, sot knowing himself the certainty of what he taught and, doubting of it, could not conceal kin doubts, mut needs! come out and enter into • communion which had not much to offer except the freedom of • larger faith, and there he worked for these yearn peat, kourn amongst on AA a diligent peator of • free faith. To inn, as I think of his life and what he had to struggle with, of his delicate health,of the trials which be did not complaint or grumble &amp;boot, but of which he told me io.confldenoe.dt seems as jilt were all like a day of July sixosbiue with clouds drifting across the sky and threatening to hide the stm altogether, were it not no strong as to make it. wey through—• life where ' the sunshine was his own genial nature, God-given, and</p>
      </div>
